2026-08-17 09:42:49,290 989962 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-17: "QMC is now pointed at 356 degrees, the YAGI is pointed at 115 degrees. there is a config setting for this, it does not work... after entering the YAGI\'s actual point of 115 the QMC should show 115 and not 356 it did not move."\n\nHis three suspicions, answered by measurement on 192.168.10.186 this session:\n\n1. "the code simply does not work" — HALF RIGHT. The DASHBOARD does not work. The FIRMWARE is correct.\n\n   ROOT CAUSE, data/dashboard.html:454:\n       send({action:"setYagiAz", args:[v]});\n   send() is declared at data/dashboard.html:295 with TWO POSITIONAL parameters:\n       function send(action, args) { ... ws.send(JSON.stringify({action, args: args ? Object.values(args) : []})); }\n   So the object literal binds to `action` and `args` is undefined. The frame on the wire is\n       {"action":{"action":"setYagiAz","args":[115]},"args":[]}\n   wsCmd (src/main.cpp:398) does `doc["a"] | doc["action"] | doc["command"] | ""`. doc["action"] is an\n   OBJECT, so the const char* coercion falls through to "" and the next line returns without\n   dispatching. The command is silently dropped.\n\n   This is the ONLY call in data/dashboard.html passing an object as the first argument —\n   `grep -n \'send({\' data/dashboard.html` returns exactly one line, 454. Every other call is\n   send("seek2az", {az:v}) etc.\n\n   MEASURED — malformed frame, exactly what the button produces:\n     BEFORE  yagiOffset=0.0 az=356.0\n     SENDING {"action": {"action": "setYagiAz", "args": [115]}, "args": []}\n     AFTER   yagiOffset=0.0 az=356.0     (nothing happened, no log line)\n\n   MEASURED — correctly formed frame:\n     ws.send({"action":"setYagiAz","args":[115]})\n     DEVICE LOG: setYagiAz: target=115.0 raw=356.0 offset=119.0\n     AFTER   yagiOffset=119.0 az=115.0   (exactly what Peter expected)\n\n   The firmware arithmetic at src/main.cpp:254-263 is sound: raw 356 -> offset = 115-356 = -241\n   -> +360 = 119; read() = getAzimuth() + 119 = -4 + 119 = 115.\n\n2. "the setting does not persist / survive reboot" — DISPROVED by measurement.\n     BEFORE REBOOT  yagiOffset=119.0 az=115.0 uptime=43867\n     AFTER  REBOOT  yagiOffset=119.0 az=115.0 uptime=14\n   con.putFloat("qmc_offset", ...) writes NVS; con.FLOATinit("qmc_offset", ...) at src/main.cpp:45\n   reads it back on boot. FLOATinit (include/prefs.h:24-32) only writes the default when the key is\n   ABSENT, so it cannot clobber a stored value. NOT TESTED: whether a full-erase reflash preserves\n   NVS — OTA does, a full erase would not, and which one Peter uses was not established.\n\n3. "the qmc calibration routine is corrupted by the 115 degrees offset" — BACKWARDS, and worse.\n   The offset does not corrupt the calibration; the calibration DESTROYS the offset.\n   src/main.cpp:105-106, the first two statements of qmcCali():\n       qmc.yagiOffset = 0;                      // new constants invalidate the old boresight\n       con.putFloat("qmc_offset", qmc.yagiOffset);\n   It zeroes and persists before doing anything else. The only alert it broadcasts\n   (src/main.cpp:107-112) is "Compass cal started — motor spinning (~20s)" — NO mention that the\n   yagi pointing has just been wiped. So the removal Peter asked for already happens; the WARNING\n   he asked for does not exist. If he ran a compass calibration after setting 115, that alone\n   explains the offset being gone with no clue on screen.\n\nThird defect found while investigating: the success toast lies. data/dashboard.html:455 fires\nshowToast("Yagi bearing set","success",3000) unconditionally, immediately after send(), with no\ncheck that the device accepted anything. That is why the failure was invisible — Peter got a green\nconfirmation for a command the firmware never received.\n\nRULED OUT: northOffset is unrelated. It is applied only to the encoder-derived calAz\n(src/main.cpp:368) and never to the compass az, so it is not a second suspect.\n\nDEVICE STATE CHANGED DURING INVESTIGATION: I set yagiOffset=119 over the WS to prove the firmware\nworks, so the device now correctly reads az=115 for a yagi physically at 115. That is the state\nPeter was trying to reach. Reversible with setOffset 0.'}
2026-08-17 09:43:16,324 989962 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'wsCmd-drops-malformed-commands-in-silence — CONFIRMED. Out of scope for setyagiaz-dashboard-fix (that task fixes the one caller; this is the reason nobody noticed).\n\nNote on ids: earlier entries in this ledger use B1..B5. That numbering predates the slug rule; new entries are slugs. B1-B5 are not renamed because they are cited by SHA 3cbd382\'s commit message and by docs/HANDOVER.md.\n\nWHAT: src/main.cpp:398-400 —\n    const char* action = doc["a"] | doc["action"] | doc["command"] | "";\n    if (!action[0]) return false;\nWhen a client sends a frame whose action is missing, or is the wrong JSON type, the ArduinoJson\n`|` coercion falls through to "" and wsCmd returns false. The client is told NOTHING — no error\nevent, no log line, no reply of any kind. The same is true of an action that IS a string but names\nno registered command: include/commands.h:31-35 logs "No such Function: <name>" to the console\nonly, and fires no done frame (this half is already noted in the retire-move2az task\'s goal).\n\nEVIDENCE, measured 2026-08-17 on 192.168.10.186. Sent the exact malformed frame that\ndata/dashboard.html:454 produces:\n    {"action": {"action": "setYagiAz", "args": [115]}, "args": []}\nSubscribed to both status and log. Result: yagiOffset 0.0 -> 0.0, az 356.0 -> 356.0, and not one\nlog line. Complete silence.\n\nEFFECT: this is why the Set pointing bug was invisible. A client cannot distinguish "command\nexecuted" from "command was garbage and I threw it away". Combined with a UI that toasts success\non click rather than on confirmation, a broken control looks identical to a working one. Peter\nfound it only because the antenna was visibly pointing the wrong way.\n\nNOT YET KNOWN: how many other clients send malformed frames that are being dropped. node-dash is\nthe main one and its frames are built by rotator.js _send() which appears well-formed, but I did\nNOT audit every call site in that repo — and it is another repo, so any finding there is raised on\nxsession, never fixed from here.\n\nSUGGESTED (not decided): reply {"evt":"error","reason":"unknown or malformed action"} to the\nrequesting client. That is a WS contract addition and belongs with the same decision as B5 (the\nreturn leg emitting no done frame) — both are "the device does not tell you when nothing\nhappened".'}
