2026-08-17 10:15:55,708 989962 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'dosteps-zero-runaway', 'title': 'BUG doSteps(0) drives the motor until something physically stops it — six revolutions observed', 'steps': ['Investigate', 'Document', 'Implement', 'Observe', 'Track']}
2026-08-17 10:16:22,920 989962 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'A zero-step move runs the motor forever. Six revolutions were observed on a masted antenna.\n\nOBSERVED 2026-08-17 09:56, recorded in the ledger as seek2az-runaway-shortly-after-boot:\n  seek2az(300) issued while the compass already read ~300 -- a zero-degree move.\n  outbound done = az 59, ok=False. Status: abs -68815, rel -68815, uptime 41.\n  68815 pulses at pulsePerDeg 30.21 = ~2278 deg = 6.3 revolutions.\n\nFIRST HYPOTHESIS DISPROVED. I assumed the QMC returned nonsense shortly after boot, so\nseek2az computed a large error for a bearing it was already on. Tested without commanding\nany move: rebooted and watched az/calAz from uptime 7 to 102. az held 62-63 with the normal\n+/-1 deg flutter, no jumps, calAz 0.0 throughout. The compass is fine from the first frame.\nThat hypothesis is dead and the real mechanism is in the motor layer.\n\nREAL MECHANISM, traced end to end:\n\ninclude/servo.h:533-541\n    void doSteps(int steps, int pwm, bool wait = false){\n        bool fwd = steps > 0;\n        stepMode = true;\n        relTarget = steps;\n        relCount = 0;\n        if(fwd) forward(pwm); else reverse(pwm);\n        if(wait) waitStop();\n    }\n\nWith steps == 0:\n  1. relTarget = 0, relCount = 0.\n  2. `fwd = steps > 0` is FALSE for zero, so it calls reverse(pwm). A zero-step move\n     STARTS THE MOTOR, backwards.\n  3. The only thing that stops a step move is the encoder ISR, include/servo.h:768-777:\n         int offset = abs(relCount - relTarget);\n         if (offset < hysteresis) { brake(); stepMode = false; }\n     with hysteresis = 1 (include/servo.h:717).\n  4. First encoder tick: relCount += direction -> +/-1. offset = abs(+/-1 - 0) = 1.\n     `1 < 1` is FALSE. No brake.\n  5. Every subsequent tick moves relCount FURTHER from 0, so offset only ever GROWS.\n     The brake condition can never be satisfied.\n\nThe motor runs until a stall, a manual brake, or a reboot. waitStop() blocks the main loop\nthroughout, so the device is also unresponsive to anything except what yield_ms services.\n\nmoveSteps GUARDS THIS EXACT CASE and doSteps does not:\n  include/servo.h:561   if(steps == 0) return true;\nSo the author knew a zero move was meaningless; the guard is simply on the wrong function.\nmoveSteps calls doSteps at :578, so callers going through moveSteps are safe and callers\ngoing direct are not.\n\nTHE TRIGGER WINDOW IS WIDER THAN EXACT ZERO, AND IS ASYMMETRIC.\n  include/servo.h:240   int steps = -roundUp(angle * pulsePerDeg);\n  include/utils.h:131   inline int roundUp(float val){ return (int)ceil(val); }\nceil rounds toward +infinity, so for a small NEGATIVE angle, e.g. -0.02 deg at ppd 30.21:\n  -0.02 * 30.21 = -0.604 ; ceil(-0.604) = 0 ; steps = -0 = 0.\nAny angle in (-1/ppd, 0] -- about (-0.033 deg, 0] -- produces zero steps. A small POSITIVE\nangle produces at least one step and is safe. So only tiny NEGATIVE errors trigger it, which\nis why this is rare rather than constant.\n\nWHICH CALLERS CAN REACH IT (grep \'doSteps(\' include/servo.h):\n  :498  seek2az initial shot   -- shot = d0 when |d0| <= creepMargin. NOT GUARDED. THIS ONE.\n  :517  seek2az jog            -- loop condition is fabsf(errN) > tol with tol 2.0, so the\n                                  angle is always >= 2 deg. Safe.\n  :631  move2az initial shot   -- PROTECTED by the early exit at :595,\n                                  `if(fabsf(d0) <= tol) { fireDone(...); return true; }`\n  :640  move2az jog            -- same tol guard as :517. Safe.\n  :373 :382 :436 :441 :448     -- constants or computed non-zero. Safe.\n  :578  moveSteps              -- guarded by its own steps == 0 check at :561. Safe.\n\nSo seek2az\'s missing already-on-target early exit is the live route in. That gap is ALREADY\nwritten down in docs/specs/v4-retire-move2az.md as something seek2az must absorb from\nmove2az -- listed there as an efficiency nicety ("a repeat aim performs a needless shot and\ncreep"). Nobody knew it was the difference between a no-op and six revolutions.\n\nThis also explains why the event looked boot-related and was not: seek2az-hold-return\'s\nreturn leg had just been disabled for a falsification run, so the antenna was sitting exactly\nwhere it had been commanded, and the next seek2az was to that same bearing -- a zero move.\nThe uptime of 41 s was a coincidence of the reflash, not a cause.'}
2026-08-17 10:17:07,082 989962 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_file_attach args={'file_path': 'docs/specs/dosteps-zero-runaway.md', 'kind': 'plan', 'label': 'Spec: guard doSteps against zero, and give seek2az the already-on-target early exit'}
2026-08-17 10:20:44,524 989962 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': "Run under /afk, five phases, no pausing. Shipped f5041bb. Spec docs/specs/dosteps-zero-runaway.md.\n\n3. Implement — include/servo.h:533 `if(steps == 0) return;` in doSteps (the root fix, protects every caller); seek2az gains the already-on-target early exit, with jogDeg/tol/maxJogs moved ABOVE d0 so the exit uses the same tol the jog loop works to rather than a second 2.0f literal.\n\n4. Observe, all on the live device 192.168.10.186 —\n   CHECK 1+2, the exact runaway command: seek2az(62) with the antenna already at 62.\n     BEFORE  az=62.0 abs=0 rel=0\n     done in 0.06s {'cmd':'seek2az','az':63,'ok':True}\n     AFTER   az=63.0 abs=0 rel=0     abs delta = 0\n     The command that produced 6.3 revolutions now turns the motor not at all.\n   CHECK 3, normal move unaffected: seek2az(150) -> done az=148 ok=True, az 149, abs -2826.\n   REGRESSION: full hold-return cycle target 240 hold 5s -> origin 149, retAz 148, held at 237, returned to 150. PASS, return error 1 deg.\n\n   NOT FALSIFIED, and this is a deliberate refusal rather than an omission. Falsifying these guards means removing them and issuing a zero-degree move — i.e. deliberately commanding an uncommanded multi-revolution spin of a masted antenna with coax attached. Destructive physical action, Peter away, /afk rule 7 says stop for those. The guards rest on: the mechanism traced line by line from source; the recorded event matching it exactly (negative counts, abs == rel, a zero-degree command); and checks 1-3. If it is to be proven, bench rig with the antenna disconnected.\n\n5. Track — servo.h hash updated in docs/specs/dosteps-zero-runaway.md and docs/modules/servo.md; servo.md gained real documentation of both guards and of the (-1/ppd, 0] window; check_specs.py passes 2 of 2.\n\nDECISIONS TAKEN ALONE:\n - Guard doSteps rather than widen `offset < hysteresis` to `<=`. The hysteresis test is correct for every NON-zero target, where relCount converges on relTarget and offset shrinks; only a zero target diverges. Widening it would change the stopping point of every move on a rotator whose pointing accuracy is already under scrutiny. Reverse by editing include/servo.h:774.\n - Did NOT change angle2steps' use of ceil. The asymmetry is real but a zero-step request is legitimate and doSteps must handle it; changing the rounding alters every move's step count. Reverse at include/servo.h:240.\n - Kept BOTH guards rather than only the doSteps one. The seek2az exit also removes a pointless shot-and-creep on a repeat aim, which is what v4-retire-move2az asked for anyway. Reverse by deleting the block at include/servo.h:494-505.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR ANOTHER TASK: retire-move2az's spec lists the already-on-target early exit as item 3a, something seek2az still had to absorb. seek2az now HAS it (and hold_ms, from seek2az-hold-return). That spec is now partly done and must be re-read before it is worked, or the work will be duplicated."}
