2026-08-17 10:21:22,846 989962 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'CORRECTION to seek2az-runaway-shortly-after-boot — DIAGNOSED and FIXED, and the slug\'s premise is WRONG.\n\nIt has nothing to do with boot. Renaming would break the reference in this ledger and in the\ncommit trail, so the slug stands with this correction attached.\n\nDISPROVED: "shortly after boot" / "the QMC may not have settled". Tested by rebooting and\nwatching az and calAz with NO move commanded, uptime 7 through 102: az held 62-63 with the\nnormal +/-1 deg flutter, calAz 0.0, no jumps. The compass is trustworthy from the first\nframe. The uptime of 41 s in the original event was a coincidence of a reflash.\n\nACTUAL CAUSE: doSteps(0) started the motor and could never stop it. include/servo.h,\n`bool fwd = steps > 0` is false for zero so it called reverse(); the ISR\'s only stop\ncondition is `abs(relCount - relTarget) < hysteresis` with relTarget 0 and hysteresis 1, so\nthe first encoder tick makes offset 1 and every later tick makes it larger. The brake\ncondition could never be met. moveSteps had guarded this all along; the guard was on the\nwrong function.\n\nThe trigger is any angle in (-1/pulsePerDeg, 0], about (-0.033 deg, 0] — angle2steps is\n-ceil(angle * ppd), and ceil rounds toward +infinity so tiny NEGATIVE angles collapse to\nzero steps. Positive ones do not. That asymmetry is why it was rare.\n\nFIXED in f5041bb: doSteps refuses zero; seek2az gained the already-on-target early exit that\nmove2az always had. Verified live — the exact runaway command (seek2az to the bearing already\nheld) now returns in 0.06 s with abs delta 0.\n\nNOT FALSIFIED, deliberately: proving it means commanding a multi-revolution spin of a masted\nantenna. See docs/specs/dosteps-zero-runaway.md.\n\nRAISED WITH node-dash on xsession — their active-tracker re-aims at the same node repeatedly\nand traceroute re-issues an aim, both of which produce exactly the zero-degree command that\ntriggered this. Any v4 they have NOT reflashed with f5041bb still has it.'}
2026-08-17 10:23:31,373 989962 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'ws-api-truth', 'title': 'The published contract lies: /schema advertises two absent commands, WS_API.md documents a mode command and seven run modes that do not exist', 'steps': ['Investigate', 'Document', 'Implement', 'Observe', 'Track']}
2026-08-17 10:23:49,918 989962 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Both published descriptions of this device\'s surface are wrong, in opposite directions.\n\nVERIFIED LIVE 2026-08-17. Sent each name over WS with the log stream subscribed:\n  device replies: [\'No such Function: diagnostics\', \'No such Function: note\', \'No such Function: mode\']\n\n1. /schema ADVERTISES TWO COMMANDS THAT DO NOT EXIST: `diagnostics` and `note`.\n   Neither is registered — `grep -n "diagnostics|\\"note\\"" src/main.cpp include/commands.h`\n   returns nothing outside httpserver.h itself. /schema is the self-describing surface node-dash\n   is told to trust, and api-schema [1342] shipped it; nobody diffed it against the command map.\n\n2. /schema OMITS ~18 COMMANDS THAT DO EXIST. Registered but undeclared: brake(declared),\n   calPwmMin, compass, conPrint, dump, encraw, mem, moveSteps, myInfo, ppd, pub, pwmMax,\n   pwmMin, pwmRun, qmcData, qmcSave, qmcscan, reset_net, settletest, show_net.\n   Registered list from `grep -oE \'\\{"[a-zA-Z0-9_]+",\' src/main.cpp`; declared list from\n   `curl -s http://192.168.10.186/schema`.\n\n3. WS_API.md DOCUMENTS A `mode` COMMAND AND SEVEN RUN MODES THAT DO NOT EXIST.\n   WS_API.md:43-52 lists `mode [n]`; WS_API.md:89-103 documents modes -1/0/1/2/10/11/12 with\n   behaviours. `{"mode"` is NOT in src/main.cpp\'s command map, and the device answers\n   "No such Function: mode".\n\n4. WS_API.md\'s status example and field table carry `runmode` and `mode`, which fillStatus has\n   NEVER emitted — this is the already-recorded ledger entry B1, now traced to its source: the\n   documentation asserted the field, so both dashboards were written to read it.\n\n5. WS_API.md is missing every field added since: retArmed, retAz, yagiStale, lat, lon, alt,\n   calAz, northOffset, stall, pwmRunPct, pwmFreq, abs, rel. And it documents seek2az as\n   `[degrees]` when it now takes [degrees, corr_pwm, hold_ms], and omits setYagiAz entirely.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, and it is the same failure as the Set pointing bug fixed in 5a70bcc: a documented\ncontrol that silently does nothing. data/dashboard.html\'s Passive and Scan buttons send\n`mode`, get "No such Function" logged where nobody looks, and no done frame — so they appear\nto work and do not. That is a UI question, not a contract one, and is recorded separately\nrather than fixed here: deleting a feature Peter may still want is his call, not mine.'}
2026-08-17 10:24:18,181 989962 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_file_attach args={'file_path': 'docs/specs/ws-api-truth.md', 'kind': 'plan', 'label': 'Spec: remove absent commands from /schema, make WS_API.md describe the real surface'}
2026-08-17 10:29:59,148 989962 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'setters-treat-a-missing-argument-as-zero — CONFIRMED, and it cost real config today.\n\nEvery `set*` command reads its argument with String::toFloat()/toInt(), which return 0 for an\nempty or absent string. None of them distinguish "the caller sent 0" from "the caller sent\nnothing". So a command with a MISSING required argument silently writes 0 to NVS.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-17, self-inflicted. I probed /schema\'s command list by sending each name\nwith `args: []` to check none returned "No such Function". That wrote zeros:\n  yagiOffset  119.0      -> 0.0      (Peter\'s pointing calibration)\n  lat         51.026329  -> 0.0\n  lon         -3.158835  -> 0.0\n  alt         31.0       -> 0.0\n  pwmRunPct   0.5        -> 0.0      (motor run speed)\nAll persisted to NVS. Restored from values verified earlier in the same session; device\nre-checked afterwards and is correct.\n\npwmFreq SURVIVED, and that is the proof the fix is easy: setPwmFreq has a range check\n(`if (freq < 10 || freq > 20000) { cl("out of range"); return; }`, src/main.cpp), so 0 was\nrejected. setLat and setLon have range checks too (-90..90, -180..180) but 0 is INSIDE those\nranges, so the check passes and the write happens. Range-checking is not argument-presence\nchecking.\n\nWORSE THAN IT LOOKS FOR ONE OF THEM: setOffset zeroing yagiOffset also called pointingSet(),\nwhich CLEARS yagiStale. So the flag added in 324e62f specifically to announce "the pointing\nis untrustworthy" reported false while the pointing had just been destroyed. The flag is\ncorrect for its intended trigger (qmcCali) and blind to this one.\n\nFIX, not applied here because it is a change to ~10 command handlers and belongs in its own\ntask: reject an empty argument string before converting. `if (!val.length()) { cl("setX:\nmissing argument"); return; }`. The command table already receives the raw String, so the\ntest is one line per handler.\n\nRELATED: this is the third member of the same family, after\nwsCmd-drops-malformed-commands-in-silence and the Set pointing bug fixed in 5a70bcc. The\ndevice accepts something it cannot act on correctly and says nothing. Peter\'s rule about a\ncontrol that appears to work and does not applies to the command surface as much as the UI.'}
