2026-08-17 10:31:37,540 989962 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'missing-arg-is-not-zero', 'title': 'BUG a command sent with no argument silently writes 0 to NVS — it destroyed a live calibration today', 'steps': ['Investigate', 'Document', 'Implement', 'Observe', 'Track']}
2026-08-17 10:31:57,428 989962 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'A command sent with no argument writes 0 to NVS and says nothing.\n\nString::toFloat() and String::toInt() return 0 for an empty string. Every set* handler\nconverts its argument unconditionally, so "the caller sent 0" and "the caller sent nothing"\nare indistinguishable. A malformed command silently overwrites persistent config.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-17, self-inflicted and recorded rather than hidden. While probing /schema\'s\ncommand list I sent each name with args: [] to check none returned "No such Function". That\nwrote zeros to the live device:\n    yagiOffset  119.0      -> 0.0        Peter\'s pointing calibration\n    lat         51.026329  -> 0.0\n    lon         -3.158835  -> 0.0\n    alt         31.0       -> 0.0\n    pwmRunPct   0.5        -> 0.0        motor run speed\nAll persisted. Restored from values verified earlier in the same session and re-checked\nlive: yagiOffset 119.0, lat 51.026329, lon -3.158835, alt 31.0, pwmRunPct 0.5, pwmRun 672.\n\nRANGE CHECKING IS NOT PRESENCE CHECKING, and the survivor proves it.\nsetPwmFreq survived only because 0 falls outside its 10-20000 range check. setLat and setLon\nHAVE range checks (-90..90, -180..180) and 0 is INSIDE them, so the check passed and the\nwrite happened. A range check cannot catch this; only testing the string can.\n\nTHE GUARD ALREADY EXISTS IN THIS FILE, ON EXACTLY ONE HANDLER.\n    src/main.cpp:229  {"ppd", ...  if(val.length()){ motor.setPulsePerDeg(val.toFloat()); ... }\nSame shape as dosteps-zero-runaway, where moveSteps guarded a zero and doSteps did not: the\nright idea, applied once, never generalised.\n\nTWO CONSEQUENCES WORSE THAN A ZEROED FIELD:\n\n1. setOffset with no argument zeroes yagiOffset AND calls pointingSet(), which CLEARS\n   yagiStale. So the flag added in 324e62f specifically to announce "the pointing is\n   untrustworthy" reported false at the exact moment the pointing was destroyed. The flag is\n   correct for its designed trigger (qmcCali) and blind to this route.\n\n2. `hold` with no argument calls armHold(0), so holdUntil = millis() + 0 — already lapsed.\n   isHeld() is false, but armHold still sets returnArmed when an origin exists, so loop()\n   fires the RETURN LEG immediately. A no-argument hold silently moves the antenna.\n\nHANDLERS AFFECTED (src/main.cpp): pwmRun, pwmMin, pwmMax, setPwmRunPct, setPwmFreq,\nsetYagiAz, setOffset, setLat, setLon, setAlt, setNorthOffset, hold.\nAlready guarded: ppd. Not in scope: fwd/rev (transient PWM, 0 just stops), moveSteps\n(guards steps == 0 itself), jog2az, calMotor and calPwmMin (both already use `.length() ?`\nto supply a default, which is the correct pattern for an OPTIONAL argument).\n\nThis is the third member of a family, after wsCmd-drops-malformed-commands-in-silence and\nthe Set pointing bug fixed in 5a70bcc: the device accepts something it cannot act on\ncorrectly and reports nothing.'}
