2026-08-17 15:21:11,799 1549532 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-17, verbatim across three messages:\n\n"ther\'s an issue with the dashboard. a dashboard should be a site. if I click a link it navigates away. needs either a top menu or a side menu / drawer and a content panel."\n\n"cant tell until I see it. my pref would be a collapsinle side drawer with Overview, and a section or sub menu with each area, as well as the life cycle. probably add some stats cards to the home page, dont know exactly what. and top of the left drawer will be a project picker. this must serve on a single port ? since all spaces is needed the drawer should appear over the page otherwise our flow charts will shrink ?"\n\n"maybe a small top bar, we can use that for badges and maybe the project selector, that would be more appropriate"\n\nAnswers given, both with reasons rather than preference:\n- **Single port, one process.** 8801/8802 are separate today only because `python3 -m http.server` serves one directory. One process reads the registry and mounts each subscribed charts dir under `/p/<project>/`. Nothing is copied; the yaml stays in its own repo.\n- **Overlay, not push.** The chart grid divides width EQUALLY, so a 240px pushing drawer takes a 5-column chart at 1024 from 166px boxes to ~118px — below the 128px floor MAX_SPINE_COLS was derived from. Charts would go illegible exactly when the menu is open. And connectors are drawn from measured positions, so a width change forces a redraw; an overlay cannot.\n- **Top bar rather than a wide element**, because vertical space is not contested — charts need width. The project selector goes there because it changes what the drawer lists, and a control that reshapes the menu cannot sit inside it.\n\nBuilt to be looked at and changed. The stats cards are explicitly his call once he sees them; the drift card must say UNKNOWN until `flow-derive drift` has actually run.'}
