2026-08-17 15:29:23,141 1578042 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': '`npm test` in `html/` exits 0 having run **nothing** — `package.json` declares\n`node --test test/*.test.js` and there is no `test/` directory. Measured 2026-08-17:\n\n```\n# pass 0\n# fail 0\n# duration_ms 8.981728\n```\n\nA green check that ran nothing is worse than no check, because the next session reads\nthe exit code. I introduced this in 5e05f44 by declaring the script and not writing the\ntests. Siblings do it properly: `bridge` 205 tests, `plugins` 99.\n\n**What is worth testing here is NOT the copied code.** The three modules are\nbyte-identical to node-dash\'s and their behaviour is node-dash\'s business. What belongs\nto *this* repo is the contract of having copied them at all, plus the wiring in\n`bin/httpd.js`. Four things, each of which encodes a decision that would otherwise be\nlost:\n\n1. **drift, local** — the seven vendored files still match their recorded hashes, so\n   nobody can quietly edit a copy\n2. **drift, upstream** — they still match node-dash\'s originals, so we find out when the\n   donor moves. Skips with a stated reason when node-dash is not on disk\n3. **the boundary** — nothing in `src/` imports outside `src/`, so a future edit cannot\n   reach back into node-dash or add a core dependency by accident\n4. **the wiring** — `/health` has the declared shape, and there is **no GET for queue\n   data**, which `pac-command-api.js:29-34` records as a real bug (Peter, 2026-07-25)\n\nTest 3 is the one that matters most in six months: it is the machine-checkable form of\n"host-free", the property the whole move was organised around.'}
