2026-08-17 15:39:19,783 1578042 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': '## LEDGER UPDATE 2026-08-17 14:40 UTC — dispositions\n\n- `alarm-image-api-is-not-host-free` — **STANDS.** Not a defect to fix; it is the\n  corrected fact. Now recorded in the task goal, the spec, the commit message of\n  5e05f44 and xsession #50. Closed as *recorded everywhere it matters*.\n- `handover-asserts-tab-control-does-not-exist` — **FIXED**, commit 432371b.\n  Correction added, original wording left intact.\n- `handover-says-sockets-stall-every-90s` — **FIXED as a documentation defect**,\n  commit 432371b. **The underlying fault is NOT fixed and is not understood** — see\n  the new entry below.\n- `node-dash-dead-stmts-import` — **NOT OURS, still open.** Raised #49, restated #50.\n  node-dash\'s cursor was at 44 when last checked; re-check before assuming they read it.\n- `node-dash-stale-tmp-file` — **NOT OURS, still open.** Mentioned in #49.\n- `mt-alarm-1-not-on-specboard` — **NOT DONE, and deliberately so. NEEDS PETER.**\n  `specboard.py subscribe` refuses without `docs/specs/AREAS.yaml`\n  (`specboard.py:231-237`): *"that is the one file a repo must provide"*. Writing one\n  means inventing this repo\'s area taxonomy, which decides how every future defect is\n  filed on a board other sessions read. Unlike the pm2 entry, that is not cheaply\n  reversible once other sessions have filed against it. Not a call I should make alone.\n\n---\n\n## bridge-sockets-stall-cause-unknown — CONFIRMED fault, cause NOT established\n\nSplit out from the documentation defect, because correcting the description of a fault\nis not fixing it and the two must not close together.\n\n**What is measured**, `/health STALLED` in node-dash\'s log, re-run 14:38 UTC:\n\n```\npm2 logs node-dash --lines 5000 --nostream | grep \'/health STALLED\'\n12:47:23  12:48:50  12:50:28  12:54:38  13:37:45  14:07:50\n```\n\n**What is NOT known:** whether the fault is in the bridge (ours), the loopback network,\nor node-dash\'s watchdog being too strict at `limit 30s = 3 ticks` against a\n`feedTickMs` of 10000.\n\n**THE EXPERIMENT IS NOW POSSIBLE AND WAS NOT BEFORE.** Since 14:23 UTC there are two\nindependent consumers of the same four bridge sockets — node-dash on :8000 and\nmt-alarm-html on :8791 — running the same `alarm-host.js`, byte-identical, in separate\nprocesses. That discriminates:\n\n| observation | conclusion |\n|---|---|\n| a stall hits **both** at the same instant | the bridge stopped pushing. Ours. |\n| a stall hits **node-dash only** | node-dash\'s socket or its watchdog. Theirs. |\n| a stall hits **html only** | this service\'s environment, not the bridge |\n\n**Current data is INSUFFICIENT and must not be read as a result.** mt-alarm-html has\nlogged 0 STALLED and 0 STALE, but has only been connected ~15 minutes, and node-dash\'s\nobserved gaps between stall bursts are 43 and 30 minutes. **A quiet window shorter than\nthe known inter-event gap is not evidence of anything.** The correct next step is to\nre-run both greps after a window comfortably longer than 43 minutes and compare\ntimestamps:\n\n```\npm2 logs html      --lines 5000 --nostream | grep -E \'STALLED|STALE\'\npm2 logs node-dash --lines 5000 --nostream | grep -E \'STALLED|STALE\'\n```\n\nBoth services log through the same `log.js` in UTC, so the timestamps are directly\ncomparable without conversion.'}
