2026-08-17 18:46:37,462 1578042 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Serve the real page from `html/`, **in parallel with node-dash**, so node-dash can be\nswitched off once this works.\n\nPeter, 2026-08-17: *"whu have you not added the UI ? there\'s no reason that node-dash\nand here cannot serve the pages. once this is working I will switch off node-dash"*.\n\nThat settles the A/B question in `docs/UI-DECISION.md` and it settles it by a route I\ndid not offer: **both, then retire node-dash.** It is the ordering the original handover\nasked for — *"mt-alarm must be running and serving BEFORE node-dash unwires"* — applied\nto the page rather than the data.\n\n**Why nothing was built until now, stated plainly: my own guard forbade it.**\n`test/static.test.js` test 8 fails any file under `public/` containing a socket. I wrote\nit reading *"browsers cannot make decisions"* as *"the browser must not reach for data"*.\nThat is wrong. A page that opens a feed and paints what arrives is doing exactly what a\npresentation layer does; **receiving is not deciding**. The guard blocked the work it was\nsupposed to protect, and I let it rather than questioning it.\n\n**The rule the guard should encode:** the page may SUBSCRIBE and RENDER. It may not\nCOMPUTE — no clock arithmetic on timestamps, no unit conversion, no percentages, no\n`setInterval` re-rendering an elapsed value. Layout maths (SVG path geometry) is\nrendering, not derivation; node-dash\'s own `app-control.js` does only that, which is the\nprecedent.\n\n**The honest constraint on this first page:** no display-ready feed exists yet — that is\n`control-surface-consolidation`, raised as #51 with the plugins session. So this page\nrenders only what needs no derivation: unit ids, booleans, radio lists, raw `rssi`/`snr`\nwith static unit labels, counters, and the event stream verbatim. **Values that need a\nstring — elapsed times, `beat` as anything but milliseconds — are shown as absent with\nthe reason named on screen, never faked in the browser.** That makes the gap visible on\nthe page instead of buried in a spec.'}
2026-08-17 18:47:03,025 1578042 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_file_attach args={'file_path': 'docs/spec/html-serves-the-dashboard.md', 'kind': 'plan', 'label': 'Spec: the dashboard page, subscribe-and-render only', 'scope': 'task'}
2026-08-17 18:54:02,751 1578042 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': '## CONSTRAINT ADDED MID-TASK — Peter, 2026-08-17, verbatim\n\n> *"http://192.168.10.205:8000/node/!8cee336b that is for 1 device, detailed view, the\n> home page needs to be a summary of important info about all devices."*\n\n**Page structure, settled:**\n\n| page | is |\n|---|---|\n| `/` — home | a **summary of important info about ALL devices** |\n| `/node/<!id>` | the **detailed view of ONE device** |\n\nWhat I built is a units grid plus the event wire. That is closer to right than wrong, but\nthe emphasis is off: the wire currently occupies the largest area on the home page, and\nthe per-unit cards carry six raw readings rather than *the important things*. The wire is\na stream of individual frames — detail, not summary.\n\n**What this does not change:** the browser still presents and never computes, so\n"important info" is limited to what a feed serves without derivation until\n`control-surface-consolidation` (#51) lands. The honest form of a summary today names\nwhat it cannot yet show rather than filling the space with what it can.\n\n**Open, and not to be guessed:** which facts count as important per device. Candidate\nlist to put to Peter rather than invent — last contact, whether it is overdue, signal,\nbeat, queue depth, and any alarm state. Most of those are exactly the fields the current\nfeed does not carry.'}
2026-08-17 18:55:49,905 1578042 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': '## CONSTRAINT ADDED MID-TASK — Peter, 2026-08-17, verbatim\n\n> *"so it needs a collapsible left menu with Board, Devices, each device should be a sub\n> menu with a page simiar to the one on node-dash"*\n\n**Navigation, settled:**\n\n```\n[collapsible sidebar]\n  Board                    ->  /                  summary of ALL devices\n  Devices                  ->  expandable\n    !987ab80f              ->  /node/!987ab80f    detail, one device\n    !8cee336b              ->  /node/!8cee336b\n```\n\nSame shape as node-dash\'s own sidebar, which has a `«` collapse toggle and lists the two\nunits as sub-items under CONTROL.\n\n**The device list is built from the feed**, not hardcoded — it is pushed data rendered\ninto a menu, which is presentation, not decision.\n\n**Routing needs one server change and it serves no data.** `express.static` cannot map\n`/node/!8cee336b` to a file, so `bin/httpd.js` gets an SPA fallback that returns\n`public/index.html` for unmatched paths. That is a **static file**, so the server still\nholds no bridge connection and serves nothing live. node-dash does the same thing\n(`index.js:240` lists its SPA page routes). Hash routing would avoid even that, but real\npaths match the house style and Peter gave a real path.\n\n**Feed per page:**\n\n| page | subscribes to |\n|---|---|\n| Board | `/plugin/dash/live` — the consolidated summary |\n| device detail | `/state/{unit}` — the full per-unit body, which is also a WS feed |\n\n`/state/{unit}` carries far more than `dash/live`: `schedule`, `comms`, `link`, `traffic`,\n`aim`, `contact`, `measured` — about 40 fields. That is what makes a detail page possible\ntoday without any new route.\n\n**Still true:** the browser presents and never computes, so elapsed strings and unit\nconversions stay absent with a reason until `control-surface-consolidation` (#51) lands.\n\n**Note on the reference:** node-dash\'s `/node/!8cee336b` would not render when I looked —\nit showed *"Loading node…"*, *"No device selected — go to Devices to connect"*, *0 NODES*\nand *No radios*. So the detail page is built from the `/state/{unit}` field list rather\nthan copied from a working screen.'}
2026-08-17 18:56:58,585 1578042 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': '## CONSTRAINT ADDED MID-TASK — Peter, 2026-08-17, verbatim\n\n> *"I dont want to see any dates in UTC and all date / times must be short form. the time\n> zone is the same as the host i.e BST."*\n\n**This cannot be satisfied today, and the reason is a rule, not a shortcoming.**\n\nEvery feed serves ISO UTC: `heardAt: "2026-08-17T17:01:26.608Z"`. Turning that into a\nshort-form BST string is a **conversion of a device value** — precisely what\n*"browsers cannot make decisions, they are a presentation layer ONLY"* forbids, and what\n`served-time.js` exists to do **server-side** so no browser owns a clock.\n\nSo there are exactly two ways to obey the instruction:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **A — the feed serves the string** | correct, and blocked on `control-surface-consolidation` (#51). The requirement is now concrete: short form, host-local, built server-side |\n| **B — the browser converts** | satisfies the instruction today, breaks the presentation-layer rule. Only acceptable as a **deliberate, documented exception**, never quietly |\n\n**Taken meanwhile, because a third option exists and is honest:** the ISO UTC stamps are\n**removed from the page**. They are long-form and UTC, which Peter has now explicitly\nrejected, so displaying them is wrong regardless of which route fixes it. The wire keeps\nits order — newest first conveys sequence without a clock — and the absence is named in\nthe GAP panel.\n\n**This escalates #51 from a nicety to a blocker.** The page cannot show a single timestamp\nuntil a route serves display-ready local strings. That is the sharpest possible argument\nfor the consolidation plugin and it should be put to the plugins session in those terms.\n\n**To put to Peter:** wait for the plugin (A), or take B as a written exception with the\nconversion isolated in one place so it can be deleted when the feed catches up.'}
2026-08-17 20:21:10,693 1578042 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'dashboard-fits-one-screen', 'title': 'GAP the board is a scrolling stack of full-width cards, not a dashboard grid', 'steps': ['TOOL split the monolithic device card into widgets that declare their own span', 'TOOL lay the board out on a 3-column grid inside one viewport, scrolling only inside cards', 'VERIFY nothing scrolls the page at the sizes actually used', 'DISCUSS edit mode with Peter before building it']}
2026-08-17 20:32:36,448 1578042 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'A **reusable card module**, plus a showcase page that displays every card it offers.\n\nPeter, 2026-08-17, verbatim across three messages:\n\n> *"I see no value in a full screen width char for anything ... any dashboard main panel\n> (not top stats cards) should probably be 3 columns, i.e a grid. and cards should be\n> designed to be 1 2 or 3 column widths."*\n\n> *"no vertical or hirizonta scrolling. cards can scroll but everything must be visible in\n> 1 page."*\n\n> *"we need a showcase page that shows the different cards available ... different styles\n> of header stats cards, i\'e card with large value only, cards with large value and sub\n> value ot text, cards with mini charts, cards with numeric text and the top and s small\n> chart beneath, the chart can be vertical bar or horizontal progress etc etc. my thoughts\n> are this could become a module that I can use in other dashboards, bear that in mind"*\n\n**The reuse constraint is the one that shapes everything.** The module may know nothing\nabout the bridge, about units, or about feeds. It takes plain data and returns markup.\nThat forces a clean split this app did not have:\n\n| file | knows about |\n|---|---|\n| `public/cards.js` + `cards.css` | **nothing** — tiles, panels, grid, ranges, mini charts |\n| `public/format.js` | units and durations only. Already generic |\n| `public/app.js` | mt-alarm — subscribes, maps bridge bodies onto card descriptors |\n\n**Consequences taken deliberately:**\n\n- **Tones are semantic, never colours.** `ok warn bad info neutral`, resolved through CSS\n  custom properties the host defines. A host restyles by redefining variables, not by\n  editing the module.\n- **Mini charts are SVG in viewBox units.** Host-agnostic, and it keeps the compute guard\n  satisfied without an exemption.\n- **Every tile carries a `title`** holding the raw value. The provenance principle from\n  this dashboard, generalised: formatting must never lose what was served.\n- **No fetching, no timers, no state.** The module renders what it is handed.\n\n**Established while investigating, correcting my own false claim:** history DOES exist —\n`witness` 40,001 rows and `beat` 13,557 spanning 4.5 days, with `alarm`/`witness`/`beat`\nunder no retention cap. I had said there was none, generalising from `obs.why` which\ndescribes only the obs series (24h/500). What is true is narrower: `/events` calls\n`bridge.subscribe()` with **no replay option** (`api.js:223`), so a browser gets live\nonly. Replay reaches a plugin, not a page — so a range-filtered chart is a plugin feed.'}
2026-08-17 20:44:08,904 1578042 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'park-composable-dashboard', 'title': 'DISCUSS a composable dashboard — draggable grid, pick a data group, pick its widget', 'steps': ['DISCUSS what a data group is, and where a layout is stored', 'TOOL make the panel grid a reusable module with drag and shuffle', 'TOOL let a data group be added to the board and its widget chosen']}
2026-08-17 20:44:23,494 1578042 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PARKED 2026-08-17 at Peter\'s request — *"not saying all of that should be done now …\nprobably the panel grid with drag and drop and the ability to add widgets and select data\ngropup as a /park task."*\n\nVerbatim:\n\n> *"the dashboard panel card grid can also be a resuable module, with the ability to move\n> / shuffle cards around. futher from that would be the ability for me to pich a \'data\n> group\' and be able to add that group to the dashboard and position it where I want, with\n> the ability to select the widget I want to use."*\n\n**Three layers, increasing in cost:**\n\n1. **Draggable grid** — the panel grid becomes its own reusable module; cards shuffle and\n   the arrangement persists.\n2. **Add a data group** — pick a subject (a device, a radio, the bridge itself) and place\n   it on the board.\n3. **Choose its widget** — the same group rendered as a sparkline, bars, progress, kv,\n   whatever suits.\n\n**What must be settled before any of it is built:**\n\n- **What a "data group" IS.** The natural candidates in this system are a unit, a radio,\n  a `measured.*` group, or a counter set. Until that is named, the picker has nothing\n  well-defined to offer, and guessing it wrong makes the whole feature awkward.\n- **Where a layout lives.** House precedent is node-dash\'s `public/app-persist.js` —\n  everything in one localStorage key `ui_prefs`. That keeps `html/` a static server with\n  no store and no API. Server-side persistence would mean the page server gains state,\n  which is an architectural step and Peter\'s call, not a slide.\n- **Widget/data compatibility.** A sparkline needs a series; a progress bar needs a value\n  and a range. The picker must only offer widgets a group can actually feed, or it will\n  offer combinations that render empty.\n\n**Groundwork already in place, so this is not starting from nothing:** `public/cards.js`\nalready renders every widget type from plain data, `RANGES` is exported as data, and\n`panel({span})` already declares its own width. A draggable grid is a wrapper over what\nexists rather than a rewrite.'}
2026-08-17 20:44:27,646 1578042 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_switch args={'name': 'dashboard-fits-one-screen'}
2026-08-17 20:46:09,111 1578042 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'park-composable-dashboard', 'kind': 'note', 'text': '## Peter, 2026-08-17, on why this is hard — and the reference that unlocks it\n\n> *"I also recognise that the parked task is not easy given that data groups are contain\n> different kinds of data, not sure I could code that. Think Home Assistant."*\n\nHe is right that it is the hard part, and Home Assistant is exactly the right reference\nbecause **it already solved this and the solution is small.**\n\nHA does not ask "what widget fits this data" at pick time. Every entity declares two\nthings up front:\n\n| HA concept | what it says | our equivalent |\n|---|---|---|\n| `device_class` | what the value **is** — signal strength, duration, timestamp, count | `rssi`, `beat`, `heardAt`, `contacts` |\n| `state_class` | how it **behaves** — `measurement`, `total`, `total_increasing` | `rssi` measures; `heard` only increases; `beat` is declared |\n| `unit_of_measurement` | dBm, ms, dB | already on our readings |\n\n**The picker then filters on those, not on the data itself.** A sparkline offers itself\nfor anything with `state_class: measurement`. A progress bar needs a measurement plus a\ndeclared min and max. A counter tile takes `total_increasing`. Nothing has to inspect a\nvalue to decide what can draw it.\n\n**So the work is a small classification table, not an inference engine** — and this\nsystem is unusually well placed to build one, because the bridge already publishes the\nprovenance HA has to be told: `beatSource`, `basis`, `source`, `declaredMs` beside\n`meanMs`, `n` beside every measurement. A `measured.*` group is self-describing in a way\nan HA entity is not.\n\n**Where that lands in this repo:** the classification belongs beside the data, not in the\npage — so it is a plugin\'s business, or a small map in `app.js` keyed by field name until\na plugin owns it. `cards.js` stays ignorant: it should accept a declared class and render,\nnever sniff.\n\n**Cheap first step whenever this is unparked:** tag the fields we already render with a\nclass and a state class, and have the showcase list which widgets accept each. That is a\ntable and a filter, and it is most of the feature.'}
