2026-08-18 09:30:56,117 1549532 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-18, going AFK: *"implement the skills for flow_onboard and the other skills we discussed. so that I can start using this in other repos."* Then, correcting me mid-setup: *"all skills related to this must be prefixed with `flow_`."*\n\nThree things block another repo, all verified rather than assumed:\n\n1. **Only `flow` is on PATH.** `command -v flow-derive` and `flow-dash` → nothing. Everything this project did yesterday used `python3 derive.py` from one directory.\n2. **No skills exist** — `ls ~/.claude/skills/ | grep flow` is empty.\n3. **The tools print commands that will not resolve** — `derive.py:833`, `dash.py:837`, and the footer of every generated page. 67 occurrences of the hyphenated names across the repo, 8 of them commands a user is told to type.\n\n**Underscore, not hyphen, and the binaries follow the skills** so the word typed in a terminal and the word typed in a session are the same. `flow` itself stays plain — it predates the convention, is not a verb, and is referenced from another repo\'s charts. Undo: delete the two symlinks in `/usr/local/bin` and revert the commit.\n\nDone when a repo that is NOT flow has been onboarded end to end from a copy (so nothing anyone else owns is touched), `command -v flow_derive` resolves, and a committed test fails if a `flow-` name is ever reintroduced.'}
2026-08-18 09:40:47,385 1549532 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'Spec: `docs/specs/skills-for-other-repos.md` (attached). Register: `the-tools-name-commands-that-do-not-exist` and `flow-cannot-be-used-in-another-repo`, both **CLOSED/commit on `983d3e2`**.\n\n1. **Done** — `/usr/local/bin/flow_derive` and `flow_dash` installed as symlinks; both run from a directory that is not the repo.\n2. **Done** — `~/.claude/skills/flow_onboard/SKILL.md`, the only one that thinks: refuse to invent areas from filenames, assign every symbol, declare underivable areas with `symbols: []` so they read UNKNOWN, never name an area `areas` or `index`.\n3. **Done** — `~/.claude/skills/flow_derive/SKILL.md`: drift, draw, **read the diff**, and the refusal table with what to do about each.\n4. **Done** — `~/.claude/skills/flow_audit/SKILL.md`: read-only, calls derive, four questions in the order they cost somebody, and an explicit ban on redrawing to make drift green.\n5. **Done** — a copy of `xsession/xs.py` onboarded end to end in the scratchpad: 35 symbols, 9 areas declared, 8 drawn, `lifecycle` UNKNOWN, `drift` → `8 area(s) match the code`, one chart read in a browser.\n6. **Done** — `README.md` has the install and the three skills.\n\n**The trial caught the skill being wrong**, which is the most useful thing here: step 1 printed only functions and constants, so a class was missed and `draw` refused with *"Board in no area"*. The skill now groups the inventory by kind. The gate working on an unfamiliar repo, first time, is the evidence that it will work on Peter\'s others.\n\n`tests/test_invocations.py` (12 cases) is the standing check: every `flow` name in a `.py` or `SKILL.md` must resolve — as a command if bare, as an installed skill if written `/flow_x`. It also fails if a hyphenated name is reintroduced.'}
