2026-08-18 10:19:51,693 1664209 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'session-passkey-refresh-not-holding', 'title': 'BUG: session_passkey_ttl_s reads 0 on both connected radios — the 240s refresh loop is not holding the key alive, so admin writes may carry an expired passkey', 'steps': ['Investigate: why the passkey refresh loop is not renewing — is the loop running, is get_owner_request being sent, is the ADMIN_APP reply reaching set_session_passkey', 'Document: spec covering the root cause and the exact change', 'Implement', 'Observe: live measurement — TTL must stay above zero on a connected device, with falsification', 'Track']}
2026-08-18 10:20:03,165 1664209 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Make the BLE session passkey stay valid for the whole time a device is connected, so admin writes (config SET, OTA, factory reset) never go out carrying an expired key.\n\nSymptom, measured 2026-08-18 09:41-09:44 against the running gateway on localhost:8001: GET /devices reports session_passkey_ttl_s = 0 for BOTH connected radios (!fa39f7b4 fw 2.7.26.54e0d8d, !2687afb1 fw 2.8.0.793a749), sampled every 30s for 8 consecutive samples. Both were state=READY throughout.\n\nThat should be impossible. core/ble_device.py:188 sets _PASSKEY_TTL_S = 300 (device-side expiry) and core/ble_device.py:1270-1275 refreshes every admin_passkey_refresh_s = 240 (core/config.py:46). A connected device should therefore never read below 60.\n\nWhy it matters beyond the number: core/ble_device.py:882-883 injects the passkey into every admin message. If firmware silently discards an admin SET bearing an expired passkey, this is a candidate root cause for the config writes that report success and do not stick — the fault node-dash raised as [config-role-write-not-adopted]. That link is a HYPOTHESIS, not established: whether firmware drops expired-passkey admin messages has not been measured.\n\nOut of scope for this task, recorded in the bugs ledger: verified:true meaning only that the BLE write returned, the optimistic cache write, the absent fresh read, ?full=1 being silently ignored, the hardcoded traceroute channel, private_app dropping packet metadata, and /schema deriving enums from the pip meshtastic package rather than the radio.'}
2026-08-18 10:27:03,816 1664209 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'Findings surfaced 2026-08-18 while answering node-dash/services on /xsession and investigating session-passkey-refresh-not-holding. All out of scope for that task. Boundary applied throughout, per Peter: mesh-gw is the BLE LAYER — the equivalent of the Meshtastic phone app\'s BLE transport. Connection, bonding, session passkey, admin framing, bytes in and out intact. It decides nothing. Several entries below are defects only because the bridge climbed above that layer, and their fix is REMOVAL, not more machinery.\n\nverified-true-is-not-verification — CONFIRMED\ncore/methods.py:266-278. _write_direct returns {"verified": True} the moment write_gatt_char returns. Meshtastic config SET is fire-and-forget; the protocol has no acknowledgement. The bridge is inventing a claim about an outcome it never observed. Fix is to stop claiming it: return "sent". Reported to node-dash as their [config-role-write-not-adopted].\n\nconfig-cache-updated-optimistically-on-write — CONFIRMED\ncore/methods.py:318-322 overwrites bridge.config[section] with the values just sent, and core/methods.py:153-164 get_config_live serves that cache with no radio round-trip (same for get_channel_live :167, get_owner_live :185). Consequence, demonstrated by node-dash over two weeks: a consumer\'s write-then-read-back compares its own input against itself and can never detect a failed write. A transport should not own an authoritative view of device state. Fix direction is removal plus pass-through of get_config_request, NOT a ?fresh=1 flag layered on the cache — I offered that flag to node-dash and have since withdrawn it.\n\nconfig-get-query-params-silently-ignored — CONFIRMED\nmodule/server.py:791-793 declares no query parameters, so ?full=1 and ?defaults=1 are dropped by FastAPI without error. node-dash reasonably read the resulting three-field response as a truncation. Silently ignoring a parameter is worse than rejecting it.\n\nschema-enums-derived-from-pip-package-not-radio — CONFIRMED\ncore/schema.py:81-88 builds GET /schema/{section} enum options from the descriptor in the installed meshtastic python package (2.7.9 in venv), not from the connected firmware (2.8.0.793a749 on !2687afb1). It therefore offers ROUTER_CLIENT and REPEATER, which is exactly the value node-dash wrote and which did not stick. Under the BLE-layer boundary this endpoint should not exist at all — "what values does this firmware accept" is answerable from Meshtastic\'s own protobufs by any consumer. Candidate for deletion rather than repair.\n\ntraceroute-channel-hardcoded-to-primary — CONFIRMED\ncore/ble_device.py:896-917 send_traceroute never assigns inner.channel, so proto3 default 0 applies and every traceroute goes out on channel index 0 = PRIMARY. Only call path is POST /{node_id}/traceroute -> core/methods.py:532. node-dash dispatched 37,424 of these before stopping. MeshPacket.channel is a protocol field; hiding it is the bridge deciding. Exposing it is mine and unblocked. Whether ABSENCE should be an error rather than PRIMARY is policy and sits with the consumer — node-dash asked, decision outstanding.\n\nprivate-app-event-drops-packet-metadata — CONFIRMED\ncore/app_router.py:132-150 _typed() attaches packet_id, channel, rx_rssi, rx_snr, rx_time, hops, hop_start, hop_limit, via_mqtt, to_num to every registered-portnum event. The unregistered-portnum branch at :253-262 does not call _typed() and emits only {type, __ble_addr, addr, node_id, from_num, portnum, payload_b64}. The values were in local variables three lines above. An unrecognised portnum is precisely the case where a consumer most needs the envelope, since the payload is opaque to them. Confirmed to services that this is a gap and not their defect: the branch is `elif handler is None`, so there is no richer typed event for the same frame.\n\nwrite-failed-error-carries-no-reason — CONFIRMED\ncore/methods.py:277 raises RuntimeError(f"Write failed — {e}"). When str(e) is empty the caller receives "Write failed — " with nothing after it, which node-dash reported as undiagnosable from their side. Include the exception type.\n\nadmin-passthrough-returns-null — CONFIRMED\ncore/methods.py:516-523 admin() returns whatever send_admin returns, which is None (core/ble_device.py:866 is typed -> None). POST /{node_id}/admin therefore answers literal `null`. Observed live twice today. The caller cannot distinguish sent from silently dropped.\n\nforced-values-and-required-key-validation-are-policy — CONFIRMED\ncore/methods.py:203-207 _FORCED_VALUES overrides fields the caller submitted; core/methods.py:235-263 _validate_config_keys rejects any body missing a key present in the cache. The latter is also redundant, since core/methods.py:308 already merges submitted values over the cached section, so a partial body cannot wipe anything. Both are the transport policing its callers.\n\nsignal-poll-loop-unguarded-await — UNVERIFIED\ncore/ble_device.py:1331-1338 has the same shape as the passkey loop being fixed: unguarded await inside `while self._state == READY`. It is probably safe because _read_rssi_pct is documented at :1276-1280 as never raising, but that is a docstring, not a guarantee, and it was not tested. Recording as unverified rather than dismissing it.'}
2026-08-18 10:27:47,514 1664209 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': "PHASE 1 INVESTIGATION, 2026-08-18. Measurements all taken this morning against the running gateway (pid 1659964, started 09:08, `python3 -m module.main --http-port 8001`).\n\nWHAT I RULED OUT, each by measurement rather than reading:\n\n1. The ADMIN_APP routing path is NOT broken. meshtastic's registry does have a handler with a protobufFactory for portnum 6 (checked directly: KnownProtocol(name='admin', protobufFactory=AdminMessage)), so core/app_router.py:248-250 reaches _handle_admin rather than falling through to the private_app branch at :253.\n\n2. The radio has NOT stopped answering. Hand-sent get_owner_request via POST /{id}/admin:\n     10:21  !fa39f7b4 (fw 2.7.26.54e0d8d)  ttl 0 -> 297, new key\n     10:24  !2687afb1 (fw 2.8.0.793a749)   ttl 0 -> 298, new key\n   Both firmware generations answer, the reply carries session_passkey, and set_session_passkey (core/ble_device.py:2418-2421) stores it. The whole capture path works on demand.\n\n3. The `while self._state == READY` guard is NOT failing at creation. _transition(READY) is at core/ble_device.py:2080; the task is created at :2098-2100, after it. My first hypothesis (loop exits immediately because state is not yet READY) is WRONG.\n\n4. The refresh interval is not misconfigured. GET /bridge_config reports admin_passkey_refresh_s = 240 at both levels, against _PASSKEY_TTL_S = 300 (core/ble_device.py:188).\n\nWHAT THE EVIDENCE ACTUALLY SHOWS:\n     09:41-10:21  ttl = 0 on BOTH radios, 20 samples at 30s, both READY throughout\n     10:23:41     !fa39f7b4's own loop fired unaided: ttl 173 -> 295\nSo one device's loop is demonstrably alive, and for 43 minutes neither device's loop had fired. That is a per-device loop that stops, not a broken mechanism.\n\nROOT CAUSE (structural, and independent of which loop stopped):\ncore/ble_device.py:1270-1275\n\n    async def _passkey_refresh_loop(self, client):\n        while self._state == READY:\n            await self._send_admin_get_owner(client)\n            await asyncio.sleep(self._ble_cfg.admin_passkey_refresh_s)\n\n_send_admin_get_owner (:1249-1267) ends in `await client.write_gatt_char(...)`, which raises BleakError on any transient BLE fault. Nothing catches it. The exception propagates out of the coroutine, the task created at :2098 dies, and:\n  - nothing awaits _passkey_task, so the exception is never retrieved or logged\n  - nothing restarts it\n  - the device stays READY, so no state machine notices\n  - session_passkey_ttl_s decays to 0 and pins there for the rest of the connection\nOne transient error therefore permanently disables passkey refresh for that device, silently. Every admin message sent afterwards (config SET, OTA, factory reset — core/ble_device.py:882-883 injects the key into all of them) carries a passkey the radio has already expired.\n\nWHAT IS NOT PROVEN, stated as unproven: that a raised BleakError is what actually stopped !2687afb1's loop today. The process logs to a socket, not a file (/proc/1659964/fd/1 -> socket:[9272419]), so there is no history to read and the trigger is unrecoverable after the fact. The defect is structural and fixable without knowing the trigger; the fix should also make the next occurrence visible, which is precisely what is missing now.\n\nSTILL MEASURING at time of writing: a discriminator sampling both devices every 20s after the hand-probes. If !2687afb1's loop is dead its ttl decays from 298 to 0 and never recovers; if alive it jumps back near 300 within 240s. Result folds into Phase 4.\n\nCALLERS AND DEPENDENTS of anything that would change: _passkey_refresh_loop is created only at core/ble_device.py:2098-2100 and referenced as self._passkey_task. _send_admin_get_owner is called from there and from the connect sequence at :1274. Nothing else calls either. The sibling loops created alongside (_schedule_priority_downgrade :1244, _signal_poll_loop :1331, _notify_loop :2209) are untouched by this change; _signal_poll_loop has the same unguarded shape but calls _read_rssi_pct, documented as never raising — recorded in the bugs ledger as unverified rather than assumed safe.\n\nRISK: a guarded loop that logs and continues will keep sending get_owner_request into a genuinely dead link every 240s. That is bounded and harmless (one admin write per device per interval, addressed to self over BLE, no mesh transmission), and the `while self._state == READY` guard still ends it on disconnect."}
