2026-08-18 12:16:01,876 1578042 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': '## page-cannot-post-to-the-bridge — CONFIRMED, NOT OURS, blocks the command card\n\n**Status:** CONFIRMED by measurement in a real browser, not inferred.\n\nThe dashboard is served from `:8791`; the bridge is `:8790`. A command is\n`POST /queue`, and the browser refuses it. Console, verbatim:\n\n```\nAccess to fetch at \'http://192.168.10.205:8790/queue\' from origin\n\'http://192.168.10.205:8791\' has been blocked by CORS policy: Response to\npreflight request doesn\'t pass access control check: No \'Access-Control-Allow-Origin\'\nheader is present on the requested resource.\n```\n\n**Both routes are shut, and I tried both:**\n\n| attempt | result |\n|---|---|\n| `content-type: application/json` | preflighted, `OPTIONS /queue` → **404**, blocked |\n| `content-type: text/plain` (a simple request, no preflight) | sent, but the response is unreadable — no `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` |\n\nSo there is no header combination a page can use. `grep -cin \'access-control\\|cors\'`\non `bridge/src/api.js` → **0**.\n\n**Why the websockets work and this does not:** WS is not subject to CORS. Every\nlive feed this dashboard reads is a socket, which is why nothing has hit this until\na command was wanted.\n\n**NOT OURS.** The fix is a bridge change — answer `OPTIONS` and send\n`Access-Control-Allow-Origin` for the page\'s origin. Raised rather than worked\naround; a `text/plain` body to dodge the preflight would be exactly the kind of\nworkaround that makes their gap permanent and invisible.\n\n**What is NOT known:** whether Peter wants the bridge to accept a browser origin at\nall. It binds `0.0.0.0` deliberately, but "reachable by curl on the LAN" and\n"scriptable from any page a browser has open" are different exposures, and that is\nhis call rather than mine or bridge\'s.\n\n---\n\n## queue-contract-is-two-fields — not a defect, recorded because the card depends on it\n\n`POST /queue` takes `{unit, text}` — both required. `text` is a verb line, not a\nstructured object: *"A device verb, with arguments"*, example `interval 60000`.\n\n35 verbs, served by the bridge itself at `/schema` under `x-deviceVerbs`, described\nas *"Every verb the firmware dispatcher matches, generated from main.cpp"*:\n\n```\nagc alarm cam camu chunk config debug detect echo env help hop interval lname ltc\nname nodes onchange ping push reboot replyack sch schema simenv simpir site\nsleepdbg sleepfor sleepmode status telemetry txp wedge window\n```\n\n**The list is generated from the firmware and served live**, so the card must read\nit from `/schema` rather than hardcode it — a hardcoded list would drift the first\ntime a verb is added and would drift silently.\n\n**The 200 is not a receipt.** The bridge\'s own words: *"Accepted immediately;\ntransmitted when the bridge next hears the unit. This response is not evidence of\ndelivery — read `traffic` on `GET /state/{unit}`, or subscribe to `reply` on the\nwebsocket."* The card must show queued and delivered as two different states, or it\nwill lie about the most important thing it does.'}
