2026-08-18 13:16:31,695 1710223 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'SHIPPED AND DEPLOYED 2026-08-18. pm2 restart of `bridge` at 12:15:32Z. NOT YET COMMITTED — Peter\nhas not asked for a commit.\n\nTHE BUG, PROVEN TWICE BEFORE IT WAS FIXED. Not reasoned about:\n\n  live daemon, curl   POST /queue, Content-Type: text/plain, Origin: https://evil.example\n                      -> reached the handler, answered about carried units\n  test, before fix    same shape -> 202 ACCEPTED, bridge.send() called\n                      test/who-may-command.test.js:62, observed failing 202 !== 403\n\nsrc/api.js never read Content-Type (grep -c was 0), so a CORS *simple request* — which a browser\nsends with NO preflight — was parsed and queued. The page cannot read the reply, which looks like a\nrefusal and is not one.\n\nWHAT WAS BUILT\n  src/callers.js        NEW. allowsOrigin / mayWrite / isWrite. Returns a REASON, never a bool.\n  src/api.js            guard before routing and before any body read; CORS echo; OPTIONS route\n  bin/bridged.js        --allow-origin, --allow-subnet (both derived by default)\n  src/surface.js        403 on POST /queue and DELETE /queue/{unit}, plus the preflight\n  test/callers.test.js         NEW, 13\n  test/who-may-command.test.js NEW, 10\n\nBY METHOD, NEVER BY PATH. POST /pointer/aim moves the ROTATOR — hardware shared with mt-radar — and\nis a plugin route the core knows nothing about. A /queue-only guard would have fixed the route I was\nlooking at and left the one that reaches outside this project open. Tested explicitly.\n\nDERIVED, NOTHING TYPED. The allowlist is os.networkInterfaces(): a page served by this host may\ncommand it, nothing else may. So html on :8791 works without this repo knowing html\'s port. No\nAccess-Control-Max-Age is sent, and that is derived too — one outstanding command at a time (409)\nmeans the preflight cost is negligible, and a cached one would ignore a changed --allow-origin.\n\nVERIFIED ON THE LIVE SERVICE\n  restart clean       2 units restored, 4 plugins (pointer/dash/notify/probe), rotator reconnected,\n                      pointer re-armed for !8cee336b\n  the old exploit     403 "a page at https://evil.example may not command this bridge"\n  nothing queued      sent 0, failed 0, queued 0, pending 0 after the refused attempt\n  preflight           204 + Allow-Origin + Vary + Allow-Methods for :8791; 403 for evil.example\n  no Origin           still reaches the handler — curl, mesh-gw, node-dash, CLI untouched\n  GET foreign origin  200, unchanged, and no Allow-Origin header\n  /events no params   open, tick arriving — node-dash\'s exact shape, unaffected\n  all plugin routes   200\n\nnpm test 243 pass / 0 fail (was 220).\n\nTHE LIMIT, RECORDED SO NOBODY READS THIS AS MORE THAN IT IS. Origin is forged trivially by anything\nthat is not a browser — proven with curl in this session. This defends against BROWSERS ONLY. Every\nunauthenticated caller that can reach the port can still queue commands, exactly as before, because\nthere is no auth of any kind on this surface. Peter was told this in those words before approving.\n\nTHE SUBNET CHECK does not help with this bug and must never be described as if it does: the hostile\npage runs in the operator\'s own browser at a LAN address and passes it trivially. Measured — one\ninterface, one route in — so today it refuses nothing. Peter was told this before he approved it.\n\nSTILL OWED: html tests the JSON command path from a REAL browser. curl cannot prove a browser\'s\npreflight behaviour, and their dashboard is the only real browser client.'}
