2026-08-18 22:45:27,578 1756978 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Every field the device puts on the air carries a human-readable label, and the label carries the unit.\n\nPeter, 2026-08-18: "EVERY SINGLE FIELD in the schema must have a HR label, even rssi which would be RSSI", and "Uptime (secs) is a HR lable".\n\nMeasured before: 235 type.key pairs across 42 frames; only the 75 rows in settings/frames had labels, and those omitted units. Everything a dashboard renders — the replies — had none, so html was showing raw keys.\n\nSpec: docs/specs/command-and-telemetry/schema-field-labels.md\nCheck: rak/tools/check_labels.py — 235 pairs, 42 frames, exit 0'}
2026-08-18 22:45:40,185 1756978 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'Labels authored in schema/frames.json (product root, shared), then a check that re-extracts from the C and fails on any unlabelled pair.\n\nThree extractor shapes were needed, and the first two silently missed a whole frame: a `const JField x[] = {}` literal, an `f[n++] = {}` builder (this is how `status` is built — invisible to the first two extractors, found only because the pair count looked wrong), and a hand-built sprintf JSON string. Count went 176 -> 200 -> 235 as each shape was added. A clean run means "the shapes I know about are covered", not "everything is".\n\nFalsified five ways against a mutated copy — label removed / blanked / equal to the key / unit dropped / duplicate reintroduced. All five go red, control stays green.\n\nFOUND EN ROUTE, NOT FIXED — the schema hash covers the raw file bytes (gen_frames.py:46, mirrored deliberately in bridge/src/frames.js:24-25), so a labels-and-comments-only edit moved it 0xd339 -> 0x6911. A deployed unit transmits 0xd339 and frames.js:108 throws on mismatch, so the next bridge restart stops decoding the fleet. Raised with bridge as [hash-covers-comments]; the whole-file-vs-wire-projection question is theirs plus Peter\'s. Live bridge still holds the old bytes in memory, so nothing is broken this instant.\n\nALSO FOUND, NOT FIXED — `type` is not unique on the wire: the alarm EVENT (main.cpp:1459/1473) and the `status alarm` domain reply (MtCmd.cpp:306) both call themselves "alarm". Ids do not overlap so labelling is unambiguous, but a consumer cannot route on `type`.\n\nWire keys deliberately NOT renamed: interval.secs, window.secs, sleepfor.secs and agc.sec all carry ms, and pong.upt is secs. The label states the unit; renaming would break every consumer at once and is Peter\'s call.'}
