2026-08-19 08:08:35,269 1774009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'BUG inFlight never returns to zero after a giveup, because dequeue does not clear the pending POST', 'description': "Measured 2026-08-19 07:07Z: GARG reports `queued:0, inFlight:1` with `lastSendAt\n2026-08-18T22:43:17.919Z` — nine hours stale, and the command it belonged to gave up at 22:58:15Z.\n\n`src/bridge.js:1237` computes inFlight as `[...pending.values()].filter(e => e.unit === u).length`.\nThe giveup path at `:956-980` calls `dequeue(next)` and publishes, but never removes the entry from\n`pending`. A POST to mesh-gw that never settles therefore leaks forever. The log shows mesh-gw was\nrefusing connections on 08-18 (837 x ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:8001, last 13:31:55Z), which is how a\nPOST comes to hang.\n\nTWO CONSEQUENCES, not one. The count is wrong, and round-trip's dirty-queue guard tests `queued`\nonly — so it starts happily against a bridge that is in this state and reports whatever follows as\na link result."}
2026-08-19 08:08:53,758 1774009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'GAP round-trip proves seven hops from four event kinds while the bridge now publishes sixteen and a rich state', 'description': 'Audited 2026-08-19 against the running bridge. round-trip was written ~2026-08-11; the bridge has\ngained a great deal since and the tool reads almost none of it.\n\nWATCHES 4 OF 16. `bin/round-trip.js` keys on `send`, `onair`, `reply`, `giveup` only.\n`EVENT_KINDS` (bin/bridged.js:36) publishes queue, send, onair, witness, reply, orphan, giveup,\ncancel, hold, overdue, returned, schedule + reply/beat/alarm/chunk/unknown.\n\nTHE ONE THAT MATTERS MOST IS `orphan` — a reply the bridge received and could NOT correlate. That is\nprecisely the "the reply existed and the bridge missed it" case the tool currently infers from\nmesh-gw as an oracle. The bridge now says it directly and the tool does not listen.\n\nAlso unread: `overdue`/`returned` (the unit went silent / came back), `schedule` (the unit redeclared\nits cadence mid-run, which moves the wake the backstop was derived from), `hold` and `pointer.aim`\n(the rotator aim, which for a unit 2501 m away is part of the chain), `beat`/`alarm` (the unit\nspeaking at all).\n\nSTATE FIELDS IGNORED, all now on GET /state/{unit}:\n  measured.landing.insideWindow / outsideWindow   whether our frame arrived while the unit was\n                                                  actually listening — the difference between\n                                                  "radiated" and "arrived in time", and currently\n                                                  invisible to the tool\n  comms.lastRx                                    the DEVICE saying it received our packet id\n  comms.answering                                 the DEVICE saying it answered our packet id\n  measured.radios[].direct / relayed              GARG is only ever heard relayed, hops=1\n  contact.overdue / missed, measured.lateWakes\n\ncomms.lastRx and comms.answering are what actually resolved the 2026-08-18 ping by hand. A tool\nwhose purpose is to name the failing hop had to be replaced by a human with a sqlite prompt, which\nis the whole argument for injecting them as hops.\n\nCANDIDATE NEW HOPS, in chain order, for Peter to accept or cut:\n  rotator aimed (pointer.aim/hold)  -> before the send, deployed units only\n  landed in the window (landing)    -> between "on the mesh" and "device heard"\n  device says it received (lastRx)  -> independent of any reply arriving\n  device says it answered (answering) -> the `ans` evidence, currently only reachable by hand\n  reply correlated OR orphaned      -> distinguishes "no reply" from "reply not matched"\n  entry settled (queued/inFlight 0) -> would have caught the leak in the step above'}
2026-08-19 08:09:02,120 1774009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'GAP maxSends is not published on any route, so no client can know how long the bridge will keep trying', 'description': '`maxSends = 3` (src/bridge.js:200) is the retry budget: the bridge spends one send per heard contact\nand then gives up. Checked 2026-08-19 — it appears on NONE of /health, /stats, /schema.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING TIDINESS. Any client that waits for a command to settle has to\ndecide how long to wait, and the honest answer is "until the bridge stops trying" — which it cannot\ndiscover. round-trip therefore had to bound its backstop by the unit\'s `schedule.must` instead, and\na `--timeout` that hard-coded 3 beats would be this repo guessing at its own configuration.\n\nSame family as `feedTickMs`, which IS published on /health precisely so a consumer does not\nhard-code our interval in its own repo. maxSends belongs beside it.'}
2026-08-19 08:41:59,512 1774009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'BUG restore moves link.heardAt BACKWARDS, and contact.overdue is computed off it, so a restart manufactures a false overdue', 'description': 'Measured 2026-08-19. The bridge restarted at 07:30:03Z and restored /var/lib/mt-bridge/state.json.\nImmediately afterwards BOTH carried units reported `contact.overdue: true` and these heardAt values:\n\n  /state/!8cee336b   link.heardAt  2026-08-19T07:13:16.165Z\n  /state/!987ab80f   link.heardAt  2026-08-19T07:08:08.828Z\n\nTHE STORE ALREADY KNEW BETTER. data/bridge.db holds GARG contacts at 07:28:17Z and 07:28:25Z — after\nthe restored snapshot and before the restart. So the restore moved "last heard" BACKWARDS by 15\nminutes, and nothing reconciles the restored snapshot against the rows already on disk.\n\n`contact.overdue` and `contact.dueAt` are then computed from that regressed value, so the restart\nINVENTED an overdue on both units. Neither was late: GARG\'s next wake was 07:43:17Z and it had been\nheard 12 minutes earlier.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. `overdue` on the only route to the units is the signal that sends\nsomebody to look at a device 2501 m away. Measured across the whole 6-day store, GARG has 1659\ncontacts, a max gap of 910 s and ZERO gaps over 20 minutes — it has never actually missed a beat. A\nfalse overdue is therefore the ONLY kind this project has ever produced.\n\nIT ALSO MISLED THIS SESSION. I read the regressed heardAt off /state and reported to Peter that GARG\nhad not been heard for 26 minutes. Peter did not accept it, and the store proved him right. Second\ntime in one session that the bridge\'s own summary was trusted over the underlying rows.\n\nFIX DIRECTION, not yet specced: on restore, take the LATER of the snapshot and what the store holds,\nper unit — and never let heardAt go backwards. A monotonic guard is cheap and the absence of one is\nwhat produced this.'}
2026-08-19 08:42:18,805 1774009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "MEASURE the rotator sits 19 degrees off GARG's aim between holds, and the bridge cannot tell whether that costs a wake", 'description': 'Measured 2026-08-19 07:39Z. GET /pointer reports `az: 141, held: false, busy: false`, while every\naim in the log puts GARG at az 121-123:\n\n  07:28:17Z  [pointer] aim !8cee336b -> az 121 by geometry (best heard -115 dBm, n=96), hold 16000ms\n  07:28:20Z  [pointer] rotator done seek2az az=122 ok=true\n  ...then released, and by 07:39 the rotator reads az 141\n\n19 degrees off, and nothing in this repo moved it. The rotator is SHARED HARDWARE with mt-radar\n(mt-radar/node-dash/docs/ALARM_TRANSPORT_BOUNDARY.md: point and hold briefly, then release; yield\nrather than queue). So this is the arbitration working as designed on our side and radar taking it\nback afterwards, which is correct behaviour and still a fact worth having.\n\nWHY IT IS A MEASURE AND NOT YET A BUG. The 16 s hold DID span GARG\'s 07:28:22Z wake and the aim\ncompleted at 07:28:20Z, so on this occasion the aerial was pointed when it mattered. What is missing\nis any way to know that in general:\n\n  - nothing records the rotator azimuth AT THE MOMENT a frame from GARG arrives\n  - `measured.radios[\'!fa39f7b4\']` holds rssi/snr and direct-vs-relayed but not where the dish was\n  - so "GARG arrived at -115 dBm" cannot be separated from "arrived while we were pointed 19 deg off"\n\nGARG\'s own numbers make this worth closing: it is heard almost entirely RELAYED (hops=1, via node 4)\nat -118 to -122 dBm, and the pointer\'s geometry aim is computed from a best-heard of -108 to -115.\nA standing 19 degree offset on a link with that little margin is the difference between a relayed\nsighting and none.\n\nCANDIDATE: stamp the pointer azimuth onto the witness/heard record, so a hop can later ask "were we\naimed at it when it spoke". Depends on nothing else in this task.'}
