2026-08-19 09:57:18,044 1774009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'BUG a config frame confirming the commanded change is proof of execution, and settlement ignores it', 'description': 'Measured 2026-08-19 on GARG, and this is SHARPER than step 1 (rxid unread) because the bridge holds\nthe proof in its OWN published state and still contradicts it.\n\nPeter queued `interval 90000` to !8cee336b at 08:29:38Z. Trace:\n\n  08:43:16.237  obs     beat=900000  ans=685ae32a       (99 ms BEFORE our send — not ours)\n  08:43:16.336  send    id=4154375385 "@336b interval 90000"\n  08:43:18.483  onair   witnessed by !fa39f7b4 -31 dBm\n  08:43:25.190  config  beat=90000   foreign=0xd339     <-- GARG CONFIRMING THE CHANGE\n  08:43:30.644  beat    TELEMETRY_APP\n  08:45:03.189  overdue\n\nGET /state/!8cee336b then reads:\n\n  schedule.beat  90000        at 2026-08-19T08:43:25.189Z   <-- the bridge BELIEVED the config frame\n  traffic        queued: 1, inFlight: 1                      <-- and still holds the command open\n\nSo the bridge accepted the unit\'s statement that the command took effect, wrote it into its own\nschedule, and will still retransmit `interval 90000` at the next wake — then give up "unanswered"\nabout a command its own state proves succeeded.\n\nWHY IT HAPPENS. `settleByAns` (src/bridge.js:369) settles on ONE piece of evidence: `obs.ans` naming\none of the entry\'s packet ids. A `config` frame carries no `ans` at all (ans=None above), so the\nsettle path cannot fire even in principle. There is no code path from "the device confirmed the\nchange I asked for" to "the command is settled".\n\nNOT THE SAME AS STEP 1. rxid says "the unit RECEIVED it". This says "the unit DID it, and here is the\nnew value". It is stronger evidence and it is equally ignored.\n\nALSO VISIBLE ONLY BECAUSE OF TODAY\'S TOLERANCE FIX. The config frame reads foreign=0xd339 — before\nthis morning it would have been refused, and this finding would have been invisible for the same\nreason last night\'s was.\n\nCANDIDATE FIX, not specced: settle a command when a config frame reports the value the command asked\nfor. Needs care — it means parsing the verb\'s intent, which the bridge deliberately does not do\ntoday ("this file adds no knowledge"). A narrower version: settle when a config frame arrives within\nthe wake that followed our send AND the entry\'s verb is one that mutates config. Discuss before\nbuilding; the wrong version would settle commands that silently failed.\n\nSEPARATE, IN THE SAME TRACE: no `interval` reply frame ever arrived, same as the two lost `ping`\nreplies. And `overdue` at 08:45:03 is arithmetic on the NEW 90 s beat while the unit is still\ndraining its last OLD 900 s sleep — BNCH did exactly this at 07:23 and came back after one full old\nbeat. So that overdue is probably false, which is step 6\'s family.'}
