2026-08-19 10:07:42,738 1787535 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': '**Peter, 2026-08-18, verbatim — the second time he has explained this, which is the signal that the first explanation did not land:**\n\n> *"i told you the analogy. think of a test point on a PCB. the TP node needs to point to either a flow line or to another node. putting it on a flow line pointing inwards indicates that a test can be done at this point. so a flow may have many test points. and it can prove that at TP 1 data was verified, at TP2 it failed, so the error must be between them. right now TPs are invisble to me and the whole point of this is visibility"*\n\n**And earlier, 2026-08-18, which I under-read:**\n\n> *"you do know the purpose of test point dont you. this is like a test point on a circuit board. it is used to attache verifyable test/pass"*\n\n**What I built, and why it is wrong.** `prove:` is an ATTRIBUTE worn by an existing node — the node keeps its type and gains a small chip. Three consequences, all of them fatal to the purpose:\n\n1. **A TP cannot sit on a flow line.** Edges are `[from, to, label]` with no identity, so nothing can point at one. But a probe belongs on the WIRE — that is what a pad on a net is.\n2. **There is no localisation.** The entire diagnostic value is that TP1 passed and TP2 failed, so the fault is BETWEEN them. That needs many probes along one path and a reading of the span. Three probes on three separate charts cannot bisect anything.\n3. **It is invisible**, which he states is the whole point: a 9.6px chip inside a node, no legend entry, and 13 of 16 charts carrying none.\n\n**The corrected model, in his terms:**\n\n- A TP is a NODE — the seventh widget (he chose this explicitly when asked).\n- It POINTS AT something: a flow line, or another node. On a line, the arrow points **inwards**.\n- It means *a test can be done at this point*.\n- **A flow may have MANY.** Not one demonstrative marker per chart.\n- Read together they localise: last TP that passed → first that failed → the fault is in the span between.\n\n**What this makes non-negotiable:** edges need identity, or nothing can point at a flow line.\n\n**Recorded against myself:** I wrote "prove: stays an attribute rather than a seventh widget. A pad is not a component either" into the previous task\'s goal note as a decision, from his first explanation, and never checked it with him. It was the exact opposite of what he meant, and it stood for a day.'}
2026-08-19 10:08:55,018 1787535 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_file_attach args={'file_path': 'docs/specs/test-points-probe-the-line.md', 'kind': 'plan', 'label': 'A test point probes the line, and many of them find the fault'}
2026-08-19 10:22:51,576 1787535 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': '**Peter, 2026-08-19, verbatim — on what a test point IS:**\n\n> *"a test point indicates where a test should happen, the tool(s) that are used and the pass fail criteria. so it defines a test but it does not do the test or carry any pass/fail state."*\n\n**Peter, 2026-08-19, verbatim — on how it must be drawn:**\n\n> *"hang on, the problem is this might mess up the layout if this is a large card the same as the others. it needs to be relatively smaller and contain only a terse description on the page. mouse over / tooltip can reveal the full description and a click should either open a modal (which can be a common resuable modal) or it opens a page to the test definition. ot would be noce if this was round so it is very distinctive from all other dards. that might not be easy as a grid is sqlare, but id does need to be very distingusable from other nodes. another consideration is layering with test points being a layer ?"*\n\n**My reading, kept separate so it can be checked against his words:**\n\n1. **A TP is a DEFINITION, and stateless.** Where a test should happen · the tool(s) · the pass/fail criteria. It does not run anything and it does not hold a verdict. The repo\'s data model already matches this and I had been describing it wrongly: the chart stores `prove: {run:, exit:}` — the definition — and `docs/flow/proof.yaml` stores `{verdict, got, at, commit, source_sha256}` — the reading. Verified by reading both files. What was wrong was my LANGUAGE and the rendering, which drew the verdict inside the node as though the node owned it.\n\n2. **A TP IS A LAYER, not a grid cell.** This is his suggestion and it dissolves the problem he opened with: a card in the grid competes for a column, widens rows and can push the layout about. A TP positioned in an overlay — the same way the wires already are — occupies no cell, so it cannot disturb the flow at all. It is also what makes ROUND achievable: the grid is square, an overlay is not constrained by it.\n\n3. **Consequence: a TP needs no `at:`.** Its position comes from what it probes. That deletes a field, a class of layout collision, and the `relocate()` interaction entirely.\n\n4. **Terse on the page, full on demand.** Small round pad with a short label · `title=` for the full description on hover · click opens a REUSABLE modal carrying the whole definition.\n\n**Decision taken, reversible:** modal rather than a page. A page navigation loses your place in the chart, and inside the dashboard\'s iframe it would either replace the chart or open a second surface. One modal, one implementation, used by every chart.\n\n**Open, and his to say:** whether the verdict appears in the modal at all. It is a READING and not part of the definition, so the honest presentation is the definition first, and the last measurement shown separately and dated — never merged into the criteria.'}
