2026-08-19 10:54:13,510 1797750 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-19: "look specboard is dead. you can fix that. the point is this repo is now invoked by idiot. hooks etc need to be adjusted and enforced to this repo. can we guard against heredoc edits since it seems that bypasses hooks and breaks the system?"\n\nThree faults. specboard\'s hook.py is still invoked by settings.json on PreToolUse, SessionStart and Stop, and the hook runs. idiot.py documents and PRINTS `acquit --id <slug>`, which argparse refuses, and while that entry is untried the hook locks idiot.py. And idiot.py:714 returns 0 for every tool that is not Edit/Write/MultiEdit/NotebookEdit, while settings.json registers it only under that matcher — so a heredoc, a sed -i or a redirect skips the register\'s enforcement entirely. This session did exactly that when it wrote docs/specs/one-idiot-skill-on-this-repo.md with a heredoc and no hook saw it.'}
2026-08-19 10:54:25,239 1797750 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': "Spec: docs/specs/hooks-enforce-this-repo.md\n\nOrder is dictated by the register, not by preference:\n1. settings.json — remove the three specboard hooks, add idiot.py under the Bash matcher. No entry names this file, so it is ordinary work.\n2. not-reproduced-prints-an-invocation-that-does-not-work — write the check, run it standalone, try (red), fix idiot.py lines 35 and 480, prove. This is what UNLOCKS idiot.py; while it is untried the hook refuses every edit to it.\n3. a-shell-write-bypasses-the-gate — add --type new, write the check, run standalone, try (red), implement, prove. The check drives hook() directly with a Bash payload so the red does not depend on settings.json having been changed.\n\nTHE RULE for the guard, deliberately broader than the register: refuse when a write target resolves inside a git repo that HAS an idiot.yaml — not merely when the target is accused. The failure is the bypass itself, not the accused-file case; a heredoc into an unaccused file in a registered repo is precisely what happened here. Permitted: anything outside a registered repo (/tmp, scratchpad, ~, ~/.claude), anything under .git/, and any command prefixed IDIOT_ALLOW_SHELL_WRITE=1.\n\nDetection is a HEURISTIC and must say so in the code, like NO_TRIAL_SIGNS already does: > and >> redirects, sed -i/--in-place, tee, dd of=, truncate -s, cp/mv/install destinations. It will miss a write inside python3 -c. False refusal costs one Edit call; false permit is the hole. Record the limit, do not claim totality.\n\nCONTROLS for the guard's check, without which it proves nothing: the same payload with IDIOT_ALLOW_SHELL_WRITE=1 must stay PERMITTED, and a payload writing to /tmp must stay PERMITTED. A guard that refuses everything is not a guard.\n\nidiot_gate.py is not touched — it is already this repo, and run-json-duplicates-idiot-yaml proposes retiring it, which a hook change should not pre-empt."}
2026-08-19 11:05:09,981 1797750 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'DONE, with one defect left open deliberately.\n\nspecboard is out of the harness: settings.json now has PreToolUse only, no SessionStart, no Stop, and grep -c specboard returns 0. idiot.py is registered under the Bash matcher as well as Write|Edit|MultiEdit|NotebookEdit.\n\nThe heredoc bypass is closed and proved live: `cat > docs/CANARY.md <<EOF` was refused by the harness itself and the file does not exist. Rule is broader than the register — any shell write whose target resolves inside a repo that HAS an idiot.yaml. Permitted: outside any repo, under .git/, reads, and IDIOT_ALLOW_SHELL_WRITE=1.\n\nClosed CLOSED/commit this session: not-reproduced-prints-an-invocation-that-does-not-work, a-shell-write-bypasses-the-gate, the-shell-guard-refuses-an-arrow-in-a-string. Register 23 of 27.\n\nTHE GUARD MISFIRED TWICE IN ONE SITTING, and both are recorded rather than smoothed over:\n1. `>` inside an arrow in a quoted string parsed as a redirect to a file named comma — refused a read-only status query ONE command after shipping. Fixed and proved (6cebd4f).\n2. The scan covers the WHOLE command string, so prose in a heredoc body mentioning a redirect reads as one. My own commit message tripped it and had to be sent with IDIOT_ALLOW_SHELL_WRITE=1. Registered as the-shell-guard-scans-heredoc-bodies-as-commands, UNTRIED and OPEN. Left for a proper trial because a guard that has misfired twice has earned one before its third change. The fix is to scan only the command line and not the heredoc body — `cat > f <<EOF` keeps its redirect on the command line, so detection survives.\n\nALSO LEARNED, and now in the skill: every option precedes the positional id. `try` takes the test as a REMAINDER, so `idiot try <slug> --criterion "..." -- <cmd>` feeds --criterion to the test and returns a mistrial. Cost one trial.\n\nThe tool refused me correctly four separate times this session —