2026-08-19 12:31:50,916 1797750 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': "Commit e31488d. Eight points, every command run by hand before it was written into a chart:\n\n  derive   idiot.py:269  is mistrial   a mistrial trial yields `mistrial`\n  closure  idiot.py:317  is commit     a really-closed entry closes on the strongest rung\n  closure  idiot.py:319  is False      THE DEFINITE NO — files_changed_between over 5101f54..58db8be for idiot.py, the historical pair that made the audit-log entry unclosable\n  enforce  idiot.py:903  is 2          a corrupt register FAILS CLOSED\n  evidence idiot.py:379  exit 0        an unlaunchable test is a mistrial, not a conviction\n  shell    idiot.py:883  is 2          a redirect into this repo is refused\n  shell    idiot.py:874  is 0          a quoted argument that merely MENTIONS a redirect is permitted\n  shell    idiot.py:856  is 0          the override permits\n\nFALSIFICATION CHANGED THE POINTS. The three shell points first called shell_hook() directly. Disabling the Bash dispatch in hook() on a throwaway copy left all three green — they proved the function computed the right answer and never that the hook ROUTES a Bash call to it, which is the wiring that was missing this morning. Re-pointed through hook(), the refusal point fails on that mutation: want '2', got '0'. The two permit points stay green in both worlds, correctly: they guard against over-refusal, not under-refusal.\n\nHarness-level falsification too: flipping `is: mistrial` to `is: guilty` reports FAIL, so points are evaluated rather than counted.\n\nMarkers survive a redraw — flow_derive re-attaches by line then by id and says loudly what it could not. Verified by drawing and re-listing.\n\nState: index 9 charts 3 holes, stamp 8, drift 8 match, prove 8 points 0 failed."}
