2026-08-19 19:31:23,419 1876987 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bridge-backlog', 'title': 'BUG the send gate fires on ANY frame heard, so half our commands are aimed at a window that has already shut', 'description': 'THE PRIMARY DEFECT. Found 2026-08-19 by measurement, not reasoning. This is the one that took the hit rate to zero.\n\nMEASURED, first attempts only, split by which frame we fired on:\n\n  fired on the announce   28 of 29 delivered   97 percent\n  fired on the drain       0 of  7 delivered    0 percent\n\nThat 97 percent is the "100 percent hit rate we had in the past" Peter remembers. Nothing regressed at 16:01:59Z - from 16:12 on, every first attempt simply happened to land on the bad phase.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. A unit transmits ONCE per 300s wake, at one of two offsets, from its own console:\n\n  LC radio  +396ms\n  LC beacon +766ms          -> announce, first TXT txgo at wake+1953ms\n  LC rxopen +2690ms win=8000\n  LC rxdone +9960ms rx=0    -> drain,    first TXT txgo at wake+10001ms\n  LC drain  +9960ms q=3\n\nThe receive window is wake+2690 to wake+10690. Fire on the announce and our frame lands inside it. Fire on the drain and the unit stopped listening BEFORE we transmitted.\n\nWHERE IT IS IN THE CODE\n  src/devstate.js:246   `if (at - s.burstStartedAt >= s.beat / 2) opened = true;`\n                        One obs per 300s wake ALWAYS clears beat/2, so every frame is treated as\n                        opening a burst, and :312 sets burstStartedAt = at.\n  src/devstate.js:111   `const opened = s.burstStartedAt;`\n  src/devstate.js:114   declares LISTENING, why: "within win=8000ms of the frame that opened the burst"\n\nSo sentInState recorded "listening" on all ten failures. It was wrong every time.\n\nTHE CODE ALREADY KNOWS. src/devstate.js:684 says the beat/2 rule "exists precisely because `win` cannot tell an announce from a drain frame". a0ca4d7 applied that insight to WAKE PREDICTION and never applied it to the send gate.\n\nCANDIDATE FIX, not specced: gate LISTENING on the frame\'s phase against the PREDICTED wake (devstate already computes expectedAt) rather than against the arrival of whatever frame was heard last. A frame arriving late-in-wake proves the window is shut, not open.\n\nTHE DEVICE IS NOT AT FAULT AND THIS IS PROVEN, not assumed. Its wake grid is exact - SLEEPNOW woke= values 300000ms apart with zero drift - and its own txgo spacing (291952/308048ms) matches the bridge\'s arrival spacing (292.0/308.1s) exactly. Peter: "do NOT blame the devices, this is a bridge issue 100%."'}
2026-08-19 19:31:40,976 1876987 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bridge-backlog', 'title': 'BUG rip out our retry loop entirely - the radio owns retransmission, and ours only ever produced duplicates the mesh discarded', 'description': 'PETER\'S RULING, 2026-08-19, VERBATIM: "well that\'s the first step rip out the retries, as I have said numerous times let mesh-gw and the radios do their job."\n\nHe has said it numerous times. Treat it as standing.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY DONE. The register entry `we-assign-packet-ids-and-retry-ourselves-instead-of-letting-the-radio` is tried-red, fixed-green, verdict guilty, and CLOSED 2026-08-19. maxSends now defaults to 1 (src/bridge.js:226) and the POST no longer supplies an id (src/bridge.js:964). Deployed 17:09Z. NOT YET PROVEN IN THE FIELD - zero sends since deploy.\n\nWHAT IS NOT DONE. Capping a loop at 1 is not ripping it out. maxSends is still a live parameter doing THREE different jobs, and they must be separated before it is deleted:\n\n  src/bridge.js:1442  `if (next.sends >= maxSends)` - THE RETRY BUDGET. This is the one to remove.\n                      With it gone a command is transmitted exactly once, always.\n  src/bridge.js:1426  `if (next.contactsAfterReceipt <= maxSends) return null;` - NOT a retry. This\n                      is how many wakes to WAIT after a confirmed receipt before declaring\n                      "delivered, never answered". It must SURVIVE, with its own name and value.\n                      Deleting it with the retry budget would settle live commands early.\n  src/bridge.js:1861  round-trip derives its timeout as `maxSends * beat + win + air`. That\n                      arithmetic breaks when maxSends goes.\n\nAlso touches: :730 and :753 (the gave-up state and its cause string), :1685 (published on /health), :1582 and :1675 (comments describing the budget).\n\nWHY OURS WAS NEVER WORTH KEEPING - measured, not argued. Every retry reused the packet id, and the ledger shows the radio discarding it before it ever entered the mesh:\n\n  sends:1   sent -> queued -> relayed -> no_ack\n  sends:2   sent ->                      no_ack\n  sends:3   sent ->                      no_ack\n\nNo queued, no relayed, and our witness radio never heard them on air. 100 percent of retransmissions were destroyed. The loop was pure cost.\n\nSTATE THIS PLAINLY WHEN REPORTING: removing our loop does NOT restore the hit rate on its own. It removes a mechanism that never worked. The hit rate depends on step 22 (the send gate) and on the implicit-ack step. Expect roughly the fraction of good-phase wakes until 22 is fixed.'}
2026-08-19 19:32:00,639 1876987 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bridge-backlog', 'title': "BUG our own witness radio cancels the sending radio's 3 retransmissions by generating an implicit ack", 'description': 'THE THIRD DEFECT, and it is the one that decides whether "let the radio do its job" actually works. Found 2026-08-19 from the Meshtastic firmware source, which is LOCAL at /usr/share/pac/dev/projects/mt-radar/firmware/src/src/mesh/ and is built for rak4631 - the same board our radios run.\n\nTHE FIRMWARE DOES RETRY A BROADCAST. ReliableRouter.cpp:19 -\n\n  if (p->want_ack) {\n      auto copy = packetPool.allocCopy(*p);\n      startRetransmission(copy, NUM_RELIABLE_RETX);\n  }\n  ...\n  return isBroadcast(p->to) ? FloodingRouter::send(p) : NextHopRouter::send(p);\n\nwant_ack ALONE starts retransmission. There is no broadcast exclusion - the broadcast test appears only on the routing line, after the decision. NUM_RELIABLE_RETX = 3 (NextHopRouter.h:93). We send to 0xFFFFFFFF with want_ack forced true, so the radio should retransmit our commands three times.\n\nBUT IT NEVER DOES. Measured across every witnessed transmission we hold:\n\n  45 onair records - nth distribution {1: 16, None: 29} - max transmissions for any packet id: 1\n  packets witnessed more than once: 0 of 45\n\nWHAT CANCELS IT. ReliableRouter::shouldFilterReceived - "We are seeing someone rebroadcast one of our broadcast attempts. If this is the first time we saw this, cancel any retransmissions we have queued up and generate an internal ack" -> stopRetransmission(key).\n\nOur YAGI (!fa39f7b4) sits beside OMNI (!2687afb1), witnesses it at -31 dBm, and the alarm device hears YAGI directly 163 times in the dongle log - so YAGI is transmitting on air. OMNI hears its own packet come back and stops.\n\nWHY THE IMPLICIT ACK IS THE WRONG SIGNAL HERE. It means "the mesh is carrying this packet". That is worthless when the destination is a duty-cycled unit that listens for 8s in every 300s. Flooding cannot reach BNCH; only a retransmission timed into its window can.\n\nMEASURED vs INFERRED, and keep the distinction:\n  MEASURED - the firmware logic, NUM_RELIABLE_RETX = 3, want_ack true, to = 0xFFFFFFFF, and\n             "never witnessed twice, 45 of 45".\n  INFERRED - that it is specifically YAGI\'s rebroadcast generating the implicit ack. Based on the\n             source plus YAGI being audibly on air. NOT directly observed.\n\nHOW TO CONFIRM: `WS /{node_id}/events`, mesh-gw\'s per-device stream, which we have never used. It separates the two radios\' transmissions and would show YAGI rebroadcasting an OMNI packet id.\n\nNOTE FOR WHOEVER PICKS THIS UP: mesh-gw has an open counter-hypothesis worth reading first - that on a broadcast, `relayed` is the strongest positive signal available and their `no_ack` is only a 30s timer, so `relayed -> no_ack` may not mean failure at all. Their argument is sound for the PROTOCOL. It does not change the OUTCOME here, because the device\'s own console shows rx=0 on every window since 16:01:59Z. Both are true.'}
2026-08-19 19:32:18,280 1876987 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bridge-backlog', 'title': 'BUG we keep only the terminal status and discard sent/queued/relayed, so a packet the radio never took looks identical to one that flew', 'description': 'FOUND BY mesh-gw, 2026-08-19, and it is a correction to something I asserted twice - to Peter and to them - that we could not distinguish a suppressed packet from a radiated-but-unanswered one. We can. We already have the signal and were reading past it.\n\nTHEIR WORDS: "queued is not ours to invent - it is emitted ONLY when the radio sends a queueStatus for that packet id with res == 0 (core/message_manager.py:140-164). It means THE RADIO ACCEPTED THE PACKET INTO ITS TX QUEUE. So sent with no queued is already the distinction you said would have saved you hours."\n\nTHE LADDER, and each rung means something different:\n\n  sent      the BLE write to the radio completed. Nothing more. Emitted immediately after the\n            write returns. It does NOT mean the radio took it.\n  queued    the radio returned queueStatus res == 0 - it accepted the packet into its TX queue.\n  relayed   it went out. On a BROADCAST this is the strongest positive signal that exists.\n  no_ack    mesh-gw\'s own 30-SECOND TIMER expiring. It is NOT the radio\'s verdict and the radio\n            may still be trying when it fires.\n  queue_failed  res != 0, with a numeric reason - the radio explicitly refusing.\n\nWHAT WE DO TODAY. We keep the terminal state and throw the intermediates away, so every failure reads as `no_ack` regardless of how far it actually got. That is why ten frames "witnessed on air" were believed radiated when the retransmissions among them were never accepted by the radio at all.\n\nWE ARE NOT GETTING queue_failed EITHER - so a discarded retransmission is silence, not a refusal with a reason.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THIS BUG. Peter asked repeatedly today for delivery statistics and the answer required ad-hoc SQL every time. The state ladder IS the statistic. Keep all of it per packet id and "did it reach the radio / the air / the unit" becomes a query rather than an investigation.\n\nRELATED, THEIRS NOT OURS, ALREADY LOGGED BY THEM:\n  - GET /{node}/outbox is keyed by packet_id and _store_sent_message OVERWRITES on every send, so\n    re-POSTing an id destroys the previous attempt\'s state history. Our retries were erasing our\n    own evidence. Presence in the outbox says nothing about the air - it is THEIR record of what\n    they sent, not the radio\'s record of what it holds.\n  - _pending_acks[pkt_id] overwrites without cancelling the previous 30s timeout task. Our ~5min\n    retry spacing means we never trigger it. Recorded in their ledger, not fixed.'}
2026-08-19 19:32:29,909 1876987 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bridge-backlog', 'title': 'BUG GET /commands is declared in /schema and 404s - only the websocket exists', 'description': 'Found 2026-08-19 while re-verifying the handover\'s RESOURCES section, not by a test - nothing covers it and npm test passes 370/370.\n\n  curl -s localhost:8790/commands  ->  {"error":"no route for GET /commands"}\n\n/schema declares it: operationId commandsFleet, "A FLEET VIEW. The same records GET /commands/{unit} returns, keyed by unit." Added today in cb921ef.\n\nWHERE IT IS. The websocket handler has it (src/api.js:493, `if (path === \'/commands\')`). The HTTP router does NOT:\n  src/api.js:244  handles `/commands/lifecycle`\n  src/api.js:248  handles `path.startsWith(\'/commands/\')`\n  src/api.js:259  `/state` HAS a bare-path handler\n  nothing matches bare `/commands`\n\nSEVERITY IS LOWER THAN IT FIRST LOOKS, and say so honestly: html asked for and uses the SOCKET, so nothing of theirs is broken today. I initially reported this to Peter as more serious than it is.\n\nWHY IT STILL MATTERS. It inverts the repo\'s own rule, which html quoted back at us from our own api.js: "ON THE SAME PATH AS THE GET, deliberately. ws://host/state and GET /state are one URL with two transports." Their original complaint was that /commands/{unit} had the GET and not the socket. We fixed that and created the mirror image on /commands - the socket and not the GET. A generated client from /schema breaks on it, which is exactly the class of defect they raised against us in queue-schema-declares-the-wrong-codes.\n\nTEST IT THE WAY surface.test.js ALREADY DOES for the queue codes: assert every path /schema declares answers on the transport it declares. That generalises the fix instead of patching one route.'}
2026-08-19 19:32:39,039 1876987 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bridge-backlog', 'title': 'DECIDE there is no git remote - every commit in this repo exists only on this disk', 'description': 'PETER\'S CALL. Nothing can be done without knowing where it should push.\n\n  git remote -v          -> empty\n  git log @{u}..HEAD     -> fatal: no upstream configured for branch \'main\'\n  git log --since=midnight --oneline | wc -l  -> 29\n\nThe handover of 2026-08-19 17:25Z claimed "20 commits today, all pushed (git log @{u}..HEAD -> 0)". Both halves are false and the command it cites cannot return 0 - it errors. That was found by running it during /handover read.\n\nSo 29 commits from 2026-08-19, including every fix in this investigation, exist in exactly one place with no off-machine copy.\n\nWHAT IS NEEDED FROM PETER: the remote to add, or a decision that this repo is deliberately local-only. The other three components of the superrepo have their own repos and may already answer this - bridge, plugins and device each have their own .git per the superrepo CLAUDE.md.\n\nDO NOT invent a remote or push anywhere speculatively.'}
2026-08-19 19:32:55,388 1876987 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bridge-backlog', 'title': 'TIDY the loose ends from the 2026-08-19 handover read - retention kind never written, device cursor behind, two peer items awaiting Peter', 'description': 'Small, verified, and easy to lose. Grouped rather than given a step each.\n\nRETENTION DECLARES A KIND NOTHING WRITES.\n  sqlite3 data/bridge.db "select * from retention"  ->  includes  received|keep||\n  sqlite3 data/bridge.db "select count(*) from record where kind=\'received\'"  ->  0\nZero rows in the whole history. Either the receipt path was meant to write kind=\'received\' and\nwrites kind=\'alarm\' instead, or the retention row is dead. rxid receipts currently land inside\nalarm frames at frame.json.rxid. Worth one look - it may be the tidiest place to persist the\ndelivery proof from step 1.\n\nDEVICE HAS NOT READ OUR ASK.\n  ls ~/.xsession/device--bridge/seen/  ->  bridge=4  device=6\n  latest msg 0007-ask-which-reply-type-does-each-verb-produce (from bridge)\nSo device\'s cursor is 6 and our ask is seq 7. It is UNREAD, not merely unanswered - a different\nsituation from a peer who read it and disagreed, and the verb sweep is blocked behind it.\n29 of 35 verbs map by name; ping->pong, sleepmode->sleep, and config/lname/name/sch have none\npublished.\n\nTWO PEER ITEMS WAITING ON PETER, NOT ON A REPO.\n  replies-not-published   DONE, needs his ok-to-prune\n  step 8 (closed)         the queue is now RECORDED as discarded on restart. Whether it should be\n                          RESTORED is his call - docs/spec/restart-discards-the-queue.md\n\nALREADY VERIFIED DURING THE HANDOVER READ, recorded so nobody re-checks:\n  npm test 370/0 - reanchor.py --check 0 stale - 24 ops on /schema - mesh-gw 44 paths -\n  store tables record/retention with column `at` not `ts` - dongle daemon pid 962658 alive 6d06h -\n  ports 8790/8791/8001/8800 - 5 peer channels - bridge IS subscribed to the :8800 flow board.\n  The previous session\'s scratchpad was rescued to this session\'s scratchpad/rescued-83fe6345/\n  (27 files incl. onetrip.py, path.py, hitrate.py, radios.py, rxid.py - worth rewriting into bin/).'}
