2026-08-19 20:08:32,109 1876987 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bridge-backlog', 'kind': 'note', 'text': '/ij Peter, 2026-08-19, VERBATIM:\n\n"if you are creating the same temp script repeatedly then it should be a reusable script"\n\nMY READING, kept separate from his words: a standing rule on how the work is done, not a one-off. The moment a throwaway query is written a SECOND time it has stopped being throwaway, and the cost of leaving it in a scratchpad is paid again by every session after this one.\n\nTHIS IS A REPEAT, AND THE FIRST ONE WAS DROPPED. The 2026-08-19 handover\'s ASKED section already recorded it: "onetrip.py, path.py, hitrate.py, radios.py, rxid.py are in this session\'s scratchpad and WILL BE DELETED. They are worth rewriting into bin/ - every one answered a question Peter asked twice." Nothing was done, the session ended, and I rescued those 27 files this morning to my own scratchpad - which is the same mistake one level along, because my scratchpad is just as temporary.\n\nWHAT I ACTUALLY REWROTE THIS SESSION, counted rather than recalled - eight python3 heredocs against data/bridge.db, and most are one tool with flags:\n  - sends per packet id for a unit\n  - attempt number vs whether the device ever named that id back in rxid\n  - heard-to-send latency per send\n  - the mesh-gw status ladder per packet (sent/queued/relayed/no_ack)\n  - onair witness records and the nth counter\n  - hit rate split by transmit phase\n  - alarm frame contents for a wake (x2)\nThat is ONE query - "for unit U, join every event to its packet id and show the ladder" - written seven different ways under time pressure, plus a frame dump.\n\nWHERE IT GOES. bin/ is the established home: bridged.js, round-trip.js, verb-sweep.js, wake-accuracy.js are already there and are already how this repo ships a diagnostic. A delivery-stats tool belongs beside them.\n\nTHE TENSION, stated so it is not resolved by accident: phase 1 of the reliability plan (lifecycle from the ledger, and keeping the whole status ladder) exists precisely to make these queries UNNECESSARY by serving them from the product. Both are right and they are not alternatives - the bin/ tool is what a human runs to audit, the route is what html and round-trip read. The rule from the round-trip /ij still binds: the tool must ASK the bridge, never compute its own answer. So the tool should read the route once phase 1 serves it, and read the ledger only for what no route exposes.\n\nAPPLIED: recorded as a step. Not started now - it would be an unscoped edit in the middle of a convicted entry\'s cycle, which is the thing /idiot exists to stop.'}
2026-08-19 20:08:48,009 1876987 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bridge-backlog', 'title': 'TOOL the same ledger query has been rewritten seven ways in scratchpads - it belongs in bin/', 'description': 'Peter, 2026-08-19: "if you are creating the same temp script repeatedly then it should be a reusable script"\n\nSECOND TIME OF ASKING. The 2026-08-19 handover\'s ASKED section already said onetrip.py, path.py, hitrate.py, radios.py, rxid.py were "worth rewriting into bin/ - every one answered a question Peter asked twice". Nothing was done and the session ended. I rescued those 27 files into my own scratchpad this morning, which is the same mistake one level along.\n\nRESCUED AND STILL TEMPORARY:\n  /tmp/claude-0/-usr-share-pac-dev-projects-mt-alarm-1-bridge/96eb321f-.../scratchpad/rescued-83fe6345/\n  onetrip.py  merged bridge ledger + device console on one clock\n  path.py     per-attempt gateway status; showed retries never got relayed\n  hitrate.py  answered/gave-up per day\n  radios.py   which radio heard what\n  rxid.py     device receipts vs our packet ids\n\nWHAT I REWROTE AGAIN TODAY - eight python3 heredocs, and seven are one query with different columns:\n  sends per packet id - attempt vs rxid - heard-to-send latency - the mesh-gw status ladder -\n  onair nth counter - hit rate by transmit phase - alarm frame dump (x2)\n\nTHE ONE TOOL UNDERNEATH ALL OF THEM: "for unit U over window W, join every event to its packet id and print the ladder queue -> send -> status(sent/queued/relayed/no_ack) -> onair -> reply/giveup, with the device receipt (rxid/ans) alongside." Flags for: which unit, time window, one packet id, summary vs per-packet, and a hit-rate rollup.\n\nCONSTRAINTS THAT ALREADY BIND IT\n  - The round-trip /ij applies: a diagnostic must ASK the bridge, never compute its own answer.\n    So once phase 1 serves the lifecycle from the ledger, this tool reads THAT ROUTE. It should\n    fall back to raw SQL only for what no route exposes,