2026-08-19 21:28:27,383 1876987 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bridge-backlog', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PHASE STATUS AS AT 2026-08-19 21:30Z, re-queried not recalled. Supersedes the status lines in note 2759; the phase DEFINITIONS there still stand.\n\nLEAD WITH IT: PHASES 1, 2 AND 3 HAVE NOT STARTED. The delivery failure is unfixed. Three commits since the plan was written and not one of them is phase 1, 2 or 3 work.\n\n  6cf08df 20:07  send record named the wrong radio      (gate blocker, unphased)\n  69c4782 21:07  GET /commands declared and unrouted    (phase 4)\n  2bcccd9 21:26  port 0 registered, not fixed           (phase 5, new)\n\nAND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL, NOT A CHOICE OF MINE - say this plainly rather than presenting\nthe three commits as progress. Phases 1, 2 and 3 ALL edit src/bridge.js. The /idiot hook\nrefuses any edit to a file named by an untried entry, and src/bridge.js is currently named by:\n\n  a-plugin-can-replace-the-resolved-channel-with-a-raw-index          untried\n  a-radio-redirect-keeps-the-channel-index-resolved-for-the-old-radio untried\n  the-lifecycle-ssot-is-memory-only-so-a-restart-empties-it           untried  (phase 1 itself)\n\nThe first two are blocked on mesh-gw shipping private/public resolution (step 31), because\nPeter replaced the fix design and building the old one would be waste. Convicting them to\nunlock the file would leave guilty-unproved entries, which gate 6 forbids at the next cycle.\nWithdrawing them would be false - they are live defects today.\n\nSO: PHASES 1-3 ARE BLOCKED ON mesh-gw. Not on effort, not on scope. When their change lands,\nstep 31 says what the bridge does and the two entries close with it.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\nPHASE 1  SEE IT              NOT STARTED - blocked as above\n  the-lifecycle-ssot-is-memory-only-so-a-restart-empties-it   untried, spec attached\n  step 25 status ladder                                       no accusation yet\n  Verified still true 21:07Z: GET /commands returns [] for both units against 340 rows on disk.\n  The 20:08 restart I did for the step 26 deploy emptied it again, which is the defect\n  demonstrating itself.\n\nPHASE 2  STOP DOING HARM     NOT STARTED - blocked as above\n  we-assign-packet-ids-and-retry-ourselves-instead-of-letting-the-radio   CLOSED\n    but it closed on work committed in 85bdc2f BEFORE this plan existed - maxSends 1 and no\n    id in the POST. The retry MECHANISM is still in the code at src/bridge.js:1442.\n  step 23 (rip the mechanism out) not begun.\n\nPHASE 3  FIX DELIVERY        NOT STARTED - blocked as above. THIS IS THE HIT RATE.\n  step 22 send gate    - the 0-of-7 vs 28-of-29 defect, untouched\n  step 24 implicit ack - needs WS /{node_id}/events confirmation first, untouched\n\nPHASE 4  CONTRACT AND TIDY   1 of 3 done\n  step 26 GET /commands   DONE  69c4782, deployed 20:08Z and verified live\n  step 28 tidy            open\n  step 27 git remote      open, PETER\'S CALL - still no remote, 32 commits local only\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\nPHASE 5  THE INSTRUMENTS LIE     NEW, and it is not "tidy"\n\nEvery entry here MADE ME DRAW A FALSE CONCLUSION TODAY. That is the membership test, and it\nis why this is its own phase rather than being folded into phase 4: a wrong measurement is\nworse than a missing one, because it is acted upon.\n\n  the-send-record-reports-the-default-radio-and-channel-not-the-ones-used   DONE 6cf08df\n      I read radio/channel out of ten send rows, saw them identical across the failure\n      boundary, and ruled out a radio switch. They were constructor defaults and would have\n      read identically either way. The check established nothing.\n\n  port-zero-switches-the-api-off-while-the-same-file-documents-it-as-any-free-port  UNTRIED\n      createApi({port:0}) returns a stub that does not listen and reports success, while\n      api.js:594 documents 0 as "any free port". Cost three runs to the harness ceiling.\n      Registered 2bcccd9 with spec. NOT FIXED.\n\n  dongle-log-has-nul-bytes-so-grep-treats-it-as-binary    RAISED WITH device, seq 8\n      16 NUL bytes in a 20MB log make grep print nothing with no error. I nearly recorded a\n      false absence about their FIRMWARE from a defect in the capture. Theirs to fix; do not\n      work around it with -a, that makes it permanent and invisible.\n\n  DONE WHEN: no instrument in this repo can produce a confident wrong answer without saying so.\n\n  THIS PHASE MUST NOT DISPLACE 1-3. It is what gets worked while they are blocked, and the\n  moment mesh-gw lands, phase 1 takes precedence over everything here.'}
2026-08-19 21:29:51,370 1876987 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bridge-backlog', 'title': 'BUG port 0 switches the API off while the same file documents it as any free port', 'description': 'PHASE 5 - THE INSTRUMENTS LIE. Membership test: it made me draw a false conclusion today.\n\nRegister entry: port-zero-switches-the-api-off-while-the-same-file-documents-it-as-any-free-port\nSpec: docs/spec/port-zero-means-off-and-also-means-any-free-port.md\nRegistered in 2bcccd9. Found while writing test/every-declared-path-answers.test.js and, before\nthat commit, recorded ONLY in a test comment and a line of chat - which /idiot says is not\nreporting at all.\n\napi.js:83   if (!port) return { close() {}, port: 0 };   // switched off\napi.js:594  // `port: 0` means "any free port"\n\nMEASURED, not inferred:\n  port=0          -> keys ["close","port"]           server undefined\n  port=null       -> keys ["close","port"]           server undefined\n  port=undefined  -> keys ["port","server","close"]  server object\n\nSo createApi({port:0}) returns a stub that does not listen, exposes no server, logs nothing and\nreports success. The worst shape a defect can take: the API appears to have started.\n\nEVIDENCE IT ALREADY BITES: test/api.test.js:310 has a freePort() helper that binds and releases a\nreal port purely because 0 cannot be used. That workaround exists BECAUSE of this. It cost three\nruns to the 2-minute harness ceiling today before the cause was found.\n\nTHE FIX: 0 must mean what the file already says it means. Switching off needs a value that cannot\nbe confused with a port number - `if (port === null || port === false)`. Then 0 falls through to\nserver.listen(0), which is Node\'s own "any free port", and get port() reports the bound one exactly\nas :594 describes.\n\nWHICHEVER WAY IT IS SETTLED, THE TWO COMMENTS MUST AGREE AFTERWARDS. The defect is not the choice\nof sentinel; it is that the file states both.\n\nNOT CHANGED: freePort() stays (binding a known port is legitimate, it just stops being compulsory),\nand the default port = 8790 is untouched.'}
