2026-08-19 22:13:13,932 1876987 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bridge-backlog', 'title': 'BUG PHASE 1 the command lifecycle is memory-only, so every restart empties it while 340 rows sit on disk', 'description': 'PHASE 1 OF THE RELIABILITY PLAN. Nothing in phases 2 or 3 can be PROVED in the field until this holds, because deploying either restarts the bridge and a restart empties the lifecycle.\n\nRegister entry: the-lifecycle-ssot-is-memory-only-so-a-restart-empties-it   UNTRIED\nSpec already attached: docs/spec/the-command-lifecycle-is-a-state-machine.md\n\nSTILL TRUE, re-verified after every restart today:\n  GET /commands            -> {"!987ab80f": [], "!8cee336b": []}\n  rows on disk             -> queue 48, send 84, status 59, onair 45, reply 90, giveup 8, cancel 6 = 340\n  retention                -> all of those are \'keep\', never purged\n\nTHE FIX, already scoped by the previous session and unchanged: rebuild commandsFor() from the ledger.\nstateOf() already DERIVES rather than stores, and every event is keyed by packet id, so it is the\nsame function over rows instead of over a Map. Check what the previous session\'s trial actually\nrecorded before re-deriving anything - the handover says it was registered and red-tested, but\n`idiot show` reports trial: null, so treat "red-tested" as unverified.\n\nWHY IT IS FIRST, in Peter\'s own words twice over:\n  "message lifecycle should be a global function... there is no SSOT message lifecycle and there\n   should be. basically a command / response is a STATE MACHINE."\n  "HTML just confirmed that it constructs it\'s own lifecycle from multiple sources of data. that is\n   wrong we should have a SSOT."\nA route that empties on restart forces html to keep its own copy, so the joins Peter told them to\ndelete cannot be deleted. The acceptance test in the task goal note still binds: if html still has\nto join sources after the fix, the fix did not happen.\n\nAND IT IS WHY EVERY MEASUREMENT TODAY NEEDED AD-HOC SQL. The 28-of-29 versus 0-of-7 hit rate, the\nstatus ladder, the witness counts - all came from raw queries against data/bridge.db because the\nroute that exists to answer them returns []. Step 30 (the reusable bin/ tool) is explicitly waiting\non this so it reads the route rather than hard-coding the joins.\n\nHOW I GOT HERE: six commits after writing the phase plan, none of them phase 1, 2 or 3. Peter:\n"have you lost the plot? - what task are you working on? you seem to be doing a lot of general\nhousekeeping." He was right - the active step was a COMPLETED phase 5 item. Recorded so the pattern\nis visible, not just the correction.'}
