2026-08-19 22:40:17,630 1933861 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-20, VERBATIM:\n\n"you need to know nothing about channels, channel indexes, PSKs, PKI, all you need\nto know is public and private."\n"so all code for resolve needs ripping out"\n\nThat is the whole specification. node-dash\'s vocabulary for a channel becomes two\nwords. Anything that computes, stores, compares or reasons about an index, a name,\na PSK or a key hash goes.\n\nTHE PEER SIDE, mesh-gw, live already:\n  [remove-your-channel-resolver] shipped 14fb823\n  [channel-now-required-on-send] shipped eb43840\n\n  POST /{id}/messages    {"text": "...", "channel": "private"}\n  POST /{id}/traceroute  {"to": N, "channel": "public"}\n  GET  /{id}/channels -> {"resolved": {"public": 0, "private": 2}, "channels":[...]}\n\nThey resolve by (role, psk), never by name, and refuse with a 400 on ambiguity or\non a renamed/re-keyed primary rather than guessing.\n\nWHAT I MEASURED BEFORE ACCEPTING ANY OF IT, because their central warning was\nthat a hardcoded index is already wrong on one of our radios:\n\n  mesh-gw resolved   !2687afb1 (OMNI)  {"public":0,"private":2}\n                     !fa39f7b4 (YAGI)  {"public":0,"private":1}\n\n  our own traffic, messages table, 14 days:\n                     OMNI  E9:B0:3F:17:27:91  ch 0 x155   ch 2 x264\n                     YAGI  F4:12:FA:39:F7:B6  ch 0 x37    ch 1 x679\n\nSo the divergence is real and our current code already handles it correctly —\nws-relay normalises per device and channels.js matches by name+psk identity,\nreturning null rather than 0. Their warning about a held index does NOT apply to\nus today.\n\nTHAT IS NOT A REASON TO KEEP IT. Peter\'s instruction is not "fix the resolver", it\nis that this repo should not know what a PSK is. Two correct implementations of the\nsame rule is how the 16-18 August outage happened, and the second one being right\nnow is no guarantee about the next firmware or the next radio. The gateway owns the\nradios; resolution belongs with them.\n\nA CORRECTION TO MY OWN EARLIER PROBE, so it is not repeated: I first compared our\nresolver against theirs by fetching /config and found channels.pub_name etc.\n"missing", and briefly concluded we resolved to null on both radios. Wrong —\n/config deliberately publishes only channels.*_present booleans because the PSK is\nsensitive. The values exist. The false-absence trap again, caught by checking\nrather than reporting.\n\nSCOPE — everything that references the resolver or an index:\n  backend   src/channels.js (239 lines, DELETE), and its four importers:\n            resolve.js, ws-relay.js, automation.js, callout.js\n            plus mesh-send.js\'s integer-channel guard and messages-api.js\n  browser   the send form\'s channel selection (tab-messages.html, app-messages.js)\n  tests     test_channels.mjs, test_send_channel.mjs, test_channel_write.mjs\n  config    channels.pub_name / pub_psk / pri_name / pri_psk retire\n\nFOUND WHILE ENUMERATING, needs checking during implementation, NOT assumed:\n  src/traceroute.js:507  bridge.post(`/${pathKey}/traceroute`, { to })\n  carries NO channel. mesh-gw now requires one on /messages and documents one on\n  /traceroute. Verify whether traceroute refuses without it — if so this path is\n  already broken, and it is in scope because it is a channel-carrying send.'}
