2026-08-19 22:48:04,983 1876987 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bridge-backlog', 'title': "DECIDE PHASE 3 is blocked by a conflict between two of Peter's own standing rules", 'description': 'STOPPED RATHER THAN BUILT. The fix I had specced for the send gate would have broken the devstate SSOT rule, and Peter said so directly while I was mid-design.\n\nWHAT I WAS ABOUT TO BUILD: gate LISTENING on the frame\'s phase against the predicted wake, so the bridge would NOT send when the receive window had already shut. That is a suppression of shout-on-heard.\n\nTHE RULE IT BREAKS, verbatim from Peter:\n  "shout on heard ###### MUST ALWAY HAPPEN #########"\n  "devstate is the SSOT. when it says device is awake (obs) we fire the message... no independent\n   determination of when the device is awake."\n  "no that is a property of devstate. NO EXCEPTIONS devstate is the SSOT."\n  And while I was working: "that is the SSOT DO NOT break that rule", then "do not duplicate what it\n  does it is the device SSOT".\n\nSo: no suppression gate, and no second implementation of wake/window anywhere. Callers ASK devstate.\n\nTHE CONFLICT THIS EXPOSES, which is why the phase is blocked rather than merely re-planned:\n\n  1. Shout on heard must ALWAYS happen. Locked, no exceptions.\n  2. Measured today: firing on a drain frame delivers 0 of 7; firing on an announce delivers 28 of 29.\n     About half of heards are drain frames, so about half of shouts are aimed at a shut window.\n  3. Since 91282f8 a command gets exactly ONE transmission. If that one lands on a drain frame the\n     command is dead.\n  4. The radio\'s own 3 retransmissions (NUM_RELIABLE_RETX, started on want_ack alone) never happen:\n     45 of 45 witnessed transmissions were witnessed exactly once.\n\nRules 1 and 3 together mean a command has one chance and no way to improve the odds of that chance,\nbecause improving them would mean not shouting.\n\nI CORRECTED A CLAIM I MADE TO PETER. I said phase 2 "does not move the hit rate" because retries\nwere destroyed 100% of the time. That was true of ID-REUSING retries. After 85bdc2f the POST carries\nno id and mesh-gw mints a fresh one, so retries would have ENTERED the mesh - they were never\ndestroyed again, and never observed either: zero sends between the 17:09Z deploy and phase 2.\nSo phase 2 may have removed a mechanism that had just become effective. Unproven in both directions.\n\nTHE ONLY RULE-COMPATIBLE PATH is the radio\'s own retransmission, because it costs no extra shout and\nno wake arithmetic. That needs the implicit-ack question settled first (step 24).\n\nMEASUREMENT ATTEMPTED AND INCONCLUSIVE, recorded so nobody repeats it: I checked the device console\nfor a packet id heard from two senders, which would show YAGI rebroadcasting OMNI. Result: 1363 RX\nlines, 1363 distinct ids, ZERO heard twice. That refutes nothing - the implicit ack needs OMNI to\nhear the rebroadcast, not the device, and the device is asleep for most of every beat. THE RIGHT\nOBSERVER IS `WS /{node_id}/events` on OMNI, which we have never used.\n\nNEEDS PETER, and it is a fork between two of his own rulings rather than a technical choice:\n  - accept ~50 percent while a shout can land on a shut window, or\n  - allow a command more than one transmission (he ripped that out today, but the reason it was\n    worthless - id reuse - no longer applies), or\n  - change the radio arrangement so the sending radio\'s own retransmissions survive (that is a live\n    radio config change, outside what I may do while he is away).'}
2026-08-19 23:02:44,738 1876987 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 24, 'text': 'HYPOTHESIS REFUTED 2026-08-19, passively, with no transmission. I attributed the cancelled retransmissions to OUR YAGI rebroadcasting OMNI. That is wrong.\n\nMEASURED over 7159 frames carrying heardBy:\n  every frame heard by exactly ONE radio (never both)\n  hops  0: 6881   1: 275   2: 3      -- relaying is real and common in this mesh\n  relayers, by the last byte of the node id, which is what Meshtastic carries:\n      4 x235,  40 x38,  176 x2,  2 x2,  177 x1\n\nOUR NODES: BNCH 0x0f=15, GARG 0x6b=107, OMNI 0xb1=177, YAGI 0xb4=180.\n\nYAGI (180) NEVER APPEARS AS A RELAYER. OMNI relayed exactly once. Relayers 4, 40, 176 and 2 are all\nFOREIGN nodes, and between them they relayed 278 frames.\n\nSO THE IMPLICIT ACK IS ALMOST CERTAINLY FOREIGN. ReliableRouter::shouldFilterReceived generates it\nwhen the sender hears ANY node rebroadcast its packet - it does not care whose node. Foreign nodes\ndemonstrably rebroadcast in this mesh, so they will rebroadcast our broadcast commands too, and OMNI\nwill stop retransmitting on the first one it hears.\n\nWHAT THAT KILLS: the remedy I proposed - "change the radio arrangement so the sending radio\'s own\nretransmissions survive". We cannot reconfigure a stranger\'s node. Do not spend time on separating\nOMNI and YAGI; it is not the cause.\n\nSTILL NOT PROVEN, and the honest gap: this shows foreign nodes relay frames FROM THE DEVICES. It does\nnot directly show one rebroadcasting one of OUR OUTBOUND commands, because those are only observable\nas `onair` witnesses and we hold no relay field for them. The clean instrument remains\n`WS /{node_id}/events` on OMNI, watched while a command goes out.\n\nEARLIER ATTEMPT, ALSO INCONCLUSIVE, recorded so nobody repeats it: I checked the device console for a\npacket id heard from two senders. 1363 RX lines, 1363 distinct ids, zero heard twice. Wrong observer -\nthe implicit ack needs OMNI to hear the rebroadcast, not the device, and the device is asleep for most\nof every beat.\n\nWHAT IT MEANS FOR PHASE 3: if the implicit ack is foreign and unavoidable, then want_ack buys us\nnothing on a broadcast in a mesh with active relays, and "let the radio do the retrying" cannot be the\nanswer here - the radio is being told to stop by strangers. That leaves addressing the command to the\nunit (unicast) rather than broadcasting it, which is a real design change and Peter\'s call.'}
2026-08-19 23:06:26,279 1876987 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 34, 'text': 'THE CONFLICT IS SHARPER THAN I FIRST WROTE, AND IT IS BETWEEN TWO OF PETER\'S OWN DECISIONS, NINE DAYS APART. Found while working the backlog on 2026-08-19 evening.\n\nfireOnHeard() in src/bridge.js carries this, from the day it was written:\n\n  "STORE AND FORWARD. The message stays queued until it is ANSWERED, so every time we hear the unit\n   we try again. It is not removed on send.\n   This was `q.shift()` - send once, drop it, never again. One mistimed send lost the command\n   permanently, which is what happened at 12:26 on 2026-08-10: the ping was queued mid-window, the\n   next frame heard was the unit\'s closing drain, the command reached the air 1.4 s after the unit\n   stopped listening, and there was no second attempt. A hand-timed curl \'worked\' and the butler did\n   not, from which I nearly concluded the DEVICE was at fault and started changing firmware.\n   Peter, 2026-08-10: \'it listens, sends messages if queued, and listens again\' - queued means queued\n   until answered. SEND-ONCE-AND-HOPE IS NOT STORE-AND-FORWARD."\n\nTHE FAILURE THAT COMMENT DESCRIBES IS THE ONE I MEASURED TODAY. "The next frame heard was the unit\'s\nclosing drain, the command reached the air after the unit stopped listening" is exactly the drain-frame\ncase: 0 delivered of 7, against 28 of 29 on the announce.\n\nAND WE HAVE REINSTATED IT. Since 85bdc2f made maxSends 1 (17:09Z today) and 91282f8 removed the budget\nentirely, a queued command is dropped after ONE transmission whether or not it was answered. That is\nq.shift() again, by a different route.\n\nWHY IT HAPPENED, and it was reasonable at each step: the 2026-08-19 instruction "rip out the retries,\nlet mesh-gw and the radios do their job" was given when retries REUSED the packet id and the mesh\ndiscarded 100 percent of them. They were worthless, so removing them cost nothing. But 85bdc2f also\nstopped supplying an id, and mesh-gw now mints a fresh one per POST - so from that commit onward a\nretry would have ENTERED the mesh. It was never observed: zero sends between the deploy and phase 2.\n\nSO THE TWO RULINGS ARE:\n  2026-08-10  "queued means queued until answered. Send-once-and-hope is not store-and-forward."\n  2026-08-19  "rip out the retries, let mesh-gw and the radios do their job."\nThey agreed while retries were duplicates. They do not agree now.\n\nI HAVE NOT DECIDED THIS ONE and it is the one thing tonight I am leaving for Peter, because both\noptions are defensible, the evidence does not settle it, and being wrong either way costs real\ndelivery:\n  - keep send-once: airtime is minimal, no non-idempotent verb can run twice, hit rate capped at\n    roughly the fraction of announce-phase contacts\n  - restore store-and-forward with a FRESH id per attempt: recovers the 2026-08-10 guarantee, and the\n    duplicate-execution hazard that made retries unsafe is real again precisely BECAUSE ids are fresh\n    (html raised it over `interval 60000` sent twice)\n\nThe second is only safe for idempotent verbs, which is a distinction the bridge does not currently\nmake. Registered as `send-once-and-hope-has-replaced-store-and-forward`.\n\nTO REVERSE PHASE 2 IF HE WANTS IT: git revert 91282f8, then set maxSends above 1. Nothing else\ndepends on it. The answerWithinContacts rename would need keeping - only the `sends > 0` line and the\n/health field are the retry half.'}
