2026-08-20 04:56:49,276 1822309 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'core/channels.py is the single source of truth for channel resolution, and outbound honours it — a caller says "private" and never sees an index. Inbound does not. Every inbound surface hands the consumer a bare integer and nothing that says what it means, so a consumer that needs to know whether a command arrived on the private channel has to fetch GET /{node}/channels and compare indices per radio. That is a second implementation of the rule living in a consumer, which is the exact class of duplication the resolver was built to remove, and it is worse inbound than outbound because index 1 is private on YAGI and index 2 is private on OMNI.\n\nRaised by node-dash as inbound-packets-carry-no-channel-role. Peter to node-dash, verbatim: "you need to know nothing about channels, channel indexes, PSKs, PKI, all you need to know is public and private."\n\nPut the resolved role on every surface that carries a channel: "channel_role": "public" | "private" | null, from the same classify() that produces `resolved`. null means the index matched neither role unambiguously on that radio, and it fails closed for the consumer by construction.'}
2026-08-20 04:56:57,074 1822309 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'core/channels.py — add `role_of(channels, index) -> str | None`. One rule, reusing classify(). Returns "public", "private", or None.\n\ncore/app_router.py — add `channel_role` to `_typed()`, so every typed packet event on WS/SSE carries it. Channels come from `self._dm.get_by_ble(addr).channels`.\n\ncore/ble_device.py — add `channel_role` in `_store_message()` (inbound, served by GET /{node}/messages) and in `_store_sent_message()` (outbox, served by GET /{node}/outbox). The outbox one matters because callers now POST channel:"private" and get an integer back.\n\ndocs/API_SSE.md, docs/API_REST.md — document the field on the typed packet events, the messages store and the outbox.\n\ntests/test_channel_role.py — role_of against the two real divergent channel sets (OMNI public 0/private 2, YAGI public 0/private 1); null for an index matching neither; null when a set has two candidate privates (ambiguous); and the event-shape assertion that _typed carries the field.\n\nNOT changing: the `channel` integer itself. It stays on every surface. This adds a field, it does not remove one — removing it would break every existing consumer, and mesh-gw is the BLE layer, not the arbiter of what a consumer may look at.'}
