2026-08-20 06:23:21,015 1965319 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'park-bridge-roster-refuses-the-third-unit', 'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-20, VERBATIM:\n\n  "Route: bridge refused it (does not carry !18a01fc4 — it carries !987ab80f, !8cee336b), so the\n   site MARK went via mesh-gw as @1fc4 site MARK on channel 2. Raised that as\n   bridge-roster-refuses-the-third-unit (#11)\n   — it\'s their roster, and I told them plainly that I put one frame around the bridge to finish\n   your instruction rather than let them discover it. seems like you have hard-coded the device ID\'s\n   I really hope you have not, as there will be more."\n\nPARKED, NOT INVESTIGATED. Captured while the mesh-gw-status work was in flight.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY KNOWN WITHOUT LOOKING, from pm2\'s args read earlier in the same session:\n\n  --unit !987ab80f,!8cee336b\n\nso the roster is a START-UP ARGUMENT, not a literal in src/. That is NOT the same as "nothing is\nhard-coded" and must not be reported as such until someone greps for unit ids across src/, bin/ and\nthe plugins. bin/bridged.js is known to carry `if (!units.length) units.push(\'!987ab80f\')` as a\ndefault, which IS a literal device id in source.\n\nTHE ACTUAL QUESTION TO ANSWER, and it is bigger than the grep: what does adding a unit require\ntoday? If it means editing a pm2 argument and restarting the only route to the units, that is a\ndesign problem regardless of whether any id appears in source — "there will be more" is the\nrequirement.\n\nSOMEBODY WENT AROUND THE BRIDGE. A frame reached the mesh as `@1fc4 site MARK` via mesh-gw directly,\non channel 2. Peter says he told them plainly. Two consequences worth holding: that frame is NOT in\nthe bridge\'s ledger, so any count of what was sent today is short by one; and the bridge is\ndocumented as the only route to the units, which is now not true of that frame.'}
