2026-08-20 07:02:21,852 1965319 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': '/ij Peter, 2026-08-20, VERBATIM — quoting my OWN words back at me with an instruction on top:\n\n  "DO NOT GLOSS OVER THIS:   The one place it is not per-device is GET /stats, whose queued/pending\n   are process-wide counters — and that is precisely what made round-trip refuse a run against BNCH\n   because GARG had a command outstanding. Your instinct was right about the symptom; the fault is\n   the tool reading a global number, not the bridge holding anything back. Already registered as\n   round-trips-dirty-queue-guard-reads-global-counters-not-the-units."\n\nMY READING, kept separate: "already registered" was me treating the register as the disposal.\nRegistering is not fixing. He wants it FIXED, now, in this session, and he is right that a defect\nwhich blocks the very testing this phase depends on is not a backlog item.\n\nWHY IT IS URGENT RATHER THAN TIDY: it blocks the acceptance run. Every round-trip against BNCH is\nrefused whenever GARG has anything outstanding, and GARG is a DEPLOYED unit on a 300 s beat, so its\ncommands are outstanding for minutes at a time. The hit-rate measurement the whole phase turns on\ncannot be taken reliably while this stands.\n\nTWO ACCUSATIONS NAME bin/round-trip.js AND BOTH ARE UNTRIED, so both lock the file and both are\nbeing settled in one cycle:\n  round-trips-dirty-queue-guard-reads-global-counters-not-the-units\n  round-trip-calls-a-delivered-frame-never-entered-the-mesh  (plus: reports state "discarded"\n    for a command the bridge holds as "queued")\n\nThey are the same class - the instrument asserting something the bridge does not say - so one spec\ncovers them.'}
2026-08-20 07:02:37,473 1965319 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'BUG round-trip refuses a run on one unit because another is busy, and reports states the bridge never said', 'description': '/ij Peter, 2026-08-20: "DO NOT GLOSS OVER THIS ... Already registered as round-trips-dirty-queue-guard-reads-global-counters-not-the-units."\n\nRegistering is not fixing. This blocks the acceptance run: GARG is deployed on a 300 s beat, so it has something outstanding for minutes at a time, and every BNCH round-trip is refused while it does.\n\nTWO ACCUSATIONS, ONE FILE, ONE CYCLE:\n  round-trips-dirty-queue-guard-reads-global-counters-not-the-units\n    bin/round-trip.js reads GET /stats, whose queued/pending are process-wide. Measured\n    2026-08-20 05:47: BNCH\'s own /state said queued 0 inFlight 0 and the run was refused\n    anyway because !8cee336b had a command in flight.\n  round-trip-calls-a-delivered-frame-never-entered-the-mesh\n    prints "NEVER ENTERED THE MESH - no relayed, so this frame went nowhere" for a frame the\n    device received, executed and answered in the same run; and reports state "discarded"\n    for a command the bridge holds as "queued".\n\nSame class: the instrument asserting something the bridge does not say. mesh-gw has since\nconfirmed relayed is not a delivery signal at all - `relayed from=<our own radio>` is the\nsending radio self-minting an implicit ack - so the line is wrong twice over.'}
