2026-08-20 09:38:13,151 1982957 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'name': 'park-telemetry-onchange-with-obs-byte-flags', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-20, verbatim:\n\n"probably the only action that will be taken now is to simply increase the beat times to 15 mins which is done. more long term we will need to look at onchange again, and adding the byte flags to the obs makes it more viable. create a task and /park it. also not my concern about it\'s bugginess"\n\nAlso his: "yes you can use a byte value, that\'s better as long as both sides know about it."\n\nAnd the framing that produced it: "live deployed units are NOT the problem, the dev units with < 15 min telem is a problem. obs cannot be avoided. so we need a gate on telemetry."\n\nCONTEXT MEASURED THIS SESSION, so the next session does not re-derive it:\n\nPer beat, three frames, measured from the dongle console (txgo -> txdone):\n  obs     63 B  730 ms   sent BEFORE the RX window opens; this is the announce\n  DEVICE  38 B  526 ms   sent AFTER the RX window closes\n  ENV     42 B  568 ms   sent AFTER the RX window closes\n  total        1.82 s  -> at a 30 s beat = 6.1% duty; measured air_util_tx was 6.31%\n                          at a 300 s beat = 0.61%; measured GARG air_util_tx was 0.60%\nBoth anchors validate the model.\n\nSo the two telemetry frames are 1.09 s of 1.82 s -- 60% of per-beat airtime -- and they are\ntransmitted after the receive window has shut, so they contribute NOTHING to responsiveness.\nThat is the airtime the gate is for. obs is untouched by design (Peter: "obs cannot be avoided").\n\nTHE GATE ALREADY EXISTS AND IS OFF:\n  teleDue()            main.cpp:490    gates exactly the two telemetry frames, on change OR keepalive\n  g_teleOnChange       main.cpp:428    default false  -> teleDue() returns true unconditionally\n  g_teleKeepaliveMs    main.cpp:472    default 45 min; !987ab80f was carrying 6 h\n  deadbands            main.cpp:482-485  g_dbTempC10, g_dbRhPct, g_dbVbatV100, g_dbConsMah\n\nDO NOT CONFUSE THE TWO FLAGS. They have similar names and different scope:\n  tele.chg / g_teleOnChange   gates the two TELEMETRY frames only.   NO TEXT VERB EXISTS.\n  beat.chg / g_beatOnChange   gates the WHOLE BEAT including obs.    Verb: `onchange 0|1`, MtCmd.cpp:910.\n                              Source says it is "GATED OFF, EVERYWHERE" and deliberately left in.\nPeter recalls on-change being "VERY BUGGY" and has explicitly said the bugginess is NOT his\nconcern -- do not treat that recollection as a blocker, but note NEITHER flag has a test: none of\nthe five C++ host tests touches either.\n\nWHY IT CANNOT BE SWITCHED ON TODAY. tele.chg is reachable only via {"type":"set","path":...} on\nPRIVATE_APP (main.cpp:2531, applySet at :2535). MtCmd.cpp:895 and :912 both say that path is\n"unreachable over a text-only gateway". THREE verbs -- agc, chunk cfg, onchange -- exist solely as\nworkarounds for that, and MtCmd.cpp:912 records the trap in its own words: "A config field with no\nverb cannot be reached over the mesh at all, which is what stalled this feature: the firmware\nshipped, the flag defaulted OFF, and there was no way to turn it on."\n\nPETER\'S CORRECTION, and it is the important one for whoever picks this up: that limitation is a\nDESIGN CHOICE, not a fact. "we own the firmware, we own the bridge, we own the mesh-gw" and\n"mesh-gw does not filter anything. bridge will do what I tell it, but that is not the answer."\nSo do NOT add a fourth workaround verb. The right fix is the transport path, and applySet already\nimplements every config field on the device side -- one route replaces N verbs.\n\nTHE DESIGN AGREED IN DISCUSSION:\n  - A BYTE per telemetry frame in obs, not a bool, so silence is declared rather than inferred.\n    Suggested: tdev 0=sent 1=suppressed; tenv 0=sent 1=suppressed 2=read failed. The third value\n    matters because "env unchanged" and "env sensor dead" are both silence today and only one is a\n    fault (the err path is MtCmd.cpp:365, {"type":"err","msg":"env read failed"}).\n  - Bridge can then record and plot the values as constant, which is the whole point: a receiver\n    must be able to tell "unchanged" from "broken".\n  - A RATE DIVISOR may be safer than change-detection -- no deadband logic, no per-field state,\n    and suppression becomes deterministic so a consumer can interpolate with certainty. This was\n    my suggestion, not Peter\'s instruction; he asked for on-change. Decide it when the task runs.\n\nFIELD BUDGET, measured by bridge: obs is at 18 of 24 fields. The presence bitmap is ceil(n/8) and\nis NOT on the wire, so adding 2 fields keeps it at 3 bytes and costs nothing. Crossing 24 would\ngrow it.\n\nLANDING CONSTRAINT, NOT OPTIONAL: any edit to schema/frames.json moves the wire schema hash. That\nis what caused the eight-hour blind-decode outage on 2026-08-19 (0xd339 -> 0x6911). Bridge has\nalready accepted a canonical wire projection that would stop comments and labels moving the hash,\nand THAT IS STILL UNWRITTEN. This change should land with the projection fix and with bridge in\nthe same breath, never before it.\n\nWHAT WAS ACTUALLY DONE INSTEAD, today: beat times increased to 15 minutes. Peter: "which is done."'}
