2026-08-20 10:03:08,421 1982957 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'name': 'park-telemetry-onchange-with-obs-byte-flags', 'text': 'PRIORITY RAISED by Peter, 2026-08-20: "I think that the telem flags should be added as a priority for future use so that we can get both sides aware of it" and "byte flags".\n\nSPLIT INTO TWO PIECES OF WORK. The schema half is now PRIORITY; the behaviour half stays parked.\n\nPIECE 1 — RESERVE THE FIELDS (priority, blocked on bridge agreeing)\n  Add to obs in schema/frames.json:\n    tdev  0 = DEVICE telemetry sent this beat\n          1 = suppressed, value unchanged\n    tenv  0 = ENV telemetry sent this beat\n          1 = suppressed, value unchanged\n          2 = suppressed, sensor read FAILED\n  BYTE values, not bools — Peter was explicit twice. Unknown values are RESERVED and a consumer\n  must read them as "suppressed, reason unknown", never as an error, so the set can be extended\n  without a second flag day.\n\n  Costs nothing on the wire: obs is at 18 of 24 fields, presence bitmap is ceil(n/8) and is not on\n  the wire, so 20 fields keeps it at 3 bytes.\n\n  RAISED WITH BRIDGE as device--bridge #24 [obs-gains-two-telemetry-state-bytes], 2026-08-20.\n  OPEN — do not touch schema/frames.json until they answer. That file is Peter\'s, at the PRODUCT\n  ROOT (/usr/share/pac/dev/projects/mt-alarm-1/schema/frames.json), and editing it moves the fleet.\n\n  MUST RIDE WITH THE PROJECTION CHANGE. Adding fields moves the wire schema hash legitimately. The\n  canonical projection bridge accepted in their #6 stops COMMENTS and LABELS moving it but will not\n  stop this. So:\n     1. gen_frames.py -> canonical projection   (agreed, still unwritten, ours)\n     2. obs gains tdev + tenv                   (this)\n     3. gating behaviour                        (later, NO schema change needed)\n  1 and 2 in one coordinated restart. The last uncoordinated hash move cost eight hours of blind\n  decoding on the live fleet on 2026-08-19.\n\nPIECE 2 — THE GATING ITSELF (stays parked)\n  Still undecided and explicitly NOT blocking piece 1: whether suppression is driven by\n  change-detection (g_teleOnChange, main.cpp:428, exists and is off) or by a plain rate divisor.\n  Peter asked for on-change. I argued a divisor is safer — no deadband logic, no per-field state,\n  and suppression becomes deterministic so a consumer can interpolate with certainty rather than\n  inference. The flags mean the same thing to a receiver either way, which is exactly why the\n  schema half can land first.\n\n  Also unresolved and NOT to be solved with a fourth workaround verb: tele.chg has no text verb and\n  is reachable only via {"type":"set"} on PRIVATE_APP. Peter: "we own the firmware, we own the\n  bridge, we own the mesh-gw" / "mesh-gw does not filter anything." Fix the transport path.\n\nNOT URGENT. Peter moved the beats to 15 minutes and that is done; mesh-gw measured our two dev\nunits falling from 74.7% of over-the-air traffic to 19.4%, total occupancy ~14% -> 3.7%. This work\nis about making the later change cheap, not about fixing today.'}
2026-08-20 10:07:25,658 1822309 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Publish channel occupancy as a live endpoint, GET /{node_id}/airtime, attributed to the nodes causing it by time-on-air.\n\ncli/airtime.py answers the question but only by listening for ten minutes, which makes it something a person runs occasionally rather than something a consumer can read. Nobody checks occupancy before drawing conclusions from delivery rates — a peer measured a 13-of-18 reception rate on a channel their own test setting had loaded to ~14%, and blamed the verb, then the air, then the receive window, before the load was noticed.\n\nThe gateway already decodes every packet, so accumulating them costs nothing and turns a ten-minute investigation into a request.'}
