2026-08-20 10:40:50,953 2028104 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PETER\'S RULING 2026-08-20 — frame decode tolerance. Verbatim, in order given:\n\n  1. "bit register i confirmed."\n  2. "most importantly schema should not be rejected based on hash. appended elements and\n      changes to the HR lables does not affect the integrity of the data sent"\n  3. "it simply means that extra elements are not decoded."\n\nWHAT EACH ONE MEANS AGAINST THE CODE, checked not recalled:\n\n(1) AGREED to device as bridge--device #26. obs gains ONE field `flags`, u32 used as an 8-bit\n    register, bits 0-2 = device-telem suppressed / env-telem suppressed / env read FAILED,\n    bits 3-7 reserved and ignored on receive. Supersedes their tdev+tenv proposal in #24.\n    obs goes 18 -> 19 of 24, bitmap stays 3 B, headroom 5. No value shifts.\n\n(2a) HASH REJECTION IS ALREADY GONE and needs no work. Removed 2026-08-19 by 23b81de\n     ("the-bridge-reads-a-unit-it-was-not-built-with"). src/frames.js:128 computes `foreign`\n     and uses it ONLY to annotate — the error text at :164 and the out.foreign key at :171.\n     Proved by test/frames-foreign-schema.test.js:66 "a FOREIGN hash decodes when the layout\n     agrees, and says it was foreign". Do not re-fix this.\n\n(2b) LABELS STILL MOVE THE HASH, and the fix for that was ATTEMPTED AND REVERTED on our side:\n     047070d reverts d6e6e74 "schema-hash-covers-only-the-wire", with NO reason in the message.\n     The ground is uneven here — find out why before retrying it. The agreed real fix is\n     DEVICE\'s: gen_frames.py switches to a canonical projection so comments and labels stop\n     moving the hash (their #24, our #6, still unwritten). Since (2a) landed, a moved hash no\n     longer blinds us — it is now cosmetic churn on /health schemaHash, not an outage.\n\n(3) THE DESIGN QUESTION IS SETTLED: tolerate the tail, do not decode it. This is the accusation\n    `appended-fields-reject-the-whole-frame-instead-of-the-tail`. The throw is src/frames.js:159\n    `if (at !== buf.length) throw`.\n\n    THE TRAP, and it is why this is not a one-line delete: trailing bytes are ALSO what a wrong\n    BITMAP WIDTH looks like, and that check is what caught the 08-18 drift.\n    test/frames-foreign-schema.test.js:77 asserts exactly that. The bitmap width is ceil(n/8)\n    from the LOADED schema and IS NOT ON THE WIRE (src/frames.js:118-120). Delete the check\n    naively and a width change is silently decoded as garbage instead of refused.\n\n    THE DISTINGUISHER, to be tried not assumed: a bitmap has spare bits. obs is 18 fields in\n    24 slots, so bits 18-23 exist and are ours to read. A sender with APPENDED fields at the\n    SAME width sets a bit BEYOND our field count -- countable, and it tells us exactly how many\n    extra varints to skip. Consume those, discard them, and THEN still require exact\n    consumption. That keeps the misalignment detection while honouring Peter\'s rule.\n    A width change has no spare bit to set, so it still fails consumption. Verify this holds.\n\n    ZERO-HEADROOM FRAMES ARE THE UNSAFE CASE: `_` is 16 fields in 16 slots, spare 0. Appending\n    there moves the width and no decoder-side change makes it safe. obs 6 spare, config 2 spare.\n\nSTATUS OF THE MARKS: steps 35 and 36 read [ ] here but are VERIFIED DONE in the repo (3f6dc28,\n86edab5; onair absent from the live /schema, confirmed on the running service). The marks lag\nthe repo. Do not redo them.'}
