2026-08-20 10:58:03,910 2028323 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Answer html\'s `verb-argument-table` (device--mt-alarm-html #14, asked 2026-08-19 08:33, extended by #15) with a DERIVED table, not a hand-written one, and make it durable so it cannot drift.\n\nWHAT THEY ASKED FOR, in their own priority order (#15):\n  kind      writes a setting | queries state | acts   <- the field that unlocks the UI grouping\n  setting   the config id the verb writes, when kind is a write — gives them ty/def/min/max/\n            writable from x-deviceConfig for free\n  arg       required | optional | forbidden\n\nWHY THEY HAVE BUILT NOTHING FOR TWO DAYS: they tried to join verbs to settings by LABEL and got\n10 of 35, with `status` landing in the writes-a-setting group because its reply echoes the\nheartbeat. Their words: "A dropdown that files `status` under settings is worse than an\nungrouped list, because it looks considered." They are explicitly waiting rather than guessing.\n\nTHEIR STANDING OFFER, which must be answered honestly rather than dodged: if this can only be a\nhand-written table, and a hand-written table drifts, say so and they keep one free-text box.\nSo a hand-written table is NOT an acceptable deliverable unless a check keeps it honest — the\n`check_labels.py` pattern (235 pairs, fails the build when the next one is missing) is the bar.\n\nWHAT ALREADY EXISTS, measured by them against /schema:\n  x-deviceConfig   43 settings with ty, def, min, max, writable\n  x-deviceVerbs    35 names and NOTHING else\n  x-deviceReplies  41 types, 204 fields — what comes back, not what goes out\n  x-unknown        says verb arguments are parsed ad hoc inside each MtCmd.cpp branch\n\nTWO THINGS THEY REPORTED THAT ARE OURS:\n1. `x-unknown` now contains a false sentence — "verb reply shapes: not extracted" stopped being\n   true when x-deviceReplies shipped. Needs splitting, not deleting: the ARGUMENTS clause is\n   still true. Bridge\'s file — raise, do not edit.\n2. The `help` reply already declares a field `use` labelled "Command grammar". If the firmware\n   states its own grammar at runtime that may be a cheaper source than parsing the dispatcher.\n   Worth checking in Phase 1 before building a parser.\n\nNOT BLOCKING THEM TODAY: the box takes free text, the device rejects what it does not like, and\ndevice error text ("interval range 10000-86400000 ms") already lands on the page verbatim. This\nis about catching it before it goes on the air.'}
