2026-08-20 11:41:28,706 2028323 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'DERIVE the table from the dispatcher; never hand-write it. Spec:\ndocs/specs/command-and-telemetry/verb-argument-table.md\n\nHOW, and every rule reads code rather than a name or a label:\n1. `setting_map()` reads applySet()/applySetStr() in main.cpp — the id -> C variable map that\n   already exists and nobody had used as a join.\n2. `verb_branches()` splits the dispatcher on the else-if chain at its own indent. NOT on the\n   strncasecmp hits: `lname`/`name` share one condition and re-test "name" inside the body, and\n   a hit-to-hit slice gave both of them a few characters instead of the branch.\n3. `verb_detail()` per branch: assignment to a mapped variable + persistence -> kind=set with\n   the setting id; an inner strncasecmp(a|sub, ...) chain -> kind=sub with the sub-words; any\n   look at `cmd + N` -> the verb takes an argument, and whether the bare form replies or errors\n   decides optional vs required.\n4. `write_api()` emits a NEW `verbargs:` key. The existing `verbs:` list is untouched, because\n   the butler parses it with /^verbs:\\n((?:\\s+-\\s+.*\\n)+)/ (bridge/src/surface.js:39) and\n   nesting it would feed another repo garbage.\n5. `rak/tools/check_verb_args.py`, wired at platformio.ini:148 AFTER gen_schema.py, fails the\n   build when a verb is undeclared, stale or malformed.\n\nWHAT CHANGED FROM THE ORIGINAL DESIGN, recorded because it is the answer to html\'s question:\n`kind` has THREE values, not the four they asked for. query vs act is NOT derivable — the 35\nbranches call 94 distinct functions and separating reading from mutating needs a hand list of\nwhich ones mutate, which is exactly the drift html refused. `plain` says only what is provable.\nSo `reboot` and `wedge` read the same as `ping`, and confirm-before-send stays html\'s policy.\n\nAND ONE CORRECTION TO THEIR PREMISE: they wrote "status takes no argument at all". It takes\n`status [domain]` — bare is the general packet, `status mem` and `status alarm` are details\n(MtCmd.cpp:265-271, :296). Their conclusion held; the premise did not.\n\nMEASURED: red 35 undeclared of 35 exit 1; green 35 declared (11 set, 7 sub, 17 plain) exit 0.\nFalsified by removing one declaration -> "UNDECLARED interval", exit 1, control green.'}
