2026-08-20 13:45:34,807 2053659 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': "A command Peter types on the private mesh channel reaches the bridge queue, and he is told on the same channel what became of it.\n\nToday there is no route. bridge/src/bridge.js:1355 drops every packet from a node outside the bridge's own unit list before it is published, so no plugin can learn of a command through ctx.subscribe. The handheld is silent in both directions.\n\nDone means: a `@xxxx ...` text from a configured commander node, heard on the private channel, is queued against the unit whose id ends in those four hex digits; and a threaded reply comes back saying it was queued, refused, answered or given up. Anything not of that form is ignored."}
2026-08-20 13:45:43,055 2053659 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'A new plugin `relay` in this repo, loaded by the bridge from plugins/src alongside dash, notify and probe (confirmed live in GET :8790/stats).\n\nIN  mesh-gw WS ws://<gw>/events, typed `text` event. Gate on from_num in config.commanders AND channel_role === \'private\' — the role is stamped by mesh-gw (core/ble_device.py:676, core/app_router.py:144) and role_of returns None on any ambiguity, so the gate fails closed. The plugin holds no channel integer.\n    Admit only /^@([0-9a-f]{4})\\s+\\S/. Resolve the token against ctx.units(); unknown token is refused, not guessed.\n\nMID ctx.queue(unit, text) — text passed verbatim, because classify.js address() sends anything starting with \'@\' unchanged. This puts `relay` on the MAY_QUEUE allow-list in test/safety.test.js, with its reason, and that is a deliberate line in the diff.\n\nOUT POST http://<gw>/<gwNode>/messages {text, channel:"private", reply_id}. No `to` — supplying it switches to PKC and TA2m cannot decode. Threaded to the id of the message being answered.\n\nConfig from plugins.d/relay.json via ctx.config: gateway base URL, gateway node id, commanders[]. No address in source — ctx.config exists for exactly this (plugins.js:341).\n\nTwo frames per command, not five: SF11 is ~1.6 s of airtime per frame on a shared channel, so only the immediate ack and the terminal outcome are sent. `send` and `status` are intermediate and stay off the air.'}
