2026-08-20 14:33:09,958 2028104 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'name': 'park-requeue-on-fail-with-beat-and-tries-knobs', 'text': 'PARKED 2026-08-20. Peter\'s words, verbatim, both statements:\n\n  "reque on fail, knobs: beat tries. you probably know the rest."\n\n  "you cannot requeu with the same id, do not attempt it"\n\nTHE SECOND IS A HARD CONSTRAINT ON THE FIRST, not a detail. A re-queue must mint a NEW\npacket id. Reusing the old one is forbidden and is not to be attempted.\n\nNOT INVESTIGATED — parked only. Whoever picks this up starts by reading, not building.\n\nTwo things that are already known and must be reconciled before any design, because they\nlook like they contradict this and may not:\n\n  - memory `we-send-to-the-radio-once-full-stop`: "resending is FORBIDDEN and the question\n    is not to be reopened; retry is the radio\'s job". That rule is about re-handing the SAME\n    frame to the radio. A re-queue on a LATER BEAT with a NEW id is a different act, and\n    Peter is the one raising it — but the distinction must be stated explicitly and agreed,\n    not assumed.\n  - the closed accusation `we-assign-packet-ids-and-retry-ourselves-instead-of-letting-the-radio`\n    and `one-command-is-transmitted-more-than-once`. Both were fixed by REMOVING retry. This\n    is not a request to restore what was removed.\n\nWhy a new id is required, from what device has already told us: the firmware dedupes on\n(from, id) via PacketHistory, and FloodingRouter::shouldFilterReceived returns TRUE for an id\nalready seen, releasing the packet without ever reaching handleReceived(). So a re-POST with\nthe same id would be silently filtered by the receiver — which is exactly why Peter says do\nnot attempt it.\n\nThe knobs named: `beat` and `tries`. Both to be DERIVED, not plucked — see /derive.'}
2026-08-20 14:33:13,423 2028104 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_switch args={'name': 'bridge-backlog'}
2026-08-20 14:37:19,669 2028104 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PETER\'S RULING 2026-08-20 — the device\'s own receipts are OPERATIONAL, not diagnostic.\n\nVerbatim:\n\n  "devstate carries that data already, you just keep ignoring it"\n\n  "you only ever look at that when I tell you, this should not be the case it should be\n   integrated so that we use it and dnot just use if for debugging. it tells us valuable\n   informatiuon and you keep just discarding it"\n\nHE IS RIGHT AND THE RECORD PROVES IT. Three times on 2026-08-20 I used these fields as\nforensics and then put them down:\n\n  - morning: counted 61 of 88 commands confirmed received by rxid, registered it, moved on\n  - 13:0x:   read BNCH\'s dongle log and hand-converted 0xeb9b2b93 to prove a reply radiated\n  - 13:2x:   found obs.txid naming a reply id we never received, and called it a "capability\n             we are not using" - while devstate had been storing and exposing it all along\n\nAnd the same mistake is recorded IN THE FILE, at src/devstate.js:560-563, about rxid:\n"The bridge held proof of delivery for every one, retransmitted anyway, and reported that\nthe unit never answered." That was fixed for rxid on 2026-08-19. txid is the same failure\nagain, in the same function, and I re-derived it from a dongle instead of reading it.\n\nWHAT "INTEGRATED" MEANS, and this is the requirement, not a summary of it:\n\nThe three receipts are the DEVICE\'S OWN ACCOUNT of what happened to a command, and they are\nthe only account that does not depend on our radios hearing anything:\n\n    rxid   the last packet it RECEIVED      -> the command arrived\n    ans    the last packet it ANSWERED      -> the command ran\n    txid   the last packet it TRANSMITTED   -> the reply exists and was put on the air\n\nThey must drive the command\'s state automatically, every beat, for every unit - not be\navailable for someone to query after a failure. A command whose txid names an id we never\nreceived is not "no reply, cause unknown"; it is "the unit answered and we did not hear it",\nand the bridge should say so without being asked.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY THERE (checked, not assumed):\n  src/devstate.js:572   stores s.txid\n  src/devstate.js:584   receipts() exposes it as `transmitted`\n  src/devstate.js:604   hasReceived / hasAnswered exist; no comparator for txid\n  devstate.receipts()   HAS NO CALLER in src/bridge.js or src/api.js - dead accessor\n  /state comms.lastTx   already published, already ignored by everyone\n\nSo the data is carried in two places, served on a public route, and compared nowhere.\n\nSCOPE NOTE: this is bigger than adding hasTransmitted(). The entry\nobs-txid-names-the-reply-the-unit-sent-and-nothing-checks-whether-we-received-it was written\nas "add a comparison". Per this ruling it is "the receipts are a first-class input to the\nlifecycle". Not to be quietly narrowed back to the comparison when it is picked up.'}
2026-08-20 14:38:29,345 2028104 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "GAP the device's own receipts are the bench-serial confirmation delayed one beat, and the bridge stores them without ever acting on them", 'description': 'Register: obs-txid-names-the-reply-the-unit-sent-and-nothing-checks-whether-we-received-it\n\nPETER, 2026-08-20, and this is the framing that justifies the whole thing:\n"that is confirmation that you get from bench via serial, only it is delayed. but it is there\nand it was put there for this very reason"\n"it should be integrated so that we use it and dnot just use if for debugging"\n\nTHE EQUIVALENCE, which is the point:\n\n  dongle console (cable, immediate)     obs receipts (over the air, +1 beat)\n  RX: id=0x...                          rxid   the command arrived\n  REPLY : id=0x... OK                   ans    the command ran\n  TXT txdone                            txid   the reply went on the air\n\nSame three facts. The cable version works on ONE unit. The receipt version works on EVERY\nunit, needs no hardware, and is already arriving every beat.\n\nALREADY BUILT AND UNUSED:\n  src/devstate.js:572   stores s.txid\n  src/devstate.js:584   receipts() exposes it as `transmitted`\n  src/devstate.js:604   hasReceived / hasAnswered exist — no comparator for txid\n  devstate.receipts()   NO CALLER in src/bridge.js or src/api.js — dead accessor\n  /state comms.lastTx   published already, compared by nobody\n\nPROVEN 2026-08-20 13:08 on !987ab80f: rxid=eb9b2b93 (our command), ans=eb9b2b93 (it ran it),\ntxid=bad2242a (the reply it sent). We received zero frames with id bad2242a. Its console\nindependently showed RX at -56dBm, \'TXT enq 229\', \'REPLY : id=0xbad2242a OK\', txgo, txdone.\nThe announce carried the same verdict as the cable, one beat later.\n\nNOT a comparator. The receipts must drive the command\'s state automatically, every beat, for\nevery unit, and be VISIBLE — Peter\'s word — rather than queryable after a failure.\n\nSame failure is already recorded at src/devstate.js:560-563 for rxid and was fixed 2026-08-19.\nThis is the second occurrence in the same function.'}
2026-08-20 14:46:04,031 2028323 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'obs-carries-the-delivery-proof-and-nothing-uses-it', 'title': 'GAP - every obs beat already says what the unit last heard and last answered, and nothing joins it to what we sent', 'steps': ['VERIFY - establish exactly what rxid, txid and ans are stamped by, and what they cannot prove', 'GAP - expose the join in our own code: what we sent vs what the next obs says arrived', "AGREE - round-trip takes the hop check from bridge and logs it (bridge's harness, so raise it)", 'VERIFY - prove the three-state inference on a live unit, red then green']}
