2026-08-21 07:44:05,951 2028323 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-21, verbatim, and it is the whole specification:\n\n"the point is we should ONLY tag the message received that was a command, and not based on\ntimeline at all"\n\nand, immediately before it:\n\n"rxid names the last packet received from anyone — our other units, mesh-gw\'s own traffic,\npresumably anything on the channel. So if the device receives our command and then hears any\nother frame before its next obs, our receipt is overwritten and gone."\n\nand his first instinct, which is NOT what we should implement and the reason is recorded below:\n\n"but, we should have a filter on the entry point that discards any traffic that does not start\nwith \\"@xxxx\\""\n\nWHAT IS TRUE TODAY\n  main.cpp:3894   g_lastRxId = rx.id;   FIRST LINE of onPacket(), before any test\n  main.cpp:2305   isCommandText() exists and tests for a leading \'@\' -- it runs 21 lines LATER\n  MtCmd.cpp:211   addressedToMe is computed from this unit\'s 4-hex suffix; \'*\' is broadcast\n\nSo a filter already exists for DISPATCH. The defect is only that the stamp precedes it.\n\nWHY A BLANKET DISCARD AT THE ENTRY POINT IS THE WRONG SHAPE. Everything below arrives at that\nsame function and none of it starts with \'@\':\n  :3905  mtdb::heard()          the node table, fed by every decoded packet\n  :3907  handleNodeInfo()       NODEINFO_APP from other nodes\n  :3914  mesh.sendAck()         the receipt for a directed want_ack text\n  :3917  handlePrivateApp()     port 260 {"type":"set"} config writes\n  :3919  chunk pull / push      port 261 transfers\n  :3892  g_lastRxMs             "the receiver is still alive" -- deliberately ANY packet\nDiscarding at the entry point loses the node DB, NodeInfo, the ACK path, the config route,\nchunk transfer and the liveness signal.\n\nTHE CHANGE: move the stamp into the command path and gate it on addressedToMe (so \'@*\' counts\nand another unit\'s \'@336b\' does not). Only three sites read the value -- :1778 (status),\n:2107 (obs), and the stamp itself -- so it is contained.\n\nMEASURED, so nobody argues about whether it matters: 891 wakes on the HOME console, 171 heard\nat least one frame, 36 heard MORE THAN ONE (~21% of them). Bridge resolved the actual values\nand found all three of our units recording EACH OTHER, and one recording a mesh-gw broadcast:\n  !8cee336b rxid=51112e62 -> a frame FROM !987ab80f\n  !18a01fc4 rxid=dd193e1f -> a MESH-GW broadcast\n\nTHE ASYMMETRY THIS FIXES: today rxid==X proves arrival, rxid!=X proves nothing. Bridge\'s\noffset-sweep experiment is built on that negative and roughly one probe in five would read\n"window shut" when the command arrived and something else simply spoke afterwards.\n\nCONSEQUENCE TO LAND WITH IT: schema/frames.json labels the field "Last packet received (hex)",\nwhich becomes wrong the moment the meaning narrows. Fixing the label edits the schema and\nmoves the wire hash, so it rides the next coordinated change; bridge is told the label is\nstale in the interim. A wrong receipt is worse than a stale label.\n\nans IS NOT AFFECTED -- written only in the four answer paths, so command-scoped by\nconstruction, and since 3-260820-3 it also requires the answer to have reached txdone.'}
2026-08-21 07:49:24,363 2028104 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'GAP the command lifecycle lives in memory and dies with the entry, so nothing can be replayed and round-trip re-derives it ad hoc', 'description': 'Peter, 2026-08-21: "the reality is message lifecycle is in memory and one replaces the next... what I am saying is persistent lifecycles so they can be replayed, AND instead of what you have now where round trip traces it ad hoc, this should be part of the code that is logged by pm2."\n\nAnd the key, corrected by him: "I am talking about our tx id, the message id, the device meta is a key in the our message id object."\n\nSo: one durable row per command, keyed on OUR packet id, with the device\'s receipts (rxid/ans/txid) nested under it as they arrive.\n\nMEASURED: events persist (queue 113, send 147, reply 191, giveup 22, cancel 10) but lifecycle rows = 0. round-trip re-derives a run from seven event kinds every time, which is why its hop 2 and hop 3 contradicted each other on 2026-08-20.\n\nAlso folds in: a-command-reads-sent-for-a-whole-beat-after-its-fate-was-already-decided, and the missing confirmation phase — `answered` is terminal today but the device\'s own confirmation arrives one beat later.'}
2026-08-21 08:01:09,192 2028323 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'rxms — Peter, 2026-08-21, verbatim:\n\n"another obs key rxms - this will be the time into the wake window that the command message was\nheard, it will default to -1"\n\nthen, on the sentinel and the space:\n\n"well you can use null if you like. it should be a mesurement of when after the window opens\nwas the command recevived. and I believe we have a lot of free space in obs still"\n\nSETTLED DESIGN\n  rxms   i32, req: opt\n         ABSENT        no command heard this wake  (bit clear -> decoder reports null)\n         0 .. win      arrived inside the window, N ms after rxopen\n         negative      arrived before it opened, during the announce\n         > win         arrived after rxdone, in the drain, radio still on\n\n  Anchor is rxopen (MtWake.cpp:156), NOT wake start. Not clamped: a command landing 200 ms\n  after rxdone is the most useful reading we can publish and clamping would hide it.\n\n  NULL RATHER THAN -1 removes the one-millisecond collision entirely. packBuild matches by KEY\n  and omits what the call site has nothing for -- MtReply.cpp:95-97: "What it omits keeps its\n  bit clear, which is a FACT the decoder reports as null rather than a zero it would have to\n  guess about." Bridge\'s decoder honours it: "absent, and that is a fact, not a zero".\n\n  First command addressed to us wins. The window closes on the first queued reply\n  (MtWake.cpp:193) so normally there is only one.\n\nCOST\n  payload 41 B of 233 today, so bytes are not the constraint -- rxms is 2 B zigzag varint.\n  FIELD SLOTS ARE: obs is 18 of 24 before the presence bitmap grows 3 -> 4 bytes, and the\n  bitmap is not on the wire, so crossing 24 misaligns every value after it for an un-updated\n  decoder. With rxms and flags: 20 of 24.\n  main.cpp:2124 is `JField f[18]  // sized EXACTLY to the appends below -- no headroom, on\n  purpose`. That bound moves to 19 in the same edit.\n\nBLOCKED ON THE HASH WINDOW. A new obs field edits schema/frames.json and moves the wire hash.\nQueue for that one coordinated change: canonical projection, flags, rxms, the rxid label\n("Last packet received (hex)" becomes wrong once the meaning narrows), and "t" -> "type" on\nthe sch page.\n\nNOTE FOR WHOEVER READS THE FIELD: in the 27% of wakes where CAD silences the announce, rxopen\nis ~43 ms into the wake and no obs preceded it. rxms is still measured from rxopen and is\nstill correct, but the obs carrying it goes out after the window -- so the receipt arrives a\nbeat later than usual. Not a late command.'}
