2026-08-21 18:59:32,858 2337678 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 59, 'text': 'SHIPPED 5434e92. npm test 560 pass 0 fail (was 555). reanchor 1 rewritten 0 problems. Default has NOT moved: transport=\'own\', every existing caller unchanged.\n\nSTOREWARD RE-PROVEN ON THE LIVE FLEET FIRST, because Peter\'s answer to a hedge was "if you are unsure re-test it". Queued 17:45:11Z, production TTLs (must x 1.2):\n  !18a01fc4  held 180 s  fired 17:48:11.7Z  packet_id 2050101549  1 reply threaded\n  !987ab80f  held 275 s  fired 17:49:46.6Z  packet_id 2368837448  1 reply threaded\nBoth within 0.2 s of the wake OUR OWN aim predicted independently. !8cee336b powered off: still held, not vanished (its expiry at 18:19:11Z fell outside the watcher ceiling, so expired-not-silently-absent is NOT claimed).\n\nLANDED: send(unit,text,{transport}) stamps the route on the ENTRY once; handToStoreward() POSTs once at queue time with no retry; fireOnHeard returns for any entry not marked \'own\' (the exclusivity guard); settleOnContact leaves a held entry alone until it fires (our budget is 1 contact, storeward\'s hold is 7 h).\n\nTWO THINGS THE TESTS CAUGHT THAT I HAD WRONG:\n1. `must` is NOT on devstate.schedule(), which is what the-storeward-ttl-comes-from-the-unit.md:23 said to read. It is on the bridge\'s state map at :1928. Spec corrected in this commit. AND IT ARRIVES ONLY ON A `config` FRAME - so after a restart we cannot derive a unit\'s TTL until it next declares config, and storeward\'s default applies in the gap. Operational, not cosmetic.\n2. I WROTE A LOAD-SENSITIVE TEST AN HOUR AFTER READING ABOUT ONE IN THE HANDOVER. The young-POST assertion needed contact two within 10 ms of contact one: green standalone, `not ok 16` under 85 files of load. Same shape as the-seam-test-is-load-sensitive-and-flaked-once. Split so each half picks the beat that makes its own condition unfalsifiable by a scheduler. Suite run 3x: 558/558 each, no flake.\n\nRAISED WITH html BEFORE IT LANDS, not after: bridge--mt-alarm-html #51 [a-held-command-has-no-packet-id] - the new non-terminal \'held\' state, and that a lifecycle row\'s subject is no longer guaranteed to be a numeric packet id. Offered to carry both keys instead if it breaks them.\n\nTOOL NOTE FOR THE NEXT SESSION: xs.py `ask` takes the TITLE positionally with --slug and --body as FLAGS. `reply` takes the slug positionally. Getting them the same way round produces an OSError about a filename 2 kB long, twice.\n\nSTEP TWO, NOT DONE: flip the default, move the own-transport path (per-radio POST, bestRadioFor, want_ack, hold-until-heard) intact into a reference module naming the last SHA it ran live under. Peter: "we should not delete our code but disconnect it and keep as a reference."'}
