2026-08-21 20:28:34,230 2360378 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'GAP: hold before the slew, and add the predicted slew time to the requested window', 'description': 'Peter, 2026-08-21: "hold before the slew, and add the predicted slew time to the requested window - yes, you will have to do that. long term I will fix that, but even if I do if you use that approach it will work before and after the fix."\n\nbridge\'s arithmetic: their 27422 ms was derived as ON-TARGET time but the clock starts before the slew, so a 121 deg home-to-garage slew left 17339 ms — short by 5513 ms, every time, invisible because nothing else could take the dish.\n\nlock_ms = requested_hold_ms + |shortest_arc(az_now, target)| / slew_deg_per_s, placed before arrival.'}
2026-08-21 20:50:38,455 2360378 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'COMING FROM THE ROTATOR FIRMWARE, not built yet — Peter, 2026-08-21: "I have a session open with rotator now. new feature \'aim in x (secs/ms not sure which yet)\' that is coming, not done yet, should not hold you back."\n\nUNITS ARE UNDECIDED — seconds or milliseconds. Do not assume; read the frame or ask before using it.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS HERE: bridge asked for "POINT AT !node FOR INSTANT T" (scheduled aim) because only they can predict a wake. The obvious implementation is a timer inside aim via ctx.spawn. If the firmware gains "aim in x", the better implementation is to DELEGATE the delay to the rotator: a firmware-held schedule survives a mesh-gw restart, cannot become an unowned timer that moves real hardware after the plugin that armed it is gone, and removes the one case ctx.spawn\'s docstring was written for.\n\nSo when a scheduled point is built: prefer delegating to the firmware if the capability is present, and keep an aim-side timer only as the fallback for a rotator that lacks it. Peter says it should not hold us back, so building the aim-side path first is fine — just do not bake in the assumption that aim owns the clock.'}
2026-08-21 20:54:49,549 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'ws-slot-exhaustion', 'title': 'BUG the dashboard locks itself out: WS slots are never reaped, so eight stale clients kill the UI while HTTP stays up', 'steps': ['Investigate', 'Document', 'Implement', 'Observe', 'Track']}
2026-08-21 20:55:13,848 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-21: "for some resson the dashboar is down but api is up. probably console errors killing it"\n\nNOT console errors. MEASURED with the browser console open:\n    WebSocket connection to \'ws://192.168.10.186:81/\' failed:\n    Error during WebSocket handshake: net::ERR_CONNECTION_RESET   (x4 retries)\nThose four are the ONLY errors on the page. No JS exception. The page loads and runs; it\nsimply never gets data.\n\nThe split Peter saw is exactly right and is the diagnostic: HTTP :80 served /schema 200\nthroughout while WS :81 reset every handshake. They are separate servers, so the dashboard\ndies and the "api" lives.\n\nSTATE AT THE TIME, all measured:\n - python websocket.create_connection -> ConnectionResetError [Errno 104]\n - curl http://192.168.10.186/schema -> 200\n - mosquitto_pub to uk/command reached the broker (echoed on uk/#) but the device answered on\n   NEITHER uk/response NOR uk/yagi/log — so MQTT was not being processed either\n - only OTA still worked; `pio run -e mqtt_ota -t upload` succeeded and the reboot restored\n   everything (WS OK, az 166, yagiOffset 119 intact)\n\nCAUSE, and it is mine in origin but a real firmware defect in kind:\n - include/wsserver.h:22   static constexpr uint8_t WS_MAX_CLIENTS = 8;\n - include/wsserver.h:104-117  wsSetup() calls ws.begin() and NEVER ws.enableHeartbeat().\nWith no ping/pong the server never learns that a client has gone away without a clean close,\nso that client\'s slot is held. Once eight slots are held, every new handshake is refused.\nMy test harness opened dozens of connections across the session, several of which died on an\nexception before closing, and Playwright navigations add more.\n\nIT RECURS FAST, which is what makes it Peter\'s problem and not just my mess. After the OTA\nreboot I made only a handful of connections — one python probe, three Playwright navigations\n— and the handshake was refused again within minutes. Eight uncleanly-closed browser tabs\nwill lock Peter out of his own dashboard, permanently, until he reboots the device. He has no\nway to reboot it from a dead dashboard either: the only HTTP routes are /, /index.html and\n/schema (include/httpserver.h:90-92), and MQTT was not answering.\n\nWHAT I HAVE NOT PROVEN: that slot exhaustion is the mechanism, as opposed to heap exhaustion\nor a library-internal failure that also manifests as a handshake reset. The symptom, the\nrecovery-on-reboot and the absence of any reaping are consistent with it, but I have not\nwatched the slot count climb. That is why this task ALSO exposes the count rather than only\napplying the fix — a defence that cannot be observed working is not a defence.\n\nThe library supports both halves:\n .pio/libdeps/dev/WebSockets/src/WebSocketsServer.h:86  int connectedClients(bool ping=false);\n .pio/libdeps/dev/WebSockets/src/WebSocketsServer.h:90  void enableHeartbeat(uint32_t pingInterval, uint32_t pongTimeout, uint8_t disconnectTimeoutCount);'}
