2026-08-21 21:17:58,620 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'aim-in-scheduled-move', 'title': 'aimIn: be on target at T, hold from T, then return — a scheduled point-and-hold', 'steps': ['Investigate', 'Document', 'Implement', 'Observe', 'Track']}
2026-08-21 21:18:15,479 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-21: "aim in = aim the antenna in x seconds (or ms whichever we use for hold) must be used in conjunction with hold."\n\nAsked whether X is when the antenna STARTS moving or when it should already BE on target.\nPeter chose: BE ON TARGET AT X.\n\nTWO OF THE THREE REQUESTED FEATURES ALREADY EXISTED. Measured before building anything:\n 1. "in the status, report the return to position" — retArmed and retAz are already in every\n    status frame and declared in /schema. Idle: retArmed=false retAz=-1. During a hold:\n    retArmed=true retAz=197, 197 being the bearing noted before the move. Also drawn on the\n    compass as a dashed marker. Shipped earlier as yagi/seek2az-hold-return work.\n 3. "hold starts when the antenna has reached its target instead of when the request was\n    received" — ALREADY TRUE. Measured: 8000 ms hold requested on a move taking 2.44 s ->\n    hold length from ARRIVAL 8.05 s, from REQUEST 10.49 s. armHold() is called after the jog\n    loop, immediately before fireDone, so the clock already starts on arrival, within 50 ms.\nOnly "aim in" is new.\n\nTHE SLEW MODEL IS TOO POOR TO ARRIVE EXACTLY AT T, and this decides the design.\nMeasured six moves, request -> done, on the live rotator:\n    arc   3   0.77 s\n    arc  15   0.48 s\n    arc  39   2.40 s\n    arc 122   2.96 s\n    arc 179   3.87 s\n    arc 148   4.77 s\nLeast squares: t = 0.797 + 0.02070 * arc  (797 ms fixed + 20.7 ms/deg, i.e. 48 deg/s).\nResiduals: +0.09 +0.63 -0.79 +0.36 +0.63 -0.91 s. The data is NOT monotonic — arc 15 took\n0.48 s while arc 39 took 2.40 s, and arc 179 took 3.87 s while arc 148 took 4.77 s — because\nthe jog phase count depends on where the initial shot happens to land and settle() is\nvariable. A +/-0.9 s estimate cannot be the thing a receive window depends on.\n\nDESIGN THAT DELIVERS THE CHOSEN CONTRACT ANYWAY: do not try to arrive exactly at T. Start\nearly enough to be certain of arriving BEFORE T, then start the hold clock AT T.\n  start_at = T - (0.797 + 0.0207*arc) - MARGIN\n  MARGIN   = 2.0 s, derived as 2x the worst observed under-prediction (0.91 s)\nArriving early is then harmless — the beam simply sits on target waiting — and the window\nboundary is exact rather than inheriting the model\'s error. The only thing the model has to\nget right is "not later than T", which the margin buys.\n\nIf T is sooner than the predicted slew plus margin, the move starts immediately and the\ndevice says so rather than silently missing the window.'}
2026-08-21 21:18:52,496 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': '/ij from Peter, 2026-08-21, VERBATIM:\n\n"nodejs is abusing it\'s access. I want to add a secret for requests, this will be kapong - I am not interested in your questions regarding security, just implement it. put in secrets so we can change at runtime"\n\nMY READING, kept separate from his words above:\n - A shared secret must accompany requests to this device. Requests without it are rejected.\n - The value is the literal string `kapong`.\n - It lives in "secrets" — i.e. NVS via `con`, like every other persisted setting — so it can\n   be changed on a running device rather than being compiled in.\n - "I am not interested in your questions regarding security" — he does not want a discussion\n   of threat model, transport encryption, replay, or whether a shared secret over plaintext WS\n   is the right primitive. Build the thing he asked for. Any such concern is recorded, not\n   raised as a blocking question.\n - Motive stated: node-dash ("nodejs") is abusing its access. This connects to work just\n   finished — ws-slot-exhaustion (6ca6269) measured node-dash and the dashboard reconnecting\n   repeatedly and consuming WebSocket slots, which is what took the dashboard down.\n\nEFFECT ON WORK IN FLIGHT: aim-in-scheduled-move is PAUSED at Phase 1 complete (slew model\nmeasured and recorded in this task\'s goal note; nothing implemented, nothing committed). The\nsecret gates the command surface that aimIn will be dispatched through, so it lands first —\nbuilding aimIn now would only mean revisiting its dispatch path immediately afterwards.\n\nCROSS-REPO CONSEQUENCE, and it is not optional: this BREAKS node-dash the moment it ships.\nnode-dash sends {action, args} with no secret (rotator.js _send). Under /xsession that is\nraised with them, never worked around and never silently shipped. Peter\'s intent is plainly\nto gate them, so the gate goes in — but they get told what the new envelope is so they can\ncomply, rather than discovering it as an outage.'}
2026-08-21 21:19:27,971 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'request-secret', 'title': 'Gate the command surface behind a runtime-changeable shared secret', 'steps': ['Investigate', 'Document', 'Implement', 'Observe', 'Track']}
2026-08-21 21:19:46,490 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-21, VERBATIM:\n\n"nodejs is abusing it\'s access. I want to add a secret for requests, this will be kapong - I am not interested in your questions regarding security, just implement it. put in secrets so we can change at runtime"\n\nDelivered to the aim-in-scheduled-move task as an /ij; this task implements it. aim-in is\nPAUSED at Phase 1 complete, nothing built, nothing committed.\n\nWHAT IS BEING BUILT, as instructed and without debate:\n - Commands carry a secret. Without the right one, the command is refused.\n - Value is the literal `kapong`, as the shipped default.\n - Held in NVS via `con`, like every other persisted setting, so it is changeable on a running\n   device rather than compiled in. New command `setSecret <value>` writes it.\n\n"I am not interested in your questions regarding security" — no threat-model discussion, and\nno blocking questions. Two facts are RECORDED here rather than raised, because a future\nreader will otherwise assume they were considered and rejected:\n - WS and MQTT are plaintext on the LAN, so the secret travels in clear and is replayable by\n   anything that can see the traffic. It gates casual and accidental access, not an attacker\n   on the wire.\n - Peter\'s stated problem is a MISBEHAVING PEER, not an adversary. For "node-dash is hammering\n   my rotator", a shared secret is a perfectly reasonable instrument, which is why this is\n   being built as asked rather than argued about.\n\nSCOPE DECISION — commands only, not subscription. He said "a secret for requests". A request\nis an action: wsCmd\'s {action, args} dispatch and onCmd\'s MQTT equivalent. The status\nbroadcast and the {op:set} subscription are NOT gated, so a read-only viewer still works and\nthe dashboard still shows the compass without a secret. If he wants subscription gated too\nthat is a one-line follow-up, and it is called out rather than silently assumed either way.\n\nTHE SECRET MUST NOT APPEAR IN /schema. /schema is served unauthenticated over HTTP to anyone\nwho asks, and api-schema\'s whole purpose is that it is self-describing. It will therefore\ndeclare THAT a `secret` field is required on commands, and never what the value is.\n\nCROSS-REPO, NOT OPTIONAL: this breaks node-dash the moment it ships — rotator.js _send emits\n{action, args} with no secret. Under /xsession that is raised with them, never worked around.\nGating them is plainly Peter\'s intent, so the gate goes in; they get told the new envelope so\nthey can comply rather than discovering it as an outage. They are already 6 messages behind\non the board (cursor 12, latest 18), so they may not read it promptly.'}
2026-08-21 21:20:56,541 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'aim-in-scheduled-move', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'DESIGN CHANGED BY PETER, 2026-08-21. VERBATIM:\n\n"ok, we have to accept the difficulty of this. change my mind, not to have hit target, just commmand it at <tim> forget about th eseeking at the end. KISS"\n\nMY READING, kept separate from his words above:\n - He has reversed the choice he made earlier today. Earlier he picked "be on target AT X";\n   this supersedes it. The new contract is "ISSUE THE COMMAND at X".\n - "forget about the seeking at the end" — drop the arrival-time compensation entirely: no\n   slew-time prediction, no starting early, no margin, no waiting-until-T-before-the-hold.\n - "KISS" — the simplest thing that works. aimIn(delay_ms, az, hold_ms) waits delay_ms, then\n   issues exactly the seek2az it would have issued anyway.\n - "we have to accept the difficulty of this" — he is agreeing with the measurement I reported\n   rather than overruling it. The slew model fitted t = 0.797 + 0.0207*arc with residuals up to\n   +/-0.91 s and non-monotonic data, and he has decided that error is not worth engineering\n   around.\n\nWHAT THIS DELETES from the design in this task\'s goal note, which now stands as history:\n - the least-squares slew model\n - the 2.0 s margin derived from the worst under-prediction\n - starting early so as to arrive before T\n - starting the hold clock AT T rather than on arrival\n\nWHAT SURVIVES: hold still starts on ARRIVAL, because it already does and he asked for that\nseparately today — measured 8.05 s of hold on an 8000 ms request. So the sequence is:\n    wait delay_ms  ->  seek2az(az)  ->  hold_ms from arrival  ->  return to origin\nEvery stage of which already exists except the initial wait.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR EFFORT: this is now a small feature. The only new mechanism is a deferred\ncommand — a scheduled time and a target held until millis() reaches it, fired from loop()\nbeside the existing return leg, which is already the place loop() commands a move from.\n\nSTATUS: still PAUSED behind request-secret, which Peter interjected first and which gates the\ndispatch path aimIn would be issued through.'}
