2026-08-21 21:32:06,354 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': '/ij from Peter, 2026-08-21. VERBATIM:\n\n"now I have an interesting one. this whole code will be ported to new hardware, there is no seeking involved, it has steppers and and encoder and is zeroed to north. so what we do is have a func that looks for a reques that does NOT have secret then respond with a done instantly upon the request. the caller must see this as if they would if it took us 1 second, 2 or 3. to them they asked and we said done. this allows me not to break existing code that already calls us."\n\nMY READING, kept separate from his words above:\n - CHANGES WHAT THE GATE DOES ON FAILURE. Shipped behaviour (88f16ad) answers a secretless\n   command with {"evt":"denied","reason":"bad or missing secret"}. That is now wrong. A\n   secretless command must instead get the NORMAL SUCCESS HANDSHAKE — started, then done,\n   ok:true — while the device does nothing at all.\n - The point is compatibility, not refusal: "this allows me not to break existing code that\n   already calls us." An un-updated caller keeps working, sees success, and never errors.\n - Background, not an instruction for today: the code is being ported to new hardware with\n   steppers, an encoder, and zeroed to north, where "there is no seeking involved". So the\n   whole compass-seek path (seek2az\'s shot-and-jog, settle, compassFault) has no successor on\n   the new board. Recorded because it bears on retire-move2az and on how much is worth\n   investing in the seek path from here.\n\nAMBIGUITY I AM NOT GUESSING AT SILENTLY — timing. "respond with a done instantly upon the\nrequest" says immediate. "the caller must see this as if they would if it took us 1 second,\n2 or 3" could mean either (a) the RESPONSE SHAPE must be indistinguishable from a real one\nthat took 1-3 s, or (b) the response must also be DELAYED by 1-3 s so the timing matches.\nBuilding (a) — instant, because that is what he said in the imperative sentence — and asking\nabout (b) in the next report rather than stalling. If he wants the delay it is a scheduled\nsend, not a redesign.\n\nCONSEQUENCE HE MAY NOT HAVE WEIGHED, stated once and not argued: node-dash aims this antenna\nand then attributes RF measurements to the bearing it believes it commanded. Under this\nchange it will be told "done" for moves that never happened, so every contact, RSSI and\ntraceroute it records while unsecreted will be filed against a bearing the beam was never\npointing at. That is a data-integrity effect on Peter\'s own mesh history, not a security\npoint, which is why it is raised despite his instruction to skip security questions. His call\nentirely.\n\nNote on the peer: message #19 already told node-dash that a secret is required and that they\nwould be refused. That message is now partly wrong about the symptom — they will see success,\nnot refusal. They are still on cursor 12 and have read none of it. Correcting it to them is\nPeter\'s call, since telling them defeats the compatibility he is asking for; #19 already\ngives them everything they need to comply if they ever read it.'}
