2026-08-21 22:45:15,269 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'ws-stale-reap', 'title': 'Disconnect stale WebSocket clients — define stale from evidence, not from silence', 'steps': ['Investigate', 'Document', 'Implement', 'Observe', 'Track']}
2026-08-21 22:45:31,868 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-21, VERBATIM:\n\n"if wsClient) is stale disconnect it, no reason for the caller to hang on the line."\n\nMY READING, kept separate from his words:\n - A connection that is doing nothing must be dropped rather than left holding a slot.\n - Trigger: wsClients has sat at 8 of 12 for ~40 minutes (uptime 3195 s, heap healthy at\n   148 KB), with only three clients I can account for — node-dash, a browser, one observer.\n\n"STALE" MUST NOT MEAN "SILENT", AND THIS IS THE WHOLE DIFFICULTY. Almost every client of\nthis device is a pure SUBSCRIBER: it sends {"op":"set",...} once and then receives status at\n25 ms forever without ever speaking again. node-dash is one. The dashboard is another.\nReaping on inbound silence would disconnect exactly the clients that are working correctly,\nwhich is worse than the slot pressure it would relieve.\n\nSo the definition has to come from measurement, not from a plausible-sounding rule. Already\ncovered by earlier work and NOT the target here:\n - dead sockets that fail a send — reaped after 20 consecutive failures (500 ms at the 25 ms\n   broadcast cadence), shipped in 6ca6269\n - clients that stop answering pings — enableHeartbeat(2000, 2000, 2), also 6ca6269, though\n   that path remains UNVERIFIED because I could not construct a true half-open socket\n\nWHAT IS NOT YET KNOWN, and is the actual Phase 1 question: what ARE those 8 slots? Until that\nis answered any reap rule is a guess, and a wrong guess disconnects Peter\'s own dashboard.\n\nThe library gives enough to answer it. Both are PUBLIC on WebSocketsServer:\n    WebSocketsServer.h:55  IPAddress remoteIP(uint8_t num);\n    WebSocketsServer.h:49  bool clientIsConnected(uint8_t num);\n(_clients[] itself is protected, so pongTimeoutCount and lastPing are out of reach.)\n\nCombined with our own wsSubs[i] that is enough to characterise every slot:\n    slot | connected | remoteIP | subscribed?\nAn IP identifies whether a slot is node-dash\'s host, Peter\'s browser, or my own scratchpad\nharness; wsSubs says whether it ever asked for anything.\n\nPLAN: ship the diagnostic FIRST as a read-only command, look at the real eight, and only then\nwrite the reap rule with the threshold derived from what is actually there. Two flashes rather\nthan one, deliberately — the alternative is inventing a definition of "stale" and finding out\nafterwards whose connection it killed.'}
