2026-08-22 07:03:17,696 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'yagi-cli', 'title': 'scripts/yagi.py — a command-line client for the rotator, with the WS traps baked in', 'steps': ['Investigate', 'Document', 'Implement', 'Observe', 'Track']}
2026-08-22 07:03:36,022 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-22, VERBATIM:\n\n"so create a python script that can be called as it it was with ws, i.e a cli"\n\nFollowing on from "aimIn <delay_ms> <target_deg> [hold_ms] how to call with curl", where the\nanswer turned out to be that you cannot:\n - there is NO HTTP command route on the v4. include/httpserver.h:90-92 registers only\n   GET /, GET /index.html and GET /schema.\n - curl 8.14.1 does list ws/wss, but its CLI can open a socket and not send an application\n   text frame. Tested: --data-binary with a JSON body returns rc=22 and nothing reaches the\n   device, no log line, aimArmed stays false.\n - MQTT is not an alternative either — publishing a valid aimIn to uk/command is echoed by\n   the broker on uk/# and the device never acts on it. Separate live fault, recorded below.\n\nSo the WS on :81 is the only working command path, and it needs a proper client.\n\nWHAT THE TOOL MUST ENCODE, because these are the traps that have cost this session real time\nand produced at least one false verdict:\n\n1. THE 25 ms BACKLOG. The status stream broadcasts every 25 ms. Reading one frame per recv()\n   replays a queue rather than reporting the present. This produced a confident "0/5 runs\n   FAILED" on firmware that was working, and twice more showed a frozen uptime. The tool must\n   drain to the newest frame or run a reader thread. Anyone using the tool then gets this\n   right for free.\n2. `busy` IS NOT A MOTION FLAG. Set at the top of update_encoder() and cleared at the bottom\n   (include/servo.h), so it is true only inside the ISR and reads false throughout a move.\n   Completion is the `done` event, never a busy transition.\n3. WAIT ON THE EVENT, NOT THE CLOCK. /derive: block on the completion event. A fixed sleep\n   either truncates a long slew or wastes seconds on a short one.\n\nTIMEOUTS MUST BE DERIVED FROM THE COMMAND, not fixed. Measured slews reach 4.77 s for a 148\ndeg arc; hold_ms is bounded by node-dash\'s shared-rotator ceiling of 120 s; aimIn\'s delay is\ncapped at AIM_MAX_MS = 86400000 (24 h). A single constant is therefore wrong in both\ndirections — the tool computes the wait from the arguments it was given.\n\nEXIT CODES so it is scriptable: 0 when done arrives with ok true, 1 when ok false, 2 on\ntimeout, 3 on connection failure.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE, recorded rather than fixed here:\n - the dead MQTT command intake (own task; broker delivers, device ignores)\n - adding an HTTP POST /cmd route, which is what would actually make curl work. Raised with\n   Peter once already as his decision: an unauthenticated command endpoint on a device that\n   moves a mast is a bigger choice than it looks, especially now the secret has been reverted.'}
