2026-08-22 07:12:36,800 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': "aiminms-reported-nonzero-after-firing — UNVERIFIED, seen once, NOT reproduced. Recorded so it is not rediscovered as new, and NOT diagnosed, because I cannot account for it.\n\nSEEN 2026-08-22 07:10:24, in `yagi watch --field aimInMs --field az --field retArmed`\nfollowing a single `aimIn 6000 200 3000`:\n\n    07:10:16  aimInMs=0     az=124.0  retArmed=False    <- fired\n    07:10:20  aimInMs=0     az=199.0  retArmed=True     <- holding on target\n    07:10:23  aimInMs=0     az=147.0  retArmed=True     <- returning\n    07:10:24  aimInMs=5733  az=145.0  retArmed=True     <- THIS\n\nfillStatus emits `aimInMs` as `aimArmed ? (int32_t)(aimAt - millis()) : 0`, so a non-zero\nvalue means aimArmed was TRUE at that moment — i.e. an aim appeared armed again, roughly 8 s\nafter the one I sent had already fired. Only one aimIn was issued.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS ENOUGH TO WRITE DOWN: an armed aim moves the antenna later, on its own. A\nspurious one is a surprise move on a masted array, which is the same class as\ndosteps-zero-runaway.\n\nNOT REPRODUCED. A 45 s run sampling status every 50 ms through a complete aimIn cycle\n(arm -> countdown -> fire -> hold -> return -> clear) showed the flag going true exactly once,\nat the moment of arming, and never again:\n    5.9s  aimArmed True  -> False   (countdown had reached -9 ms)\n    8.9s  retArmed True,  az 193\n   16.7s  retArmed False, az 128  (origin was 125)\nA detector watching specifically for a false->true transition after firing found only the\nINITIAL arm at t=0.85 s, which is correct behaviour and was my check miscounting.\n\nDevice checked twice afterwards, 8 s apart: aimArmed False, aimAz -1, aimInMs 0. Nothing is\narmed now.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT DO: invent a mechanism. Candidate explanations I can neither confirm nor rule\nout — a torn read of aimArmed vs aimAt inside fillStatus while loop() is blocked in the\nreturn leg's seek2az and status is being broadcast from yield_ms; or something in the CLI's\nown sampling. Both are guesses.\n\nIF IT RECURS: sample at 50 ms as above and capture whether `aimAz` is also non-(-1) at the\nsame instant. aimAz and aimInMs are both gated on the same aimArmed, so if aimAz stays -1\nwhile aimInMs goes positive the fault is in fillStatus's read, not in the flag."}
2026-08-22 07:30:36,248 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'missing-arg-moves-the-antenna', 'title': 'BUG a motion command with no argument slews to 0 — and the CLI lets it reach the wire', 'steps': ['Investigate', 'Document', 'Implement', 'Observe', 'Track']}
2026-08-22 07:30:57,478 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-22, VERBATIM:\n\n"script is buggy and the help is poor. I can call yagi aimIn with missing args and it still works. if I call a cli withour args it should tell me"\n\nHe is right about the CLI. Testing it turned up something worse that he did not report.\n\nTHE SERIOUS ONE — A MISSING ARGUMENT MOVES THE ANTENNA.\n    $ yagi seek2az            # no target at all\n    started  cmd=seek2az target=0\n    done     cmd=seek2az az=1 ok=True\nThe mast slewed from 126 deg to 1 deg. Empty String -> toFloat() -> 0.0 -> seek to north.\nReproduced live; Peter\'s antenna was moved by it and has been put back to 119.\n\nI CAUSED THIS BY MIS-SCOPING MY OWN EARLIER FIX. bf7fa5b (missing-arg-is-not-zero) added\nneedArg() to the setters and to `hold`, and its spec explicitly listed the motion commands as\nOUT of scope: "fwd, rev — transient PWM; a missing argument stops the motor rather than\ncorrupting anything", "jog2az — transient". That reasoning was wrong for the ones that take a\nBEARING: seek2az with no argument is not transient, it is a full slew to 0 deg.\n\nAUDIT of every registration in src/main.cpp, checked individually rather than by proximity:\n    seek2az    :314  NO guard   -> slews to 0 deg          DANGEROUS, proven\n    move2az    :309  NO guard   -> moves to 0 deg          DANGEROUS\n    jog2az     :308  NO guard   -> jogs toward 0 deg       DANGEROUS\n    moveSteps  :315  NO guard   -> moveSteps(0), saved only by doSteps\' zero guard (f5041bb)\n    fwd/rev    :263  NO guard   -> raw motor on at pwm 0\n    home       :235  no arg needed (force is optional)     fine\n    calMotor   :248  uses .length() ? : default            fine, correct pattern\n    hold       :313  GUARDED (bf7fa5b)\n    aimIn      :385  GUARDED — which is why Peter\'s aimIn case behaved correctly\n    all set*         GUARDED (bf7fa5b)\n\nNote this is the THIRD appearance of one root cause: an empty String converts to 0 and every\nhandler that converts unconditionally treats "you sent nothing" as "you sent zero". It has now\nproduced a wiped calibration (bf7fa5b), a six-revolution runaway via doSteps(0) (f5041bb),\nand now an uncommanded slew to north.\n\nWHAT PETER ACTUALLY REPORTED, both confirmed:\n 1. "I can call yagi aimIn with missing args and it still works" — the DEVICE refuses it\n    correctly (aimIn is guarded) and the CLI exits 1, so nothing bad happens. But the CLI\n    sends the malformed command to the wire and only then reports the device\'s complaint. It\n    knows the schema and should refuse locally, before anything is transmitted.\n 2. "if I call a cli withour args it should tell me" — `yagi` alone prints argparse\'s bare\n    usage line and exit 2. It never lists what actions exist, what arguments they take, or\n    that `status`, `watch` and `schema` are available. The device publishes all of that at\n    /schema and the tool already knows how to read it.\n\nTWO FIXES, one root cause each:\n A. FIRMWARE: needArg() on seek2az, move2az, jog2az and moveSteps\' first argument. A command\n    that cannot know where to point must refuse, not point at north.\n B. CLI: validate arity locally against /schema before sending; and with no arguments, print\n    a real help listing the device\'s own commands rather than an argparse stub.'}
