2026-08-22 07:51:42,840 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'rip-out-the-move-beat', 'title': 'Stop broadcasting status during blocking moves — the beat is inflating every seek', 'steps': ['Investigate', 'Document', 'Implement', 'Observe', 'Track']}
2026-08-22 07:52:08,141 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-22, VERBATIM:\n\n"the seek part is very slow, it nevert used to be like that, it seems this was all introduced when the 150ms beat was added"\n\nthen:\n\n"i think we need to rip out that tick. it\'s only of benefit when another dashboard calls it and wants to plot the real time rotation, but that\'s not necessary. it simply outputs an even when reached"\n\nHE IS RIGHT THAT SOMETHING WAS ADDED, AND MY FIRST ANSWER WAS WRONG.\nI initially told him the 150 came from the v3 fork and was not added for v5 emulation, on the\nstrength of `git log -S"int maxMs = 150"` returning only b717bbf. That search was too narrow —\nit matches the DEFAULT ARGUMENT only, not the machinery around it. Searching properly:\n\n    git log -S"void yield_ms" -- include/servo.h   -> a3856f4  (NOT the fork)\n    git log -S"        settle();" -- include/servo.h -> a3856f4, b717bbf\n\na3856f4 "Refactor motion events: global onStart/onDone callbacks in motor layer" INTRODUCED\nyield_ms and touched the settle() call sites. So the beat is a v4 addition, exactly as he\nremembered, and I should not have contradicted him from a single narrow grep.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. yield_ms is not a delay:\n    void yield_ms(unsigned long ms) {\n        unsigned long t0 = millis();\n        do { if (yieldFn) yieldFn(); delay(1); } while (millis() - t0 < ms);\n    }\nand yieldFn (src/main.cpp) is\n    wsLoop(); httpLoop(); amqtt.loop();\n    if (millis() - lastYieldBcast >= 25) { lastYieldBcast = millis(); broadcastStatus(); }\nSo a yield_ms(20) runs the WS/HTTP/MQTT service loops TWENTY times and serialises a full\nstatus broadcast to every subscribed client roughly once. settle() calls yield_ms(20) in a\nloop, and seek2az calls settle() after the shot and after EVERY jog — so the beat is\nmultiplied by every settle iteration of every jog.\n\nMEASURED, four arcs, timestamping each "@seek j" log line:\n    with settle():   per-jog 258-337 ms   arc 30 total 4.30 s   arc 152 total 4.57 s\n    settle stubbed:  per-jog 108-127 ms   arc 30 total 1.16 s   arc 151 total 2.22 s\nAll four stubbed runs still landed ok=True. So settle is over half the per-jog cost and\nroughly halves total seek time.\n\nA HYPOTHESIS I HAD AND DISPROVED, recorded so nobody re-runs it: I expected the cost to scale\nwith connected WebSocket clients, since broadcastStatus serialises to each. Measured 7 vs 12\nclients: 276 -> 290 ms per jog, then 337 ms back at 7 clients. The slowest run had the FEWEST\nclients. Client count does not explain it; that spread is noise.\n\nWHAT PETER IS ASKING FOR, and its consequence: remove the periodic status broadcast from\nyieldFn. The dashboard will then jump from start bearing to end bearing instead of sweeping\nthe needle during a move. He has explicitly accepted that — "it simply outputs an event when\nreached". This reverts the visible half of 95c55f2 ("yield-broadcast: sweep needle during\nmoves").\n\nWHAT MUST NOT BE REMOVED: wsLoop(), httpLoop() and amqtt.loop() from yieldFn. A seek blocks\nthe main loop for seconds; without those the device would service nothing during a move — no\nWS, no OTA, no commands — and clients would time out. Only the broadcast goes.\n\nSTILL OPEN AFTER THIS, and separable: settle() itself is the larger measured lever, and its\ntol of 2.0 deg is the same as the seek\'s accept threshold while the QMC flutters +/-1 deg at\nrest, so its "stable" test is satisfied by noise and it behaves as a fixed ~100 ms delay\nrather than an adaptive settle. Not touched here — one change, one measurement.'}
2026-08-22 08:13:48,481 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'bare-hold-arms-stale-return', 'title': 'BUG a standalone hold arms a return to a stale origin; and the CLI hangs on a busy refusal', 'steps': ['Investigate', 'Document', 'Implement', 'Observe', 'Track']}
2026-08-22 08:14:10,262 2396009 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-22, VERBATIM:\n\n"The one that didn\'t do what I expected — a move during a hold was not refused:\n  hold 6000            → held=True  holdMs=3461\n  move2az 150 (held)   → \\"@seek errF: -1.00\\" … timeout after 30s waiting for done\n  after                → az=91, held=False    (it had been 121; it never reached 150)\nThe dish moved away from both the held bearing and the requested one, the hold was cleared, and no done came. Reporting it, not diagnosing it — you may already know, and both commands came from the same client, which may be the relevant difference."\n\nREPRODUCED EXACTLY, and his instinct that something was off is right — but not where he\nthought. THE MOVE WAS REFUSED. The hold gate works.\n\n    1. seek2az 91            -> az 92\n    2. seek2az 121           -> az 120, retArmed=False        (origin 92 noted internally)\n    3. hold 6000             -> held=True holdMs=4563  retArmed=TRUE retAz=92\n    4. move2az 150 (held)    -> replies: [\'busy\']             <- CORRECTLY REFUSED\n    5. hold lapses           -> "@return: 92.0" then @seek jogs -> az 93, held=False\n\nTWO SEPARATE DEFECTS, and the same-client detail he flagged is NOT the relevant difference.\n\nA. A STANDALONE `hold` ARMS A RETURN TO A STALE ORIGIN — firmware, and the surprising one.\n   armHold() promotes whatever originAz is lying around from an EARLIER, unrelated seek2az.\n   So `hold 6000` silently means "hold, then drive back to wherever you were before your last\n   move". Peter never asked to go to 92; it was the origin of the seek2az that took him to\n   121, minutes earlier. That is a surprise move on a masted antenna, same class as\n   dosteps-zero-runaway.\n\n   My own spec for seek2az-hold-return said a bare hold must NOT arm a return: "hold with no\n   preceding move -> no return. A bare hold(ms) claims the beam without moving it (that is its\n   documented purpose)". I implemented the weaker condition — originValid, which stays true\n   forever after the first seek2az — instead of "this hold belongs to a move".\n\n   THE RIGHT RULE: the return means "go back to where you were before THIS move". So only a\n   hold requested as part of a move (seek2az\'s hold_ms argument) arms it. The standalone\n   `hold` command must not.\n\nB. THE `@seek errF` LINE HE SAW WAS THE RETURN LEG, NOT HIS move2az. move2az logs @m2az;\n   @seek is seek2az. loop() fires seek2az(returnAz) when the hold lapses. So the log he was\n   reading was the device driving itself back to 92, which is why the dish ended up at\n   neither 121 nor 150.\n\nC. THE CLI HANGS ON A REFUSAL — mine, in scripts/yagi.py. The refusal is\n   {"evt":"busy","held":true} and _await_done() only ever returns on `done`, so it waited the\n   full derived timeout and reported "timeout after 30s waiting for done". The device answered\n   immediately and correctly; the tool ignored the answer. That is the same failure the tool\n   was written to avoid — see the No such Function handling already in it.\n\nNOT THE CAUSE, ruled out: "both commands came from the same client". The gate is\nmotor.isHeld() in wsCmd, which is client-agnostic — it refuses every client identically. Same\nor different client makes no difference.'}
