2026-08-22 11:00:10,950 2435970 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Move the test harness out of the session scratchpad and into this repo, so a suite can be **re-run against the build that fixes what it found**.\n\nPeter, 2026-08-22: *"you might need to write test scripts that you can reuse rather than creating adhoc temp scripts."*\n\nThat is not tidiness. The project\'s own goal is an iterative loop with the owning session — test, report, they fix and flash, re-test. A scratchpad does not survive the session, so a re-run would mean rewriting the suite from memory and comparing today\'s result against a harness that no longer exists.\n\nIt paid for itself the same afternoon: v4 shipped three fixes in under an hour and every one of them was verified by re-running the suite that found it.'}
2026-08-22 11:00:16,049 2435970 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'See the attached spec `docs/specs/reusable-test-rig.md` for requirements R1-R6 and the done-when list.\n\nShape: `rig/` is repo-agnostic machinery (transport, contract fetch/diff, verdicts, the safety gate); `suites/<target>/` holds what one repo claims about itself; `run.py` selects and runs them. The measure of R1 is that adding a second target requires no edit to `rig/`.\n\nTwo pieces exist because of specific incidents rather than by design:\n- `rig/safety.py` — the `v4` session invented a "safe sweep range" for a live masted antenna and retracted it an hour later. Physical constraints are accepted from the operator only, quoted verbatim.\n- reboot and attribution detection — a non-motion suite appeared to swing the antenna 18°. It had not; a third party drives the device, and it was also being reflashed underneath the run. Both produce **false findings**, which are worse than missed ones because they send the owner hunting a bug that does not exist.'}
