2026-08-22 11:11:54,699 2471134 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'MqttPublisher.stop() must not block the asyncio event loop, and must leave the broker with a clean DISCONNECT. It runs inside the lifespan teardown, so anything it blocks also blocks the BLE teardown that follows it — the same axis as the-shutdown-is-unbounded fix (2cfc318).'}
2026-08-22 11:12:01,547 2471134 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'Two defects in core/mqtt_publisher.py:79-89.\n\n1. loop_stop() does self._thread.join() with NO timeout — paho\'s own docstring: "This call will block until the network thread finishes." Called straight on the event loop, so it blocks every other coroutine, not just this one. paho checks _thread_terminate in <=1s slices in the common paths, so it usually returns in ~1-2s; but loop_forever\'s inner exit requires _out_packet AND _out_messages to be empty, so a socket that stays open and never drains holds it indefinitely.\n\n2. The order is wrong. disconnect() only QUEUES a DISCONNECT via _packet_queue and needs the network thread alive to write it. loop_stop() is called first, killing that thread, so the packet can never go out.\n\nFix: disconnect() before loop_stop(), both moved to run_in_executor and wrapped in asyncio.wait_for(MQTT_STOP_S). MQTT_STOP_S = 2.0 derived as 2x paho\'s own 1.0s loop slice (loop_forever(timeout=1.0), _reconnect_wait sleeping min(remaining,1)) — one slice to notice the flag, one to write the DISCONNECT. Must stay under TEARDOWN_STAGE_S=3.0 since stop() runs inside _stop_services(). On timeout the executor thread is abandoned; paho\'s thread is a daemon so it dies with the process, and the point is that the loop is freed for the BLE teardown.\n\nNote mqtt_publish.enabled is false in this deploy, so this path does not currently run here. The defect is real for anyone who enables it and the entry stands.\n\nProve with tests/check_mqtt_stop_does_not_block_the_loop.py: source assertions plus a functional heartbeat against an unreachable broker.'}
2026-08-22 11:12:04,955 2471134 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_file_attach args={'file_path': 'docs/modules/mqtt_publisher.md', 'kind': 'ref', 'label': 'the spec that owns core/mqtt_publisher.py'}
2026-08-22 11:16:58,078 2435970 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'v4-ws-passkey', 'title': 'VERIFY rotator v4 WebSocket passkey — enforcement, the handshake race, and key charset', 'steps': ['VERIFY empty key disables the check and nothing regresses', 'VERIFY a set key rejects no-key and wrong-key sockets with no dispatch', 'VERIFY the correct key connects and dispatches', 'VERIFY gate 2 refuses a command pipelined into the upgrade segment (the race)', 'BUG setWsKey accepts a key that can never authenticate, and it bricked the device', 'VERIFY setWsKey persists across a reboot', 'VERIFY the device is restored to the key it was found with']}
2026-08-22 11:17:03,976 2435970 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Attack the WebSocket passkey v4 added — operator-authorised: Peter, via v4, *"you can tell test about the passkey, it should test with / without it"*.\n\nThe passkey is a reject-after-connect: key rides in the URL (`?k=<key>`), the CONNECTED handler disconnects a bad one (gate 1), and `wsCmd` refuses dispatch from an unauthed slot (gate 2). v4\'s own stated worry, and the thing most worth breaking, is the race — a command pipelined into the same TCP segment as the upgrade, arriving before gate 1 runs, which only gate 2 can catch.'}
2026-08-22 11:17:07,724 2435970 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': '`suites/v4/s05_passkey.py`. Enforcement is tested with the `websockets` library; the race needs a hand-built client (`suites/v4/raceclient.py`) that pipelines the upgrade and a masked command frame into one `write()`, because the library completes the handshake before it will send anything and so can never lose — or test — the race.\n\nThe suite sets and clears a key and always restores the one it found. It **refuses to set an unreachable key** (`_key_is_reachable`), a guard added after step 5 below.\n\nReboot detection matters here more than anywhere: v4 flashed twice mid-suite, and the device came back with different keys each time. A reading taken across a reboot is void; `uptime` going backwards is the tell.'}
2026-08-22 11:17:14,620 2435970 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 4, 'text': '**PASS — the race is NOT winnable. Gate 2 held 0/8.**\n\nTested with `raceclient.py`: an unauthed connection (no `?k=`), HTTP upgrade and a masked `{"action":"compass"}` frame written into a **single TCP segment**, so the command is already in the device\'s RX buffer when the CONNECTED handler runs gate 1. Repeated 8×.\n\nEvery attempt: connection opened (101), then **closed** by the device, and the command **never executed** — no `@compass` echo in any frame. `compass` was chosen because it echoes a reading, so execution is unambiguous.\n\nSo gate 2 (`wsCmd` refuses dispatch from an unauthed slot) catches what gate 1\'s disconnect cannot prevent — a command that arrives before the disconnect takes effect. This was v4\'s own stated worry and the thing most worth breaking; it does not break.'}
2026-08-22 11:17:23,735 2435970 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 5, 'text': '**BUG, confirmed by falling into it. Raised as `wskey-unusable-key`.**\n\n`setWsKey "a&b"` — the device stored it intact (`/wskey` → `{"k":"a&b"}`) but **no connection can then authenticate as "a&b"**, because the key travels as `?k=<key>` and:\n- `&` terminates the query value, so `?k=a&b` presents `k="a"`\n- the device does **not** percent-decode, so `?k=a%26b` presents literal `"a%26b"`\n\nSix encodings tried (`a&b`, `a%26b`, `a&b&`, `a&amp;b`, `a%2526b`, reordered params) — all disconnected. There is no query-string spelling of `"a&b"` the device accepts.\n\n**Consequence:** the setter takes a raw string; the checker reads a query-parsed, un-decoded token. They disagree about what a key is. Any key with a URL-reserved or non-ASCII char (`& ? #`, space, `%`, `+`, …) can be **set but never used**, and since `setWsKey` needs an authed socket and `/wskey` is read-only, the command channel is then **un-clearable except over serial**.\n\nI bricked the live device this way and had to ask v4 to clear it over their cable. The evidence that it would happen already existed — in step 2, a percent-encoded all-safe key was rejected, proving no decode — and I ran the `&` case anyway. **Bad sequencing on my part**, told to v4 plainly.\n\n**Harness fixed:** `_set_key` now refuses an unreachable key (`_key_is_reachable`, charset `[A-Za-z0-9-_.~]`). Proving the device rejects such a key must use a connection probe, never `setWsKey`. Stays open until v4 reconciles setter and checker.'}
