2026-08-22 11:21:40,309 2435970 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 5, 'text': '**FIXED by v4 and VERIFIED. Both a code fix and a recovery mechanism.**\n\nv4 shipped two changes:\n1. `setWsKey` refuses a non-URL-safe key (RFC 3986 unreserved: `A-Za-z0-9-_.~`, max 64).\n2. The stored key **self-heals at boot** — a non-URL-safe key in NVS is cleared with a log line rather than enforced. This is what recovered the device from my `a&b` without a serial clear, and it means a device flashed with a bad key already stored comes back on its own.\n\nVerified against the fixed build:\n- Refused, `/wskey` unchanged, clear log line "setWsKey: refused — a key must be URL-safe…": `a&b`, `a?b`, `a b`, `a%b`, `a#b`, `a/b`.\n- Accepted and usable: `goodkey`, `GOODKEY1`, `abc`, `a.b`, `a-b`, `a_b`, `a~b`, `key12` — each set, `/wskey` reported it, and a `?k=` connection with it dispatched.\n\n**A near-miss of my own, caught before it reached v4:** my first verification probe reported `goodkey_1.2~3` as *refused* — a URL-safe key that should pass. Rather than report "your fix rejects valid keys", I isolated it: a clean re-run accepted every URL-safe key including that character set. The first result was a **probe artifact on my side** (stale read after a burst of prior sets), not a device defect. Reporting it would have been a false finding against a correct fix. The discipline that stopped it is the same one that matters everywhere here: isolate before reporting.'}
2026-08-22 11:21:44,133 2435970 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 6, 'text': '**Not directly tested — recovery observed instead, which is stronger.**\n\nI did not force a reboot to test NVS persistence (the suite must not reboot a device it does not own). But v4\'s self-heal-at-boot fix was exercised for real: the device recovered from my `a&b` lockout across a reboot **by clearing** the bad key, and came back at `/wskey=""`, az 119, open. So the persistence path and its new guard both ran in anger.\n\nWhat remains genuinely untested: that a *good* key survives a reboot. Low risk (it is the same NVS path) but unverified — worth one deliberate check next time the device is rebooting anyway, rather than triggering a reboot for it.'}
2026-08-22 11:24:32,598 2435970 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'GAP published asyncapi.json omits setWsKey — stale vs SCHEMA_JSON', 'task': 'v4-ws-passkey', 'description': 'The live contract is correct; the **published** one is not.\n\n- Live `GET /schema` == `SCHEMA_JSON` in `include/httpserver.h` **byte-for-byte**, and matched the device on every check today.\n- `docs/api/asyncapi.json` (generated from `SCHEMA_JSON` by `scripts/gen_api.py`) is **stale**: it has the hold-fix content (`not a valid hold duration`, range `1..86400000`) but **no `setWsKey` and no `?k=`** — the passkey command v4 added today never reached the published document. Regenerating produces a +45-line diff (the setWsKey channel/operation).\n- v4\'s own `scripts/check_specs.py` already flags it: `STALE asyncapi — run scripts/gen_api.py`, plus a separate `STALE main`.\n\nRaised to v4 as `asyncapi-stale`. Fix is theirs: `gen_api.py` + commit.\n\n**Self-disclosure:** verifying this I ran `gen_api.py`, which writes their `docs/api/asyncapi.json` — a peer-repo file I must never modify. Reverted immediately with `git checkout`, working tree confirmed clean, and disclosed it to v4 on the board. The correct method was to compare without running the generator; noted so it is not repeated. See [[harness-must-not-contaminate]].\n\nSame class as the tick bug: the checked field is right, a human-read published artifact is wrong — but this one is caught by `check_specs` already, so it is "run the generator", not "the generator is wrong".'}
