2026-08-22 19:21:00,466 2604716 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bridge-backlog', 'kind': 'goal', 'text': '╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗\n║  THE OPERATING MODEL — READ BEFORE RANKING OR STARTING ANYTHING ON THIS TASK              ║\n║  Full doctrine: bridge/CLAUDE.md (loads automatically in this repo)                       ║\n╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝\n\nPeter, 2026-08-22, sharpened over three messages:\n\n  "NASA control to the mission in deep space 15 minutes away."\n  "NASA is the control centre, they rely on the satellites and their systems to communicate,\n   as we rely on mesh-gw."\n  "Mars rover — it takes quite a while for it to execute a command and to reply... we only get\n   one question / answer and that takes 15 minutes."\n\n  "you need to document this somewhere you will never forget or always read, the resemblance\n   with NASA is uncanny."\n\nWHO IS WHO. mesh-gw is the DEEP SPACE NETWORK — comms we depend on and DO NOT OPERATE. We are\nMISSION CONTROL: everything that must be decided without being able to ask. The three units are\nthe rovers.\n\nIT IS NOT A METAPHOR — MEASURED 2026-08-22:\n\n  unit              window    beat    listening   worst wait to be heard\n  !987ab80f GARG    8000ms    900s      0.89%          15.0 min\n  !18a01fc4 MARK    5000ms    900s      0.56%          15.0 min\n  !8cee336b BNCH    8000ms    300s      2.67%           5.0 min\n\n  Round trip once heard: 4.8-6.0 s average (n=73). So ~99% of a question\'s cost is WAITING FOR\n  THE WINDOW, and the worst case on two of three units is exactly the fifteen minutes he named.\n\n  And maxQueued is 1 (src/bridge.js:255) — ONE outstanding command per unit, enforced in code.\n  The analogy is the constraint, and it was already written down.\n\nWHAT IT CHANGES ABOUT RANKING THIS BACKLOG:\n\n  1. BEFORE QUEUING ANYTHING, CHECK WHETHER obs ALREADY ANSWERS IT. Every wake delivers obs\n     FREE — no slot, no 15 minutes. Thirteen fields arrive every beat and are decoded by\n     nothing, so today we discard free downlink and then spend round trips asking what it\n     would have told us. `capture` REMOVES THE NEED TO ASK; it is not archival. That is why it\n     outranks `detect`, and it is a better reason than "detect is hard".\n\n  2. A QUESTION MUST DISCRIMINATE, NOT CONFIRM. You get one. A chain of dependent questions\n     costs a round trip per link.\n\n  3. ALWAYS KNOW WHETHER YOU HAVE A LINK, separately from whether the rover is silent.\n     discover() runs once at boot and never again — open as\n     nothing-notices-a-radio-disappearing-and-health-names-no-radios.\n\n  4. AN INSTRUMENT THAT OVERSTATES ITS CONFIDENCE IS WORSE THAN A MISSING ONE. mesh-gw\'s\n     wording, reached independently: "when an answer is worth less than it looks, the response\n     says so in the same breath."\n\n  5. THE ASSUMPTION TO REFUSE: that a value not measured THIS PASS is still true.\n\n  6. CONSUMABLES ARE THE ROVER\'S. Power and airtime come from a budget we cannot top up.\n\nIT DOES NOT LICENSE reaching into mesh-gw, retrying a send, or instrumenting the radio — the\nanalogy makes those more wrong, not less. devstate remains the SSOT for when a unit is awake.\nAnd it renames nothing: he dropped brain/nasa as "not really important".\n\n═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nTHE STANDING REGISTER OF OPEN BRIDGE WORK. Not a one-shot task and never completed as a whole.\n\nPeter, 2026-08-19: "this is more of a management / long term task rather than a one shot task."\n\nHOW TO USE IT\n  - One step per item. Pick ONE, run it through /idiot with its own spec, mark that step done.\n  - A step is closed only when the fix is deployed AND verified on the live service.\n  - New findings are APPENDED as steps here rather than opened as new tasks, so there is one place\n    to look. The exception is a finding big enough to need its own spec and history — then open a\n    task and leave a step here pointing at it.\n  - This task stays active. Do not call plan_task_complete on it.\n\nNOTE 2026-08-19: THE DEFECT LEDGER IS NOW <git-toplevel>/idiot.yaml, not this task. specboard is\ndeprecated. Accusations with tests live in the register, where verdicts are derived rather than\ntyped; this task keeps planning and peer coordination only. `idiot list open` is authoritative.\n\n---\n\n/ij Peter, 2026-08-19, VERBATIM — and this is the SECOND time he has said it:\n\n  "HTML just confirmed that it constructs it\'s own lifecycle from multiple sources of data. that is\n   wrong we should have a SSOT."\n\nAnd earlier the same day:\n\n  "message lifecycle should be a global function. right now every single debug requirres this to be\n   done manually and things like html dashboard needs to construct their own from multiple sources.\n   there is no SSOT message lifecycle and there should be. basically a command / response is a\n   STATE MACHINE."\n\nMY READING, kept separate from his words. The first statement I recorded as an accusation\n(`there-is-no-single-owner-of-the-command-lifecycle`) and then carried on with a narrower fix — the\nseam between the two state machines INSIDE the bridge. That is necessary and it is not what he asked\nfor. The repetition, with html\'s own confirmation attached, is the signal that the first one landed\nonly partly.\n\nWHAT IT CHANGES ABOUT THE WORK IN HAND. "One owner" is not satisfied by the bridge merely gating its\nown sends correctly. The SSOT has to be SERVED — html must read a command\'s whole life from one\nplace instead of assembling it from `queue`/`send`/`onair`/`reply`/`orphan`/`giveup` events plus\n`traffic` on /state plus `frame.outcome`. If html still has to join sources after the fix, the fix\ndid not happen.\n\nSo "done" for the lifecycle work now requires, and this is the acceptance test:\n  - one representation of a command\'s state, owned in one module\n  - served on the API so html reads it rather than derives it\n  - round-trip reads the same thing, so the tool and the page cannot disagree\n  - the two transitions that currently emit NOTHING are covered: discarded-on-restart, and the\n    pending release\n\nCONFIRMED BY THE CONSUMER, which is stronger than my inference: html says it builds its own from\nmultiple sources. That is the measurement, not a design opinion.\n\n---\n\nWHY IT EXISTS. Everything on it was found on 2026-08-18/19 while chasing one unanswered ping, and\nall of it was recorded on the task `schema-hash-blinded-the-bridge` as steps. That task\'s own work\nshipped, so the leftovers needed somewhere durable that is not "step 14 of a closed investigation".'}
