2026-08-22 21:16:39,994 2586895 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'A consumer — human, peer repo, or a future session of me — must be able to ask this gateway what it can answer, and get the truth.\n\nPETER, 2026-08-22, VERBATIM: "you need to inventory all of the ws resources that we have including aim, messages, antenna info etc etc because if we do the same thing again, instead of you creating huge lookups, when the answer we may already know."\n\nAnd when I overstated it as "we don\'t advertise": "hang on, are you saying that we dont advertise our services?"\n\nHE WAS RIGHT TO PUSH BACK. We advertise well and machine-readably:\n  /openapi.json   all 92 routes, 30 of them plugin routes, generated by FastAPI, cannot drift\n  GET /plugins    every loaded plugin: prefix, routes, feeds, failures — generated from the host\n  each plugin index  aim and storeward return {plugin, what, routes}\n  GET /streams    self-auditing: "74 rows retained, NEVER READ"\n\nThat is how bridge found 76 routes. Nobody is flying blind. The accurate statement is:\nWE ADVERTISE EVERY DOOR AND ALMOST NONE OF THEM SAY WHAT IS BEHIND IT.\n\nTHREE GAPS:\n1. /help contradicts all of it. 92 routes exist; /help mentions ZERO occurrences of plugins, airtime, aim, utils, link, traceroute, storeward, inbox, outbox. docs/modules/help.md does not merely permit this, it states it as an INVARIANT: "Content is a static string; it does not auto-update when routes change."\n2. Advertising is PATH-shaped, not QUESTION-shaped. /openapi.json says GET /{node_id}/airtime exists; it does not say that is the answer to "who is using the channel". plugins/utils is the only surface that does it properly — {"name":"bearing","answers":"the heading and range between two ends..."} — and only for its two stats.\n3. Some plugin indexes return DATA instead of describing themselves: traceroute and nodes answer their bare path with a route list and a node list, so a probe for "what is this" gets a payload.\n\nMEASURED COST, THE SAME EVENING: 120 sequential calls to utils/bearing to answer "what is the farthest node we know exists", because no index says which question each surface settles. I knew bearing existed; nothing said no "farthest" stat did, so I built one by hand. Third time today the same pattern: airtime was rebuilt by hand four times, the yagi rssi twice.'}
