2026-08-23 08:28:23,339 2586895 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'hard-gate-commands-off-the-public-channel', 'title': 'SECURITY: an @xxxx command must never radiate on the primary channel, from ANY send path including the TCP gateway', 'area': 'messaging', 'steps': ['VERIFY: red check - a command reaches the radio on channel 0 through send_toradio', 'BUG: refuse it at the chokepoint every path shares', 'TRACK: hashes, check_specs, commit, register verdict', 'MEASURE: what else has gone out unguarded via TCP']}
2026-08-23 08:28:40,880 2586895 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-23: "can we put a hard gate on the primary (public) pipe that blocks @xxxx commands from going over public."\n\nWHY IT IS URGENT. A real operator on the open mesh posted "Oh no a bot has appeared" (Mark~G6OMT, 2026-08-22 14:40, via 2 hops). And at 08:20 on 2026-08-23 a broadcast of "x" radiated on channel 0 from the omni, PROVEN by the yagi hearing its own echo direct at -31 dBm. Peter confirms he sent nothing.\n\nTHE PART THAT DECIDES WHERE THE GATE GOES. That send did not come from mesh-gw\'s messaging layer. Its outbox row reads:\n\n  "adopted": true\n  "why_adopted": "sent by a route that does not use the messaging layer; its text came with the status event"\n  "verdict": {"radiated": true, "why": "adopted from a status event; this layer did not send it"}\n\nmesh-gw learned about it AFTERWARDS by hearing the frame. So it bypassed the channel resolver, the primary-channel guard and the outbox entirely.\n\nTHE OPEN DOOR IS OUR OWN TCP GATEWAY. core/ble_device.py:888-894 starts TcpGateway on tcp_port with on_to_radio=self.send_toradio, and 0.0.0.0:4403 is listening on every interface. Anything on the network can speak the Meshtastic protocol straight to the radio. A Meshtastic phone or desktop client connecting there transmits with zero mesh-gw involvement.\n\nSO A GATE IN THE MESSAGING LAYER WOULD BE DECORATIVE. It would not have stopped the x. The only point every path shares is BleDevice.send_toradio(data) at core/ble_device.py:1119, which does the raw write_gatt_char(TORADIO_UUID, data) — and it receives raw ToRadio bytes, so it can decode the packet, read the channel and the payload, and refuse.\n\nSEPARATELY ESTABLISHED, and not this task: 9 outbox rows went out as to=broadcast with role=None on 21-22 Aug (@b80f ping, @336b site HOME, @1fc4 ping) — directed commands sent to everyone on the channel. And 37 of 133 outbox rows carry role=None, so nothing downstream can say whether they were public or private; that is why the dashboard put private traffic in its Public tab and why my first answer to Peter was wrong.\n\nALSO NOT THIS TASK: ~1,000 traceroutes per day, 10-17 August, to 130-200 distinct targets a day, on the public channel by design. 6,952 in the week; priced at 641 ms per frame that is 3.19% of a whole day at peak once relays rebroadcast. Stopped on 18 August by someone. Nothing prevents it restarting.'}
