2026-08-23 09:48:14,907 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'command', 'description': "A command from accepted to settled — queue it, hand it to storeward or fire it ourselves, correlate the reply, end it in exactly one terminal state.\n\n`scope: src/bridge.js` · `watch: —` · `depends: —`\n\n**The composition root gets its own area.** Giving it to a feature area would make that area look foundational and make every finding straddle two. Fourteen of the other sixteen areas *watch* this file. Absorbs what flow's chart list calls `queue` and `lifecycle`: at path level they are one file and no glob can separate them.\n\n**TWO LIVE DEFECTS:**\n- At `maxQueued` 1 (`:255`) an unsettled row refuses every new command to that unit, and `claimFromStoreward()` (`:2241`) only runs on an incoming reply (`:1922`) — so a fired-but-unanswered command blocks its unit for ever. All three units were blocked 2026-08-22 12:04Z → 18:41Z.\n- `/commands/lifecycle` publishes 10 states; the bridge emits 12 — `held` (`:2338`) and `contradicted` (`:872`) are unpublished, and a command has been seen leaving `answered`, which is declared terminal.\n\nSpec attached: `docs/spec/the-bridge-learns-the-id-storeward-mints.md`"}
2026-08-23 09:48:18,360 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'wire', 'description': 'Whether a frame\'s bytes can be trusted — decode a positional frame, refuse one whose layout cannot be proven, decide what kind it is, and decide what happens to a class nobody is consuming.\n\n`scope: src/frames.js, src/classify.js, src/handlers.js` · `watch: src/bridge.js` · `depends: —`\n\nA labels-only schema edit blinded the whole fleet for eight hours on 2026-08-18 — the hash moved though the wire layout did not.\n\nTolerance is proven in production: **1281 foreign-hash frames decoded across four hashes, zero "did not consume exactly" refusals.** The exact-consumption check is `src/frames.js:184`, and it is held against our own frames too — trusting the header over the bytes is what caused the outage.\n\n**Trap:** the presence-bitmap width is `ceil(n/8)` from the schema WE loaded and the wire carries no width. If a field count crosses a multiple of 8, every value after the bitmap is wrong.'}
2026-08-23 09:48:26,939 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'contact', 'description': 'Whether a unit is awake, overdue, or has returned.\n\n`scope: src/devstate.js, src/schedule.js` · `watch: src/bridge.js` · `depends: —`\n\n**THE SSOT FOR WHEN A UNIT IS AWAKE** — Peter, 2026-08-19: *"no independent determination of when the device is awake… NO EXCEPTIONS."* Holds the declared beat and window, the measured beat, the phase correction four other places used to compute for themselves, and the overdue detector.\n\n**This is where the wake prediction lives** — the "when to point" half that stays ours after pointing moved to mesh-gw.\n\n**Two known defects:** a Meshtastic telemetry frame counts as a beat, and a restart moves `link.heardAt` BACKWARDS, manufacturing a false overdue.'}
2026-08-23 09:48:30,463 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'store', 'description': "What is kept, what is purged, and what is never purged.\n\n`scope: src/db.js, src/history.js` · `watch: src/bridge.js` · `depends: —`\n\nThe one door to sqlite — no escape hatch, no raw SQL argument, and a test that fails if anything else imports the driver. Six command-response kinds are mode `keep` as a RULE. `src/history.js` is the read half: given a window, hand back the events in it as one timeline.\n\n**Traps that have cost time:** columns are `id, at, kind, subject, body` — `at` is **INTEGER epoch-ms, not an ISO string**, so a `LIKE '2026-08-22T%'` filter matches nothing and fails silently. Use `datetime(at/1000,'unixepoch')`. There is no `frame` column; the JSON is a string in `body`.\n\n`witness` and `beat` have no retention policy at all — parked as `park-derive-retention-per-table`."}
2026-08-23 09:48:34,416 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'surface', 'description': 'What the bridge publishes about itself — the OpenAPI document, the x- extensions describing the far side of the radio, the HTTP routes and the websocket feeds. **24 paths live**: `curl -s localhost:8790/schema`.\n\n`scope: src/api.js, src/surface.js` · `watch: src/bridge.js` · `depends: —`\n\nTrust it over any prose — but only since 2026-08-19, when it stopped declaring 200 for a route that returns 202.\n\n**IT IS WRONG RIGHT NOW** about the lifecycle machine: `/commands/lifecycle` publishes 10 states and the bridge emits 12. See **command**. The test that should catch it compares two hand-written lists, which is why it has recurred three times.\n\n**Trap:** plugin routes are generated from the LOADED plugin array (`pluginPaths`, `:505`), but a plugin may serve a route without a `/plugin/` prefix — `relay` served `/relay`. Absence of `/plugin/<name>/*` is NOT evidence a plugin is unloaded.'}
2026-08-23 09:48:43,000 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'plugins', 'description': 'What a plugin may and may not reach.\n\n`scope: src/plugins.js` · `watch: src/bridge.js` · `depends: —`\n\nThe contract refuses `res`, the filesystem and any transport, so a plugin can never serve HTML or open a socket of its own. Loads from disk, namespaces routes, refuses a plugin that would shadow a core route. `ctx.queue` is the only call that reaches a radio and is on the `MAY_QUEUE` allow-list by name.\n\n**`--plugin-dir` IS REPEATABLE.** The live service passes TWO: `bridge/plugins` and `mt-alarm-1/plugins/src` (a peer repo). **pm2 rewrites argv, so `/proc/<pid>/cmdline` shows `argc=1` and hides them** — read `pm2 jlist` for the real arguments. That cost a wrong "the plugin is not loaded" conclusion on 2026-08-23.\n\nTwo items open from the plugins repo: a live-loaded plugin logs a false REFUSED on rescan, and ctx cannot read a unit\'s live queue.'}
2026-08-23 09:48:44,415 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'callers', 'description': 'Who may command the units.\n\n`scope: src/callers.js` · `watch: src/bridge.js` · `depends: —`\n\n(`src/api.js` belongs to **surface** — one file, one area.)\n\nA request carrying an Origin header is a browser, and only pages served by this host may write. Absent Origin is curl and the CLI and passes.\n\n**Defends against browsers ONLY** — Origin is forged trivially by anything else, and there is no auth on this surface at all.'}
2026-08-23 09:48:47,874 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'daemon', 'description': 'The process itself — what is wired to what at boot, which transport a command takes, and the ports and defaults nothing else declares.\n\n`scope: bin/bridged.js` · `watch: src/bridge.js` · `depends: —`\n\n`transport` defaults to `storeward` (`:254`) — every command is handed to mesh-gw at queue time and they own the waiting. **`--transport own` is the way back and it is ONE RESTART**; the own path is kept reachable, not deleted.\n\nPort 8790 is declared here and in no document. Runs under **pm2 as `bridge`**, which rewrites argv — see **plugins** for why `/proc` cannot be trusted for its arguments.\n\nOpen: `park-bridge-roster-refuses-the-third-unit` — the unit roster is `--unit`, and it must not be hard-coded.'}
2026-08-23 09:48:57,856 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'aim', 'description': '**BEING EMPTIED, not improved.**\n\n`scope: plugins/**` · `watch: src/bridge.js` · `depends: —`\n\nSUPERSEDED 2026-08-21: Peter ruled all pointing goes through mesh-gw\'s aim plugin — *"you will do no aiming directly to the rotator."* The WebSocket to the rotator, the great-circle bearing, the learn-a-bearing-from-signal history and the slew model are all **already deleted** from `plugins/pointer`, not ported. `az` in a reply is AS ACHIEVED, never as requested.\n\n**WHAT IS STILL OURS AND IS NOT YET MOVED:** `plugins/pointer/index.js:201` is the ONLY caller of `ctx.aim(...)` anywhere, and `ctx.aim` → `bridge.aim()` → mesh-gw `POST /plugins/aim/point` (`src/bridge.js:2801`). Measured 2026-08-23: **93 of the last 100 rows in mesh-gw\'s aim ledger are `by: pointer`.** Nothing else aims at our units.\n\nSo removing this plugin stops the dish ever being aimed before a unit speaks, silently. The *deciding when* belongs in **contact** (the wake prediction); it has not been moved there yet, and that move must land before this area can be emptied.'}
2026-08-23 09:49:02,091 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'capture', 'description': '**NEEDED, NOT BUILT.** Everything the unit says is kept, whatever it is.\n\n`scope: —` · `watch: src/bridge.js, src/classify.js, src/db.js` · `depends: wire, store`\n\nA frame nobody recognises is still evidence: 56 real alarms were discarded as `unknown`, and 13 fields arriving free on every wake are decoded by nothing — cons, grst, n, rxms, and both telemetry frames including battery voltage.\n\n**THIS IS THE HIGHEST-VALUE UNBUILT AREA AND THE REASON IS THE OPERATING MODEL.** Every wake delivers `obs` FREE — no slot, no 15 minutes. Two of three units listen 0.89% and 0.56% of the time, so a question costs up to 900 s and `maxQueued` is 1. **Capture is not archival: it is the thing that REMOVES THE NEED TO ASK.** Today we discard free downlink and then spend 15-minute round trips asking what it would have told us.\n\nThat is why it outranks `detect`, and it is a better reason than "detect is hard".'}
2026-08-23 09:49:06,543 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'health', 'description': "**NEEDED, NOT BUILT.** The twelve vital signs, judged against each unit's own normal.\n\n`scope: —` · `watch: src/devstate.js` · `depends: capture`\n\n5 of 12 decoded, none judged. Every threshold comes from the unit's own declarations — silence against its own `must` (21600 s on both deployed units), stack against the 58% floor measured across 8,412 obs. A number nobody reads has not been monitored.\n\n**LINK MARGIN CANNOT COME FROM SNR.** device measured 2026-08-22 that LoRa SNR SATURATES at the top end — a strong signal reports ~+6.75 however strong it is. Two implied noise floors 55 dB apart on the same radio at the same instant is the clamp, not physics. Any SNR-derived margin is not a physical quantity. RSSI deltas are sound; filter `direct=1`, because a relayed copy's rssi is the last relay's (`src/bridge.js:1843`).\n\nSpec: `docs/spec/the-fleet-has-vital-signs.md`"}
2026-08-23 09:49:12,790 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'evidence', 'description': "**NEEDED, NOT BUILT.** The picture of what happened, or why there isn't one.\n\n`scope: —` · `watch: —` · `depends: capture`\n\nZero camera frames of any kind have ever reached this bridge. `cam`, `camu`, `grab`, `chunk`, `push`, `err` and `diag` are seven frame families the device can send and `src/` names one of them.\n\nAn image that never arrived is exactly as invisible as one never taken, and **the difference is the whole diagnosis**.\n\nRelated measurement: we have never received a single port-261 (`PAC_CHUNK_APP`) frame, from any unit, in the bridge's whole history."}
2026-08-23 09:49:14,908 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'act', 'description': "**NEEDED, NOT BUILT, AND BLOCKED.** Keeping the unit alive without being asked.\n\n`scope: —` · `watch: —` · `depends: health` · `closeable: false`\n\nLengthen a beat while there is still power to hear the command, re-anchor a drifting wake, hold intent across a six-hour sleep.\n\n**BLOCKED on Peter's ruling** — watch and tell, or watch and act unasked — because three vital signs have actions that spend the unit's own power budget, and that budget cannot be topped up. Consumables are the rover's, not ours."}
2026-08-23 09:49:16,771 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'detect', 'description': '**NEEDED, NOT BUILT, AND DELIBERATELY LAST.** Something happened, and Peter is told, and kept told.\n\n`scope: —` · `watch: —` · `depends: capture`\n\nPeter, 2026-08-22: *"the alarm detection / handling / triggers would really be the final thing to be done."*\n\nTrigger rules written before the data can be seen are written against a guess, and a system that cries wolf is worse than one that is quiet. Built on the history that **capture** collects — which is why capture outranks it.'}
2026-08-23 09:49:24,652 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'tests', 'description': 'The suite that proves the product.\n\n`scope: test/**` · `watch: src/bridge.js` · `depends: —`\n\n590 tests. **The only check in this repo with a caller** (`package.json` `scripts.test`). 587 pass, 3 committed RED on purpose in `test/round-trip-follows-a-storeward-command.test.js` — do not silence them, they encode the storeward packet-id defect.\n\n**COARSEST DECISION IN THE AREA LIST, and the first to revisit.** 99 files in one area, so "what is open in `wire`" will never include wire\'s own tests. Splitting per area is the obvious refinement; not done because a test filename does not reliably say which area it proves, and guessing would misfile silently.\n\nThere is **no CI and there are no git hooks** — a commit can land red with nothing objecting.'}
2026-08-23 09:49:28,149 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'harness', 'description': "The instruments that ask the live fleet a question. They reach the product; nothing they produce ships.\n\n`scope: bin/round-trip.js, bin/verb-sweep.js, bin/wake-accuracy.js` · `watch: src/bridge.js` · `depends: —`\n\n**`round-trip.js` LIES on the storeward path** — it latches the packet id from our own `send` event (`:872`), which carries `id: null` because mesh-gw mints the id when it fires. It has reported `FAILED AT HOP 3` for a command the device answered. Three committed-red tests encode it.\n\n**`verb-sweep.js` and `wake-accuracy.js` are LIVE ON INVOCATION — `--help` STARTS A SWEEP.** Assume any invocation puts frames on the air, and remember each costs a unit's only queue slot.\n\nNeither is invoked by anything automatic."}
2026-08-23 09:49:32,011 2629388 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'charts', 'description': "Keeping the flow charts honest.\n\n`scope: tools/**` · `watch: —` · `depends: —`\n\n`tools/reanchor.py`, `tools/anchors.yaml` — **the only python in this repo.** Re-derives every chart's `src: file:line` from a stable grep pattern, because a line number is invalidated by any edit above it and `flow_derive drift` is explicit that it cannot see that. 39 citations went stale three separate times in one day.\n\n`python3 tools/reanchor.py --check` says what is stale and changes nothing. **Invoked by nothing** — it and `flow_prove` are the two checks here with no caller, recorded across nine consecutive handovers.\n\nCharts live in `docs/flow/` and are AUTHORED, not derived — `flow_derive` has no javascript backend. That list is a different axis from these areas: it names symbols, many-to-many, and no path glob can express it."}
