2026-08-23 10:10:32,982 2586895 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'park-pki-management-belongs-here-not-in-a-consumer', 'title': "PARKED: PKI key management is mesh-gw's job — stale keys, duplicate keys, and no way to force a regen", 'steps': ['VERIFY: read the current mt firmware on PKI — does a node request a new key, and does the radio adopt one from an incoming message', "MEASURE: how many nodes in each radio's db carry a key, how many are duplicates, and how stale", 'DECIDE: where it lives — plugin vs core, and what the surface is', 'BUG: the only known repair is deleting the node from the radio db to force a regen']}
2026-08-23 10:10:46,936 2586895 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PKI KEY MANAGEMENT BELONGS IN mesh-gw, NOT IN A CONSUMER. Peter, 2026-08-23, verbatim:\n\n"we dropped DMs for the alarm project because they were simply unable to understand or manage the PKI issue, whereby keys become stale, more than 1 device has the same PKI and lots of other fuck ups, so we switched backed to \'@xxxx\' addressing on the private channel. problem is the PKI is stored on the radio, and it can become stale, seems that the only way to solve it is to delete the node from the radio db and let it regen. but that whole PKI management needs to be here and not done by a consumer. I also recall that the new mt firmware allso a node to request a new PKI and / or if a node sends a message then the radio will adopt and save that PKI automatically. /park that but it is needed, not sure where it fits, probably a plugin"\n\nTHE THREE FAILURE MODES HE NAMES:\n  1. keys go STALE\n  2. MORE THAN ONE DEVICE holds the same PKI\n  3. "lots of other fuck ups" — unenumerated, and worth enumerating before designing\n\nWHY IT IS mesh-gw\'s: it is radio state. The key lives ON THE RADIO, in its nodedb, which is exactly the layer this repo owns. A consumer cannot see it, cannot repair it, and four consumers each guessing at it is the same duplication the channel resolver was built to end. Peter has ruled it belongs here.\n\nTHE ONLY KNOWN REPAIR TODAY is destructive and manual: delete the node from the radio\'s db and let the key regenerate.\n\nTO VERIFY, NOT ASSUME — Peter said "I also recall", so this is a claim to check against the firmware source, not a fact to build on: (a) can a node REQUEST a new PKI, and (b) does a radio ADOPT and persist a key automatically from an incoming message. If (b) is true, the repair may be "make the node speak" rather than "delete and wait".\n\nWHY IT CONNECTS TO TODAY\'S WORK. This is the reason the "@xxxx" convention exists at all — addressing on the private channel is the workaround for abandoning DMs. So the primary-channel gate shipped today (block_commands_on_primary) is protecting the ENTIRE addressing scheme, not a cosmetic prefix. That reframes it: an @tag reaching the public mesh leaks how this system addresses its own devices.', 'name': 'park-pki-management-belongs-here-not-in-a-consumer'}
2026-08-23 10:11:00,118 2586895 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'PARKED 2026-08-23. Not started. Placement undecided — Peter: "not sure where it fits, probably a plugin".\n\nEVIDENCE ALREADY IN HAND, measured today while testing the primary gate and worth keeping because it is a live instance of the failure:\n\n  POST /!2687afb1/messages  {"text":"@xxxx test","to":"!da5af428","channel":"private"}\n  -> HTTP 200, then the radio rejected it:\n     states: sent -> queue_failed -> failed\n     error_reason 39, error_name PKI_SEND_FAIL_PUBLIC_KEY\n\n  !da5af428 is in NEITHER radio\'s nodedb. No entry, so no key, so the firmware\n  could not encrypt a DM to it and refused at the queue.\n\nThat is failure mode "no key" rather than "stale key", but it is the same surface and the same error path, and it shows the gateway currently reports the radio\'s PKI refusal HONESTLY — full lifecycle with the error name. So the observability for this already exists and can be built on; what is missing is the MANAGEMENT.\n\nNOTE THE CONTRAST that makes this measurable: the same text broadcast on the private channel went sent -> no_ack_needed with no PKI involvement at all. Broadcast does not touch PKI; only DMs do. That is precisely why dropping DMs made the problem go away rather than solving it.\n\nFIRST QUESTIONS, before any design:\n  - what does each radio\'s nodedb actually hold — how many entries carry a public key, are any two identical (Peter\'s duplicate-PKI failure), and how old are they\n  - does the admin surface already expose enough to delete one node rather than purge the whole db? purge_nodedb exists and is a reboot-causing sledgehammer\n  - what does current firmware do on receiving a message from a node whose key we hold but which no longer matches', 'name': 'park-pki-management-belongs-here-not-in-a-consumer'}
2026-08-23 10:12:27,515 2633801 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'command', 'scope': ['src/bridge.js'], 'description': "A command from accepted to settled — queue it, hand it to storeward or fire it ourselves, correlate the reply, end it in exactly one terminal state.\n\n**The composition root gets its own area.** Giving it to a feature area would make that area look foundational and make every finding straddle two. Fourteen of the other sixteen areas *watch* this file. Absorbs what flow's chart list calls `queue` and `lifecycle`: at path level they are one file and no glob can separate them.\n\n**TWO LIVE DEFECTS:**\n- At `maxQueued` 1 (`:255`) an unsettled row refuses every new command to that unit, and `claimFromStoreward()` (`:2241`) only runs on an incoming reply (`:1922`) — so a fired-but-unanswered command blocks its unit for ever. All three units were blocked 2026-08-22 12:04Z → 18:41Z.\n- `/commands/lifecycle` publishes 10 states; the bridge emits 12 — `held` (`:2338`) and `contradicted` (`:872`) are unpublished, and a command has been seen leaving `answered`, which is declared terminal.\n\nSpec attached: `docs/spec/the-bridge-learns-the-id-storeward-mints.md`"}
2026-08-23 10:12:35,269 2633801 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'wire', 'scope': ['src/frames.js', 'src/classify.js', 'src/handlers.js'], 'watch': ['src/bridge.js'], 'description': 'Whether a frame\'s bytes can be trusted — decode a positional frame, refuse one whose layout cannot be proven, decide what kind it is, and decide what happens to a class nobody is consuming.\n\nA labels-only schema edit blinded the whole fleet for eight hours on 2026-08-18 — the hash moved though the wire layout did not.\n\nTolerance is proven in production: **1281 foreign-hash frames decoded across four hashes, zero "did not consume exactly" refusals.** The exact-consumption check is `src/frames.js:184`, and it is held against our own frames too — trusting the header over the bytes is what caused the outage.\n\n**Trap:** the presence-bitmap width is `ceil(n/8)` from the schema WE loaded and the wire carries no width. If a field count crosses a multiple of 8, every value after the bitmap is wrong.'}
2026-08-23 10:12:38,800 2633801 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'contact', 'scope': ['src/devstate.js', 'src/schedule.js'], 'watch': ['src/bridge.js'], 'description': 'Whether a unit is awake, overdue, or has returned.\n\n**THE SSOT FOR WHEN A UNIT IS AWAKE** — Peter, 2026-08-19: *"no independent determination of when the device is awake… NO EXCEPTIONS."* Holds the declared beat and window, the measured beat, the phase correction four other places used to compute for themselves, and the overdue detector.\n\n**This is where the wake prediction lives** — the "when to point" half that stays ours after pointing moved to mesh-gw.\n\n**Two known defects:** a Meshtastic telemetry frame counts as a beat, and a restart moves `link.heardAt` BACKWARDS, manufacturing a false overdue.'}
2026-08-23 10:12:41,962 2633801 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'store', 'scope': ['src/db.js', 'src/history.js'], 'watch': ['src/bridge.js'], 'description': "What is kept, what is purged, and what is never purged.\n\nThe one door to sqlite — no escape hatch, no raw SQL argument, and a test that fails if anything else imports the driver. Six command-response kinds are mode `keep` as a RULE. `src/history.js` is the read half: given a window, hand back the events in it as one timeline.\n\n**Traps that have cost time:** columns are `id, at, kind, subject, body` — `at` is **INTEGER epoch-ms, not an ISO string**, so a `LIKE '2026-08-22T%'` filter matches nothing and fails silently. Use `datetime(at/1000,'unixepoch')`. There is no `frame` column; the JSON is a string in `body`.\n\n`witness` and `beat` have no retention policy at all — parked as `park-derive-retention-per-table`."}
2026-08-23 10:12:48,997 2633801 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'surface', 'scope': ['src/api.js', 'src/surface.js'], 'watch': ['src/bridge.js'], 'description': 'What the bridge publishes about itself — the OpenAPI document, the x- extensions describing the far side of the radio, the HTTP routes and the websocket feeds. **24 paths live**: `curl -s localhost:8790/schema`.\n\nTrust it over any prose — but only since 2026-08-19, when it stopped declaring 200 for a route that returns 202.\n\n**IT IS WRONG RIGHT NOW** about the lifecycle machine: `/commands/lifecycle` publishes 10 states and the bridge emits 12. See **command**. The test that should catch it compares two hand-written lists, which is why it has recurred three times.\n\n**Trap:** plugin routes are generated from the LOADED plugin array (`pluginPaths`, `:505`), but a plugin may serve a route without a `/plugin/` prefix — `relay` served `/relay`. Absence of `/plugin/<name>/*` is NOT evidence a plugin is unloaded.'}
2026-08-23 10:12:53,224 2633801 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'plugins', 'scope': ['src/plugins.js'], 'watch': ['src/bridge.js'], 'description': 'What a plugin may and may not reach.\n\nThe contract refuses `res`, the filesystem and any transport, so a plugin can never serve HTML or open a socket of its own. Loads from disk, namespaces routes, refuses a plugin that would shadow a core route. `ctx.queue` is the only call that reaches a radio and is on the `MAY_QUEUE` allow-list by name.\n\n**`--plugin-dir` IS REPEATABLE.** The live service passes TWO: `bridge/plugins` and `mt-alarm-1/plugins/src` (a peer repo). **pm2 rewrites argv, so `/proc/<pid>/cmdline` shows `argc=1` and hides them** — read `pm2 jlist` for the real arguments. That cost a wrong "the plugin is not loaded" conclusion on 2026-08-23.\n\nTwo items open from the plugins repo: a live-loaded plugin logs a false REFUSED on rescan, and ctx cannot read a unit\'s live queue.'}
2026-08-23 10:12:55,089 2633801 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'callers', 'scope': ['src/callers.js'], 'watch': ['src/bridge.js'], 'description': 'Who may command the units.\n\n(`src/api.js` belongs to **surface** — one file, one area.)\n\nA request carrying an Origin header is a browser, and only pages served by this host may write. Absent Origin is curl and the CLI and passes.\n\n**Defends against browsers ONLY** — Origin is forged trivially by anything else, and there is no auth on this surface at all.'}
2026-08-23 10:13:02,463 2633801 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'daemon', 'scope': ['bin/bridged.js'], 'watch': ['src/bridge.js'], 'description': 'The process itself — what is wired to what at boot, which transport a command takes, and the ports and defaults nothing else declares.\n\n`transport` defaults to `storeward` (`:254`) — every command is handed to mesh-gw at queue time and they own the waiting. **`--transport own` is the way back and it is ONE RESTART**; the own path is kept reachable, not deleted.\n\nPort 8790 is declared here and in no document. Runs under **pm2 as `bridge`**, which rewrites argv — see **plugins** for why `/proc` cannot be trusted for its arguments.\n\nOpen: `park-bridge-roster-refuses-the-third-unit` — the unit roster is `--unit`, and it must not be hard-coded.'}
2026-08-23 10:13:07,529 2633801 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'aim', 'scope': ['plugins/**'], 'watch': ['src/bridge.js'], 'description': '**BEING EMPTIED, not improved.**\n\nSUPERSEDED 2026-08-21: Peter ruled all pointing goes through mesh-gw\'s aim plugin — *"you will do no aiming directly to the rotator."* The WebSocket to the rotator, the great-circle bearing, the learn-a-bearing-from-signal history and the slew model are all **already deleted** from `plugins/pointer`, not ported. `az` in a reply is AS ACHIEVED, never as requested.\n\n**WHAT IS STILL OURS AND IS NOT YET MOVED:** `plugins/pointer/index.js:201` is the ONLY caller of `ctx.aim(...)` anywhere, and `ctx.aim` → `bridge.aim()` → mesh-gw `POST /plugins/aim/point` (`src/bridge.js:2801`). Measured 2026-08-23: **93 of the last 100 rows in mesh-gw\'s aim ledger are `by: pointer`.** Nothing else aims at our units.\n\nSo removing this plugin stops the dish ever being aimed before a unit speaks, silently. The *deciding when* belongs in **contact** (the wake prediction); it has not been moved there yet, and that move must land before this area can be emptied.'}
2026-08-23 10:13:12,040 2633801 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_area_describe args={'name': 'capture', 'scope': [], 'watch': ['src/bridge.js', 'src/classify.js', 'src/db.js'], 'depends': ['wire', 'store'], 'description': '**NEEDED, NOT BUILT.** Everything the unit says is kept, whatever it is.\n\nA frame nobody recognises is still evidence: 56 real alarms were discarded as `unknown`, and 13 fields arriving free on every wake are decoded by nothing — cons, grst, n, rxms, and both telemetry frames including battery voltage.\n\n**THIS IS THE HIGHEST-VALUE UNBUILT AREA AND THE REASON IS THE OPERATING MODEL.** Every wake delivers `obs` FREE — no slot, no 15 minutes. Two of three units listen 0.89% and 0.56% of the time, so a question costs up to 900 s and `maxQueued` is 1. **Capture is not archival: it is the thing that REMOVES THE NEED TO ASK.** Today we discard free downlink and then spend 15-minute round trips asking what it would have told us.\n\nThat is why it outranks `detect`, and it is a better reason than "detect is hard".'}
