2026-08-23 10:46:20,158 2586895 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': '/ij 2026-08-23, Peter, verbatim: "and storeward will also work with DM\'s ?"\n\nMY READING, kept separate from his words: not a question about whether storeward survives this change — a question about whether storeward gets the ack too. It does not today, and that is one of the "issues elsewhere" this solves.\n\nMEASURED, not assumed. plugins/storeward/__init__.py:680 sends via `ctx.send_text(slot["channel_role"], text, ...)` with NO `to` — a tagged broadcast. Its own lifecycle vocabulary already admits the cost, at MEANS["sent"]:\n\n    "transmitted into a wake window we believe is open; a tagged broadcast\n     carries no ack, so arrival is the device\'s to confirm"\n\nSo storeward inherits the exact blindness the @xxxx workaround has. Its strongest possible statement about a store-and-forward message is "we transmitted into a window we BELIEVE was open" — a belief about a wake, not evidence of receipt. For a plugin whose entire purpose is delivering to a device that was asleep, that is the weakest place for the confirmation to be missing.\n\nWHAT THIS ADDS TO THE PKI WORK: healing keys is not only about restoring DMs as an addressing mode. It upgrades storeward from "sent into a believed-open window" to "delivered, acked", which is the difference between store-and-forward and store-and-hope. storeward already takes `channel_role` per slot, so the send path can carry a `to` without reshaping its API — the blocker is purely that a DM needs a live key.\n\nNOT DONE HERE, and not started: storeward is outside this task\'s file list and this entry is closed. Recorded so the connection is not lost — the pki plugin\'s success criterion should eventually include "storeward can confirm a delivery", not merely "DMs work".', 'name': 'park-pki-management-belongs-here-not-in-a-consumer'}
2026-08-23 10:46:31,016 2618727 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'docs/modules/relay-observation.md states of the 2026-08-23 transport backfill:\n\n  "The mapping is bijective, so nothing is lost and the change is reversible."\n\nThat is true of the VALUE and false of the PROVENANCE, and I wrote it.\n\nFound by device, not by us. They checked their own tree against the enum warning we sent and raised a caveat about their meshgw-tap.py — it writes our events VERBATIM, which is correct for a witness log, but means any future query over it inherits the two-encoding defect. Their rule: filter a tape by NUMBER or timestamp bracket, never by enum name.\n\nApplying their reasoning back to our own fix is what exposed this. Measured 2026-08-23:\n\n  TRANSPORT_INTERNAL  505754  2026-08-03 07:39:39 -> 2026-08-23 09:45:03\n  TRANSPORT_LORA      268824  2026-08-03 07:39:43 -> 2026-08-23 09:44:34\n\nTwo clean rows, continuous ranges, NO SEAM. PRAGMA table_xinfo(relay_observation) returns\nid, ts, from_num, relay_byte, rx_device, hop_start, hop_limit, transport, decoded — no\ncolumn holds the raw wire value.\n\nSo the table can no longer answer "which of these arrived as 0.0". The transition instant\n(2026-08-22 17:06) survives only in the spec prose and in device\'s tap. THEIR TAPE IS NOW\nTHE AUTHORITY ON OUR OWN PEER SURFACE, not our store, and I had that backwards.\n\nNOT proposing to un-backfill. Consistency in the live column is what six consuming queries\ndepend on and the trade was the right way round for a working table. The defect is the\nCLAIM, not the change.\n\nThe general rule this yields, which is better than the one we sent them:\n  Never store a peer\'s enum verbatim in a table you QUERY.\n  Never rewrite it in a log you keep as EVIDENCE.\n  If you hold only one of those, you are missing the other.'}
2026-08-23 10:50:40,732 2586895 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': '/ij 2026-08-23, Peter, verbatim: "well that\'s a good solution but should accept auto (we will decide) or explicit"\n\nREADING, kept separate: storeward takes a `delivery` field — `auto` (default, storeward picks) | `dm` | `broadcast`. Auto keeps PKI out of the caller\'s hands, which is the standing rule; explicit exists for a caller that has a reason auto cannot know.\n\nTHE ONE CONSEQUENCE THAT IS NOT OBVIOUS, and it decides the code:\n\n  auto      prefer DM when a live key exists, fall back to broadcast when it does not.\n            Records which mechanism carried it. Never fails for want of a key.\n  dm        the caller asked for the ACK. If the key is stale, REFUSE — do not\n            silently downgrade to broadcast. A silent downgrade returns "sent" while\n            destroying the exact guarantee the caller named, which is the defect\n            fixed in the outbox this morning, one layer up. Refusing names the key\n            as the reason and the caller can retry as auto if it does not care.\n  broadcast always works, never needs a key, never confirms.\n\nSo explicit is not merely a preselection of what auto would have chosen — it changes the FAILURE behaviour, and that is the whole reason to offer it. Asking for `dm` is a caller saying "confirmable or not at all".\n\nALSO: `auto` must record WHY it chose, not just what. "dm, key live" and "broadcast, no key for this node" are different facts and a reader of the slot cannot reconstruct the second from the first.\n\nPlumbing already exists — ctx.send_text(node, text, channel_role) accepts a NODE ID as channel_role and Messaging.send turns that into a DM on the private channel. storeward:680 currently passes slot["channel_role"] ("private") into both arguments, so this is one decision, not a new send path.', 'name': 'park-pki-management-belongs-here-not-in-a-consumer'}
