2026-08-23 11:50:27,869 2618377 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 3, 'text': 'DONE. enqueueFrame refuses a frame longer than FRAME_CAP, counts TXR_FRAME_LEN, and traces "enqbig".\n\nWHY THERE AND NOWHERE ELSE. Three callers hand a frame to the queue: buildAndQueue after crypto, serviceAck\'s retransmit, and resend(). The last two pass an ALREADY-BUILT frame and bypass buildAndQueue entirely, so a check living only there would not cover them. enqueueFrame is the one funnel.\n\nNOT startSending(), despite its own comment calling itself "choke point for the actual transmit". By then the frame is built and encrypted, the message context is gone, and there is no requestId to correlate a reply to. A size failure first visible there is unanswerable — which is why _txDropped has never told anybody anything.\n\nIT COUNTS AND DOES NOT REPLY, deliberately. This function holds bytes, not a message: no portnum, no requestId. Inventing a correlation here would be worse than none. The reply belongs to buildAndQueue, which still knows what was being answered; this is the assertion that catches whatever slipped past it.\n\nA MISTAKE I MADE AND THE CODE CAUGHT, recorded because the shape recurs. I first put the size test at the TOP of the function — above the one-shot consumption. The very next comment says why that is wrong: "Consume the one-shot class FIRST — before any early return — so a rejected enqueue can never leak its class onto the next, unrelated frame." A size refusal is still a rejected enqueue, so my version would have leaked _nextClassSet and _nextTxDelaySet onto whatever frame came next. Moved below the consumption, and the reason is now written at the test rather than only at the thing it depends on.\n\nThe uint16_t cast on the line below is what made the gate necessary at all: a length over 65535 would have wrapped silently into a plausible small number.\n\nBUILD: SUCCESS. The whole change is +24 B RAM, +672 B flash.'}
