2026-08-23 11:52:25,900 2618727 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 3, 'text': 'LEFT OPEN DELIBERATELY — this is Peter\'s call, not mine, because it has a standing\ncost and no defect forces it. Analysis done so the decision is cheap when he makes it.\n\nTHE GAP, restated: node-dash normalises mesh-gw\'s values on the way in (correctly, for a\ntable six queries read). Nobody anywhere holds an unfiltered durable record of what\nmesh-gw actually published. So when a peer changes a field\'s encoding again — and they\ndid it once with no notice on 2026-08-22 — we will find out the same way: a peer reading\nour database, or a query silently returning short.\n\nMEASURED COST, rather than guessed:\n\n  our ingest              31,061 relay_observation rows in 24 h (21.6/min)\n  our whole store         244 MB on disk, ~2 months\n  device\'s tape           48 MB/DAY — and that is ONE node, FILTERED\n\n  So an unfiltered verbatim tape of the gw stream is plausibly >48 MB/day, i.e. it\n  outgrows our entire two-month database inside a week. This is not a small feature.\n\nTHREE OPTIONS, with what each actually buys:\n\n  A. DO NOTHING. Accept that we cannot audit a peer\'s encoding changes, and rely on the\n     next one being caught the way this one was — by accident, late, after silently\n     wrong queries. Cost: zero. Risk: exactly what already happened, again.\n\n  B. NARROW TAPE — record only the fields we normalise, verbatim, alongside the\n     normalised value. Concretely: a `transport_raw` column (and the same for any future\n     normalised field), written on insert, never rewritten. Cost: one column, a few bytes\n     a row, no new process, no new file. Buys: the encoding-change question specifically,\n     which is the one that bit us. Does NOT buy a general record of the gw surface.\n\n  C. FULL TAPE — a supervised writer appending every gw event verbatim to real disk with\n     rotation and retention. Cost: >48 MB/day, a process to supervise, a retention policy,\n     and it is a second system. Buys: everything, including questions nobody has asked yet.\n\nMY RECOMMENDATION IS B, and the reasoning is that it is the only one whose cost matches\nthe actual failure. The thing that cost us 18,671 rows was not "we lack a record of the\ngw" — it was "we lack the raw form of the ONE field we rewrite". B closes exactly that,\npermanently, for a few bytes a row, and it cannot rot because it is written by the same\ninsert that writes the normalised value.\n\nC is what device already half-has and it is instructive that theirs is filtered, in\ntmpfs, unsupervised and 13 days old with nobody having read it. A full tape that nobody\nmaintains is not evidence, it is a liability that looks like evidence.\n\nNOT STARTED. B is a schema change to relay_observation and needs its own /idiot task with\na red test, not an extension of this one.'}
2026-08-23 11:53:37,588 2618377 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 4, 'text': 'HALF PROVEN, HALF BLOCKED — and the blocked half is the one that needs hardware.\n\n=== PROVEN: the source gate, by mutation against a real build ===\n\n`rak/tools/check_tx_refusals_report.py`, new, wired as a pre: script in rak/v3/platformio.ini.\nBaseline green: "tx refusals ok — 8 refusal(s) in buildAndQueue, every one reports or is declared".\n\nThree mutations, each MT_SRC_ROOT against a copy, each a real `pio run` in the real rak/v3:\n\n  A  removed the reportOverflow from the !peer branch (the silent form it had this morning)\n     FAIL every refusal reports or is declared — SILENT: block ending at body line 85\n     [FAILED] 0.74 s\n\n  B  reported JSON_CAP instead of TEXT_CAP_PKI on the PKC lane\n     FAIL PKC refusals report TEXT_CAP_PKI, never JSON_CAP\n     [FAILED] 0.79 s\n\n  C  moved the size gate ABOVE the one-shot consumption in enqueueFrame\n     FAIL the size gate sits AFTER the one-shot consumption (consume at 464, gate at 49)\n     [FAILED] 0.76 s\n\nC is the one worth keeping. It is the exact mistake I made while writing step 3 — the code\ncaught me by comment, and now the build catches it by assertion. A check that only catches\ndefects nobody has actually made is a check nobody has tested.\n\nReal tree after all three: SUCCESS, 4.20 s, RAM 25.5%, Flash 39.9%.\n\n=== BLOCKED: forcing each refusal at RUNTIME ===\n\nThe spec is explicit that source assertions are not enough here — "MT_SRC_ROOT is not enough:\nthese are runtime paths, not source assertions", and a check that greps for a call to\nreportOverflow passes on code that never reaches it. That is the trap the three __main__-guarded\ngates fell into.\n\nSo each of the seven refusals still has to be DRIVEN on a bench unit, with the console attached,\nconfirming (a) refused, (b) an err frame with the CORRECT cap and reason reaches the air or the\nconsole, (c) the named counter moved.\n\nWHY IT CANNOT BE DONE TODAY:\n  - the only board that was on a cable is now on the garage wall (swapped with GARG)\n  - rak-dongle\'s daemon (pid 962658, 9 days old) holds /dev/ttyACM1 and has logged nothing\n    for 32+ hours, and clearing it is entangled with the AIN1 harness question — Peter\'s\n  - Peter\'s standing instruction: do not probe or configure the deployed unit\n\nTXR_NO_KEY is the one that matters most and is also the easiest to force once a board is on a\ncable: send to a node whose key we hold nothing for. It is the likeliest DM failure and the\nreason this task blocks the DM reintroduction.\n\nNOT MARKING THIS STEP DONE. The counters are live and the check is green, but no refusal has\nbeen observed to actually fire. A counter that has never moved is exactly the defect this task\nexists to remove, and claiming otherwise would reproduce it one level up.'}
