2026-08-23 13:06:26,952 2647419 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': '/ij 2026-08-23, Peter, verbatim: "you need to flow the DM message life cycle so I can see it."\n\nREADING, kept separate: a FLOW CHART of what happens to a DM from POST to ack, including the PKI failure branches and the healing path — not prose, not a log excerpt. He has spent an hour watching me describe this in paragraphs and correcting me twice; a picture is how he checks whether my model matches the system.\n\nWHY IT IS OVERDUE AND NOT DECORATION. Three of today\'s wrong turns were failures of MY OWN MODEL of this lifecycle, each stated confidently in prose and each wrong:\n  - I said add_contact was the repair; it suppresses the handshake that heals the far end\n  - I said the stale case would self-recover; it sends NO NodeInfo at all, so nothing fires\n  - I said the sync-back would close the loop; it only triggers on a LEARN, which a stale\n    pin never produces\nEvery one would have been visible as a dead-end arrow on a chart. Peter is asking for the\nartefact that makes my reasoning checkable instead of taking my word for it a fourth time.\n\nWHAT IT MUST SHOW, because these are the parts that actually bit:\n  - the happy path: accepted -> sent -> queued -> relayed -> delivered (the routing ack)\n  - want_ack: set for any addressed frame, and for a broadcast only when retry is asked\n  - PKI_SEND_FAIL_PUBLIC_KEY  = we hold NO key for the target\n  - undecryptable inbound (channel 0, no portnum, no payload) = OUR pin is STALE\n  - the firmware handshake PKI_UNKNOWN_PUBKEY -> sendOurNodeInfo, and the guard that\n    fires it ONLY on absent, never on stale\n  - our two interventions and which state each belongs to:\n      stale  -> remove_by_nodenum   (turns stale into absent so the handshake can run)\n      absent -> add_contact         (nothing to clear, no handshake to preserve)\n  - the sync-back DM after learning, which heals the SENDER\'s view of us\n  - no_answer after NO_ANSWER_AFTER_S, distinct from a stated failure\n\nAPPLIED NOW, in this task, not deferred.', 'name': 'park-pki-management-belongs-here-not-in-a-consumer'}
2026-08-23 13:27:02,546 2655398 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_switch args={'name': 'dms-carry-the-reply-and-the-ack-proves-it'}
2026-08-23 13:27:15,639 2655398 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "BUG - a PKI_UNKNOWN_PUBKEY NAK is counted and discarded, so we never heal a peer's view of US", 'description': 'MeshtasticTransport.cpp:802 decodes the Routing reply, tests only for error_reason == NONE, bumps _ackFailTotal and throws the REASON away. PKI_UNKNOWN_PUBKEY is indistinguishable from any other refusal and nothing answers it.\n\nUpstream\'s whole handshake is: receiver holds no key -> NAK PKI_UNKNOWN_PUBKEY -> the SENDER answers with its NodeInfo. mesh-gw\'s healing works by making that NAK happen (delete the stale entry so "stale" becomes "absent"). We are the sender that never answers.\n\nSo our healing is ONE-DIRECTIONAL. We repair OUR view of a peer -- noKey or authFail, forget, then maybeRequestKey. We do nothing to repair a PEER\'S view of US when it tells us in the protocol\'s own words that it cannot decrypt us.\n\nNOT REACHABLE TODAY, and that is the only reason it has never bitten: sendPki() has no caller, so we have never sent a PKC frame and never received a PKI NAK. It goes live the instant the reply path becomes a DM.\n\nSTILL MATTERS EVEN THOUGH OUR KEYS CANNOT ROTATE. FICR-derived and recomputed every boot, so a peer\'s stored key for us never goes STALE -- but it can be ABSENT: a fresh gateway, a purged NodeDB, a radio swapped in. Then mesh-gw NAKs, we count a failure and stay silent, and they wait for our next periodic NodeInfo -- the 3 h path the handshake exists to avoid.\n\nTHE FIX IS SMALL AND EVERY PIECE EXISTS: branch on the reason, and on PKI_UNKNOWN_PUBKEY call sendNodeInfo(peer) through maybeRequestKey\'s existing 12 h limiter.\n\nFound 2026-08-23 by checking our side against mesh-gw\'s #19. Not built: Peter has held the DM work while mesh-gw reworks healing.'}
2026-08-23 13:54:31,655 2655136 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'THE DESIGN CONSTRAINT, and everything before it was built without it. Peter, 2026-08-23, verbatim:\n\n"PKIs and DMs change in later firmware versions, you need to handle both. YAGI and OMNI are differnt hardware and different firmware. do not assume that yagi and omni will always have the same hardware and firmware and that another radio might be added."\n\nREADING, kept separate: PKI/DM behaviour is a PER-RADIO CAPABILITY, not a property of "the firmware". Every decision must be made per radio, recorded per radio, and must tolerate radios being added, replaced, or upgraded independently.\n\nTHIS IS THE ROOT CAUSE OF A WHOLE AFTERNOON OF CIRCLING. I treated two radios running two different firmwares as one system and was punished for it every time:\n\n  !2687afb1  OMNI  RAK4631      firmware 2.8.0.793a749\n  !fa39f7b4  YAGI  HELTEC_HT62  firmware 2.7.26.54e0d8d\n\n  - the nodes-only want_config fetch: answered in full by YAGI (99 nodes), IGNORED\n    entirely by OMNI. Registered as a defect against the fetch; it is a version difference.\n  - healing: worked on one radio, then the other, then back. I built three theories for\n    the alternation (unicast NodeInfo, cooldowns, want_ack guards) and each explained one\n    observation and failed the next, because I was averaging two different behaviours.\n  - "you fixed omni then broke yagi, you fixed yagi then broke omni" — Peter\'s own summary,\n    and it is exactly what treating them as one thing produces.\n\n  I even WROTE the caveat in docs/PKI_PLUGIN.md this morning - "the two radios are two\n  versions apart, so the firmware does X may not be true of both" - and then reasoned as\n  if it were false for the rest of the day.\n\nWHAT A FRESH DESIGN MUST DO:\n  - detect per radio what that radio\'s firmware actually does, rather than assuming\n  - keep per-radio state: what it holds, what it answered, what it ignored\n  - degrade per radio: one radio lacking a capability must not disable the feature on the\n    others, and must not be reported as a fleet-wide failure\n  - survive a radio being ADDED, replaced, or upgraded with no code change\n  - never report "it works" or "it is broken" without naming which radio\n\nNOT STARTED. Peter has called for the current implementation to be ripped up and restarted; this note is the constraint the restart must be built on, recorded before any code so it is not lost the way the version caveat was.', 'name': 'park-pki-management-belongs-here-not-in-a-consumer'}
