2026-08-23 20:01:24,020 2696393 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'BUG: the radio view says "holds no record" beside the record it holds', 'description': 'TA2m !da5af428 joined mesh-gw as a BLE device, so its own NodeDB is readable for the first time — 100 nodes, 99 keyed, visible=true. That is the far-end read-back the state machine needs and it immediately paid: it falsified the fast-path accusation (TA2m holds a CURRENT key for both OMNI and YAGI), and it exposed this defect in the instrument I was about to trust.\n\nplugins/nodes/__init__.py:290 chooses the "means" sentence on `visible`, not on whether a record came back. Every visible radio is therefore described as "this radio holds no record for that node" while the record beside it carries a full record and a public key.\n\nFix the instrument before using it.'}
2026-08-24 00:05:22,466 2696393 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'BUG: the liveness heartbeat diverges from the phone app on trigger and on nonce', 'description': 'MEASURED 2026-08-24: both mast radios are connected only ~67% of tracked time over 7 days (OMNI 110.7h READY / 54.9h not; YAGI 110.4h / 55.1h). Peter: "we need to fix this because that makes these antennas work at 30% capacity!"\n\nRead the phone app\'s source at Peter\'s instruction. Meshtastic-Android:\n\n  SharedRadioInterfaceService.kt  HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_MILLIS = 30 * 1000L\n                                  startHeartbeat(): while (true) { delay(30s); keepAlive(); checkLiveness() }\n                                  LIVENESS_TIMEOUT_MILLIS = 60s -> zombie, force reconnect\n  HeartbeatSender.kt              "to keep the firmware\'s idle timer from expiring"\n                                  "Each call uses a monotonically increasing nonce to prevent the\n                                   firmware\'s per-connection duplicate-write filter from silently dropping it"\n\nmesh-gw diverges on three axes: it fires only after 120s of SILENCE (so never on a busy link), it hardcodes nonce=0 (so the firmware may silently drop every one), and it waits 120s where the app gives up at 60s.\n\nTwo register entries, each with its own red:\n  our-heartbeat-is-silence-triggered-where-the-phone-app-s-is-periodic\n  every-heartbeat-we-send-carries-nonce-zero-so-the-firmware-may-drop-it-as-a-duplicate'}
2026-08-24 10:37:37,437 2696393 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "NEW: subscribe to the radio's own debug log over BLE, so OMNI's side of a want_config is visible", 'description': 'Seven candidate causes for OMNI ignoring a mid-session want_config have been eliminated by measurement (admin writes, per-session limit, the db61803 probe budget, the 4403 gateway, upstream #10754 and #10944, firmware version). Every log mesh-gw holds is from OUR side of the BLE link and can only say that nothing came back.\n\nLOGRADIO_UUID 5a3d6e49-06e6-4423-9944-e9de8cdf9547 is DEFINED at core/ble_device.py:123 and never subscribed. It carries the radio\'s own debug log.\n\nTHE DISCRIMINATOR, from the firmware source read earlier:\n\n    case meshtastic_ToRadio_want_config_id_tag:\n        config_nonce = toRadioScratch.want_config_id;\n        LOG_INFO("Client wants config, nonce=%u", config_nonce);\n        handleStartConfig();\n\nThat LOG_INFO fires BEFORE handleStartConfig() sets pauseBluetoothLogging = true, so it should reach us over LOGRADIO.\n\n  - we SEE "Client wants config, nonce=69421"  -> OMNI decoded our frame and chose not to answer\n  - we DO NOT see it                            -> the frame never reached PhoneAPI at all\n\nThose are different faults with different owners, and nothing else we have can tell them apart.'}
